</tr>
</tbody></table>', 'draft', 1, '', '2026-02-19 10:11:05.852893', '2026-02-19 10:11:05.852893', 'general', false, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'pending', '2027-02-19 00:00:00', NULL),
	(1613, 3, 7, NULL, 'Access Control Policy', '<h1>Access Control Policy</h1>
<h2>1.0 Purpose and Objectives</h2>
<p>The purpose of this Access Control Policy is to establish a comprehensive framework for the secure and controlled access to Intellfence’s information assets, systems, networks, and physical facilities. As a cybersecurity company, Intellfence handles highly sensitive data, including proprietary threat intelligence, client security data, internal research, and critical infrastructure. This policy enforces the principles of least privilege, need-to-know, and segregation of duties to protect the confidentiality, integrity, and availability (CIA triad) of all assets. Its objectives are to prevent unauthorized access, ensure accountability for all actions, and provide a clear, auditable trail for compliance with industry standards, including SOC 2, ISO 27001, and relevant data protection regulations applicable in North America and the European Union.</p>
<h2>2.0 Scope</h2>
<p>This policy applies universally to all individuals and entities that interact with Intellfence’s information resources. This includes, but is not limited to:</p>
<ul>
<li>All full-time, part-time, and contract employees of Intellfence.</li>
<li>Third-party vendors, contractors, consultants, and business partners (collectively, &quot;Third Parties&quot;).</li>
<li>Temporary staff and interns.</li>
<li>Any other individual granted access to Intellfence systems, such as auditors or visitors.</li>
</ul>
<p>The policy covers all forms of access: logical access to networks, systems, applications, and data; physical access to offices, data centers, and secure areas; and remote access from any location. All information assets owned, leased, or managed by Intellfence fall within the scope of this policy.</p>
<h2>3.0 Terms and Definitions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Access Control:</strong> The process of granting or denying specific requests to: 1) obtain and use information and related information processing services; and 2) enter specific physical facilities.</li>
<li><strong>Principle of Least Privilege:</strong> A security concept in which a user is granted the minimum levels of access – or permissions – necessary to perform their job functions.</li>
<li><strong>Need-to-Know:</strong> A restriction of access to information which is not merely role-based but also contextual, ensuring individuals can access only the specific information necessary for a particular task.</li>
<li><strong>Segregation of Duties (SoD):</strong> The principle of splitting critical tasks and associated privileges among multiple users to prevent fraud, error, and misuse.</li>
<li><strong>Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA):</strong> An authentication method that requires the user to provide two or more verification factors to gain access to a resource.</li>
<li><strong>Privileged Access:</strong> Elevated access rights that allow users to bypass standard security controls, typically granted to system administrators, network engineers, and senior developers. This includes root, administrator, and power user accounts.</li>
<li><strong>Information Asset:</strong> Any data, device, or other component of the environment that supports information-related activities, which can be assigned a value and is therefore worthy of protection.</li>
<li><strong>User Account:</strong> A unique digital identity established for an individual to access specific systems or applications.</li>
<li><strong>Service Account:</strong> A non-personal account used by an application, service, or system to interact with other systems or resources.</li>
<li><strong>JIT (Just-In-Time) Access:</strong> A privileged access management strategy where elevated privileges are granted for a limited time window only when explicitly needed for a task and are revoked immediately after.</li>
</ul>
<h2>4.0 Policy Requirements and Statements</h2>
<h3>4.1 User Access Management</h3>
<h4>4.1.1 User Registration and De-provisioning</h4>
<p>A formal user registration and de-registration process shall be established for granting and revoking access to all information systems and services.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Onboarding:</strong> Access rights shall be requested by the hiring manager via the formal Access Request Form, approved by the relevant system/data owner and the CISO or delegate. Access shall be provisioned based on the principle of least privilege, aligned with the individual’s role and responsibilities.</li>
<li><strong>Offboarding:</strong> Upon termination of employment or contract, all access rights for the individual shall be revoked immediately and synchronously. The People Operations team shall notify the IT/Security team of all terminations as part of a formal offboarding checklist. All company-owned assets must be returned, and access from personal devices must be remotely wiped or revoked.</li>
</ul>
<h4>4.1.2 Privilege Management</h4>
<p>The allocation and use of privileged access rights shall be restricted and controlled.</p>
<ul>
<li>Privileged accounts (e.g., domain administrators, cloud subscription owners, root accounts) shall not be used for daily operational tasks such as email or web browsing.</li>
<li>Administrative access shall require a separate, uniquely identifiable account (not a shared generic admin account).</li>
<li>Where technically feasible, Just-In-Time (JIT) access mechanisms shall be implemented for privileged task execution.</li>
<li>The use of all privileged accounts shall be logged and monitored with heightened scrutiny.</li>
</ul>
<h4>4.1.3 User Access Review</h4>
<p>Access rights shall be reviewed at regular intervals to ensure they remain appropriate.</p>
<ul>
<li>A formal quarterly access review shall be conducted for all critical systems (e.g., source code repositories, customer data platforms, security tools).</li>
<li>Managers and data owners shall be required to re-certify that their team members&#39; access is still required for business purposes.</li>
<li>Any access rights not re-certified shall be automatically flagged for revocation.</li>
<li>A comprehensive annual review shall encompass all user accounts and access privileges across the organization.</li>
</ul>
<h3>4.2 Authentication and Authorization</h3>
<h4>4.2.1 Authentication Controls</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Unique Identification:</strong> Every user shall have a unique user ID for personal accountability. Shared or generic accounts are prohibited for human users.</li>
<li><strong>Password Management:</strong> Strong passwords are mandatory. Where possible, systems shall enforce: a minimum of 12 characters; a mix of uppercase, lowercase, numbers, and symbols; and prevention of password reuse. A company-managed password vault shall be used for storing shared service account credentials.</li>
<li><strong>Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA):</strong> MFA is mandatory for all access to corporate networks, cloud consoles (e.g., AWS, Azure, GCP), source code management systems (e.g., GitHub, GitLab), administrative interfaces, and any system containing sensitive or regulated data. MFA shall also be required for all remote access via VPN.</li>
</ul>
<h4>4.2.2 Authorization Controls</h4>
<ul>
<li>Access to information and system functions shall be controlled in accordance with the defined access control policy (this document).</li>
<li>Application-level and menu-level permissions shall be configured to enforce need-to-know and least privilege.</li>
<li>Default access for any new system or resource shall be &quot;deny all.&quot;</li>
</ul>
<h3>4.3 Network and System Access Control</h3>
<h4>4.3.1 Network Security</h4>
<ul>
<li>A formal process shall be in place to control the allocation of network access rights to users, devices, and services.</li>
<li>Network segmentation shall be implemented to isolate development, production, and corporate environments. Critical assets, such as security operations centers (SOC) infrastructure and sensitive research data, shall be placed in further segmented, highly restricted network zones.</li>
<li>Firewall and router configurations shall be reviewed and documented at least semi-annually.</li>
</ul>
<h4>4.3.2 Operating System Access Control</h4>
<ul>
<li>Secure login procedures shall be implemented on all servers and user workstations, including automatic session locking after 15 minutes of inactivity.</li>
<li>Access to utility programs that can override system and application controls (e.g., debuggers, system management tools) shall be severely restricted and logged.</li>
</ul>
<h4>4.3.3 Application and Information Access</h4>
<ul>
<li>Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) models shall be designed and implemented for critical business applications.</li>
<li>Sensitive information, such as source code, threat intelligence feeds, and client vulnerability data, shall be classified and protected with appropriate access controls (e.g., encryption at rest, strict folder permissions).</li>
</ul>
<h3>4.4 Remote Access and Mobile Computing</h3>
<ul>
<li>All remote access to Intellfence networks and systems shall occur through approved, secure Virtual Private Network (VPN) solutions with mandatory MFA.</li>
<li>Company data accessed or stored on mobile devices (laptops, smartphones, tablets) must be encrypted. A Mobile Device Management (MDM) solution shall be used to enforce security policies, including the ability to remotely wipe lost or stolen devices.</li>
<li>The use of public Wi-Fi for accessing sensitive systems or data is discouraged and, if necessary, must be done through the corporate VPN.</li>
</ul>
<h3>4.5 Physical Access Control</h3>
<ul>
<li>Physical access to Intellfence offices, server rooms, and other secure areas shall be controlled using electronic key card systems or biometrics.</li>
<li>Access logs from physical security systems shall be retained for a minimum of 90 days.</li>
<li>Visitors must be logged, escorted at all times, and wear visible identification badges.</li>
<li>Clean desk and clear screen policies shall be enforced to protect sensitive information from unauthorized physical viewing.</li>
</ul>
<h3>4.6 Audit Logging and Monitoring</h3>
<ul>
<li>All significant security events, particularly those related to authentication, authorization, and privileged access, shall be logged. Logs must include user ID, timestamp, action performed, and success/failure status.</li>
<li>Logs shall be centralized in a Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) system, protected from tampering, and retained for a minimum of one year to support forensic investigation and compliance audits.</li>
<li>Proactive monitoring and alerting shall be configured for anomalous access patterns or policy violations.</li>
</ul>
<h2>5.0 Exceptions</h2>
<p>Exceptions to this policy are strongly discouraged and will only be granted under compelling business or technical circumstances where no reasonable alternative exists.</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Request Process:</strong> All exception requests must be submitted in writing to the CISO using the official Policy Exception Request Form. The request must include:<ul>
<li>A detailed justification for the exception.</li>
<li>The specific policy sections from which an exception is sought.</li>
<li>A comprehensive risk assessment detailing the residual risk and proposed compensating controls.</li>
<li>A defined expiration date for the exception, not to exceed one year.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Approval Authority:</strong> The CISO is the sole approval authority for all policy exceptions. Approval will be based on business necessity, risk evaluation, and the adequacy of proposed compensating controls.</li>
<li><strong>Documentation and Review:</strong> All approved exceptions shall be formally documented, tracked in a central register, and subject to review at least every six months or upon the expiration date, whichever comes first. The CISO reserves the right to revoke an exception at any time based on changes in risk posture or business conditions.</li>
<li><strong>Automatic Expiration:</strong> Exceptions automatically expire on the date specified in the approval. Continued need requires a new exception request.</li>
</ol>
<h2>6.0 Roles and Responsibilities</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate owner of this policy; responsible for its development, approval, dissemination, periodic review, and the approval of any exceptions.</li>
<li><strong>IT/Security Team:</strong> Responsible for the technical implementation, enforcement, and daily operation of access controls. This includes user provisioning/de-provisioning, managing authentication systems, monitoring access logs, and implementing compensating controls for approved exceptions.</li>
<li><strong>People Operations:</strong> Responsible for timely notification of employee status changes (hires, transfers, terminations) to the IT/Security team.</li>
<li><strong>Department Heads / Data Owners:</strong> Responsible for defining access requirements for their teams and data, participating in periodic access reviews to certify ongoing need, and ensuring their teams comply with this policy.</li>
<li><strong>All Users:</strong> Responsible for understanding and complying with this policy, safeguarding their authentication credentials, and reporting any suspected policy violations or security incidents immediately.</li>
</ul>
<h2>7.0 Enforcement and Violations</h2>
<p>Compliance with this Access Control Policy is mandatory for all individuals within its scope. Violations of this policy may result in disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contract, and may carry legal or financial consequences.</p>
<ul>
<li>Suspected violations shall be reported to the CISO or via the established incident reporting channel.</li>
<li>All incidents will be investigated promptly and thoroughly.</li>
<li>Disciplinary actions will be determined based on the severity and intent of the violation, in consultation with Human Resources and Legal departments.</li>
</ul>
<h2>8.0 Policy Review and Maintenance</h2>
<p>This policy shall be reviewed by the CISO at least annually, or more frequently in response to significant security incidents, changes in business operations, or updates to relevant laws and standards. All revisions require approval by the CISO. The latest version of this policy shall be made readily available to all personnel through the company’s internal policy repository.</p>
<p><strong>Approval and Effective Date:</strong></p>
<p>This policy is effective immediately upon publication.</p>
<p><strong>Approved By:</strong> [Signature of CISO]
<strong>Title:</strong> Chief Information Security Officer
<strong>Date:</strong> October 26, 2023</p>
', 'approved', 3, 'Charlie Analyst', '2026-02-19 10:19:00.603', '2026-02-19 10:12:34.893507', 'general', true, '"{}"', NULL, NULL, 'pending', NULL, NULL),
	(1614, 3, 28, NULL, 'Backup and Recovery Policy', '<h1>Backup and Recovery Policy</h1>
<h2>1.0 Purpose and Objectives</h2>
<p>The purpose of this Backup and Recovery Policy is to establish a comprehensive, risk-based framework for the creation, storage, testing, and restoration of Intellfence’s critical information assets. As a cybersecurity organization, our intellectual property, client data, and operational integrity are paramount. This policy ensures business continuity, minimizes data loss, and supports compliance with regulatory and contractual obligations, including but not limited to SOC 2, ISO 27001, and relevant data protection frameworks (e.g., GDPR). The primary objectives are to define roles, mandate procedures, and enforce controls that guarantee the availability and integrity of data in the event of accidental deletion, corruption, system failure, or a cybersecurity incident such as ransomware.</p>
<h2>2.0 Scope</h2>
<p>This policy applies universally across the Intellfence environment. It encompasses all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and any third-party service providers who are authorized to access, manage, or process Intellfence data or information systems. The policy covers all company data, classified according to the Data Classification Policy, including but not limited to: source code for proprietary cybersecurity tools, threat intelligence databases, internal research, client project data (under NDA), financial records, human resources information, and all configuration data for critical infrastructure (e.g., firewalls, SIEM platforms, development environments). This includes data residing on-premises, in company-managed cloud infrastructure (IaaS/PaaS), and within sanctioned Software-as-a-Service (SaaS) applications.</p>
<h2>3.0 Policy Statements</h2>
<h3>3.1 Commitment to Data Resilience</h3>
<p>Intellfence is committed to implementing and maintaining a robust, defense-in-depth backup and recovery strategy. This strategy is integral to our overall cybersecurity posture and risk management program, designed to protect against data loss scenarios and ensure rapid restoration of services.</p>
<h3>3.2 Data Classification and Backup Prioritization</h3>
<p>All data must be classified according to the Data Classification Policy. Backup frequency, retention periods, and recovery point objectives (RPOs) are directly determined by this classification. For example, critical data such as active client engagement data and proprietary algorithm source code will have more frequent backups and shorter RPOs compared to archival or publicly available information.</p>
<h3>3.3 The 3-2-1 Backup Rule</h3>
<p>Intellfence mandates adherence to the 3-2-1 backup rule as a minimum standard for all critical and sensitive data:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>3</strong> copies of data: The primary production data plus at least two backup copies.</li>
<li><strong>2</strong> different media types: To avoid common failure modes (e.g., both copies on the same SAN). This typically means one copy on fast, local disk for quick recovery and one copy on a separate, immutable medium.</li>
<li><strong>1</strong> copy stored offsite and offline/immutable: A geographically separate copy must be maintained. For the highest classification tiers, one copy must be stored in an immutable or air-gapped state to protect against ransomware or malicious deletion.</li>
</ul>
<h3>3.4 Encryption of Backup Data</h3>
<p>All backup data, both in transit and at rest, must be encrypted using strong, industry-standard cryptographic algorithms (e.g., AES-256). Encryption keys for backup data must be managed separately from the backup storage location and in accordance with the Cryptographic Controls Policy.</p>
<h3>3.5 Regular Testing and Validation</h3>
<p>Backup integrity is meaningless without proven recoverability. Scheduled recovery tests must be conducted quarterly for critical systems and annually for all other in-scope systems. Tests must validate both file-level and full-system recovery capabilities, with results documented, including success/failure metrics and time-to-recovery.</p>
<h3>3.6 Roles and Responsibilities</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate accountability for the policy, its approval, and its alignment with organizational risk tolerance.</li>
<li><strong>IT/SysOps Team:</strong> Responsible for the daily implementation, execution, monitoring, and technical execution of backup procedures and recovery operations.</li>
<li><strong>Data Owners (e.g., Department Heads):</strong> Responsible for identifying critical data assets within their domain and defining the required RPO and RTO in consultation with the CISO.</li>
<li><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Required to store all business-related data on approved, centrally managed systems that are covered by this backup policy. Local storage on individual workstations (e.g., C: drives) is not protected by enterprise backups.</li>
</ul>
<h2>4.0 Detailed Backup Procedures</h2>
<h3>4.1 Backup Frequency and Retention</h3>
<p>Backup schedules are defined by data criticality and are documented in the Backup Schedule Register maintained by the IT team.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Tier 1 (Critical - e.g., Production Databases, Source Code Repositories):</strong> Incremental backups nightly, with full weekly backups. Retention: 90 days for daily/weeklies, with monthly archives retained for 12 months.</li>
<li><strong>Tier 2 (Important - e.g., Internal Systems, Email, Collaboration Tools):</strong> Full backups weekly. Retention: 60 days.</li>
<li><strong>Tier 3 (General - e.g., Archived Project Data):</strong> Full backups monthly. Retention: 12 months.</li>
</ul>
<h3>4.2 Backup Storage and Security</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Onsite/Online Storage:</strong> Used for operational recovery, providing fast RTO. Access is strictly limited to authorized backup administrators.</li>
<li><strong>Offsite/Immutable Storage:</strong> Backups are replicated to a geographically separate cloud storage provider configured with object lock or immutable storage features to prevent alteration or deletion for a defined retention period.</li>
<li><strong>Logical Separation:</strong> Backup administration credentials are distinct from production system credentials and are subject to strict privilege access management (PAM) controls.</li>
<li><strong>Monitoring and Alerting:</strong> All backup jobs are monitored. Failure alerts are generated immediately and sent to the IT/SysOps team and the CISO for critical systems.</li>
</ul>
<h2>5.0 Detailed Recovery Procedures</h2>
<h3>5.1 Recovery Request and Authorization</h3>
<p>All recovery operations, except those performed as part of a scheduled test, must be initiated via a formal ticket in the ITSM system. The request must be authorized by the data owner or their delegate, and by the CISO for significant or security-related incidents.</p>
<h3>5.2 Recovery Types and Objectives</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Recovery Point Objective (RPO):</strong> The maximum tolerable period of data loss. For Tier 1 systems, the RPO is ≤ 24 hours.</li>
<li><strong>Recovery Time Objective (RTO):</strong> The maximum tolerable downtime for a service. For Tier 1 systems, the RTO is ≤ 4 hours for critical functions.</li>
<li><strong>Recovery Procedures:</strong> Step-by-step recovery runbooks are maintained for all critical systems, detailing the process for file-level, database, and full-system restoration from both online and offline backup media.</li>
</ul>
<h3>5.3 Post-Recovery Validation</h3>
<p>Following any recovery operation, the requesting user or data owner must validate the integrity and completeness of the restored data. This validation is documented within the recovery ticket before the ticket is closed.</p>
<h2>6.0 Policy Review and Maintenance</h2>
<p>This policy shall be reviewed annually by the CISO or following any significant security incident, technological change, or business process change that impacts data resilience. Updates will be communicated to all relevant personnel.</p>
<h2>7.0 Enforcement and Violations</h2>
<p>Adherence to this policy is a condition of employment and for third-party relationships. Violations of this policy may result in disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contract, and may lead to legal action. Examples of violations include: deliberately storing critical business data on unapproved, non-backed-up storage; attempting to disable or interfere with backup processes; or performing unauthorized recovery operations.</p>
', 'draft', 1, 'Charlie Analyst', '2026-02-19 18:30:46.803479', '2026-02-19 18:30:46.803479', 'general', true, '"{}"', NULL, NULL, 'pending', '2027-02-19 00:00:00', NULL),
	(1615, 3, 52, NULL, 'Shadow IT Policy', '<h1>Shadow IT Policy</h1>
<h2>1.0 Purpose and Objectives</h2>
<p>This Shadow IT Policy establishes the mandatory governance, risk management, and compliance framework for the acquisition, deployment, integration, and use of all information technology (IT) resources within Intellfence. As a cybersecurity enterprise, our credibility, operational integrity, and contractual obligations are intrinsically tied to the security and compliance of our technology ecosystem. The purpose of this policy is to proactively mitigate the significant and often cascading risks—including information security breaches, data privacy violations, intellectual property theft, operational instability, and financial loss—associated with unauthorized or unmanaged technology.</p>
<p>The primary objectives are to ensure all technology used for business purposes is aligned with corporate security architecture, complies with relevant regulatory frameworks (including GDPR, CCPA/CPRA, and potential client-mandated standards like NIST CSF or CIS Controls), and is integrated into our formal Information Security Management System (ISMS). This policy is designed to support, not hinder, innovation by providing a clear, secure, and accountable pathway for adopting new technologies.</p>
<h2>2.0 Scope</h2>
<p>This policy applies universally and without exception to all individuals and entities that interact with Intellfence''s information assets. This includes, but is not limited to: all full-time and part-time employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, interns, and authorized third-party partners. The policy governs all technology, irrespective of ownership (corporate or personal), funding source, or deployment model, that meets any of the following criteria:</p>
<ul>
<li>Stores, processes, or transmits any Intellfence data (including data classified as Public, Internal, Confidential, or Restricted).</li>
<li>Connects to any Intellfence network, whether on-premises, virtual private cloud (VPC), or via corporate VPN.</li>
<li>Is used to develop, test, or deliver Intellfence services, products, or intellectual property.</li>
<li>Could impact the confidentiality, integrity, or availability of Intellfence''s business operations.</li>
</ul>
<p>Governed technology explicitly includes: Software-as-a-Service (SaaS), Infrastructure-as-a-Service (IaaS), Platform-as-a-Service (PaaS), desktop/mobile applications, open-source libraries integrated into products, unsanctioned collaboration/communication tools, personal hardware (BYOD) used for work purposes, and Internet of Things (IoT) devices within corporate facilities.</p>
<h2>3.0 Definitions</h2>
<ul>
<li><p><strong>Shadow IT:</strong> Any information technology system, solution, service, hardware, or software component deployed and used for business purposes without explicit, documented approval from the designated IT governance and security authority (as defined in Section 7.0). This includes using personal subscriptions for business tasks, deploying unauthorized cloud instances, installing unvetted software on corporate endpoints, or using non-standard communication channels for sensitive discussions.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Sanctioned IT:</strong> Technology solutions that have successfully undergone and passed the formal procurement, security, architectural, and legal review process as defined by the IT and Security Departments. These assets are registered in the corporate IT Asset Management (ITAM) system, are fully supported, and are integrated into security monitoring and backup regimes.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Cloud Service:</strong> Any on-demand, internet-accessible IT resource provisioned and managed by a third-party vendor. This encompasses SaaS (e.g., CRM, project management), IaaS (e.g., virtual servers, storage), PaaS (e.g., application development platforms), and managed security services.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Authorizing Official:</strong> The designated individual or body with delegated authority from executive management to approve or deny the use of a technology solution based on a comprehensive risk assessment. For most requests, this is the Chief Information Security Officer (CISO) or their designee. For strategic or high-impact solutions, the IT Governance Board may serve as the Authorizing Official.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Data Owner:</strong> The business unit leader or department head who has ultimate responsibility for the classification, integrity, protection, and lifecycle management of a specific data set. The Data Owner is a key stakeholder in the risk acceptance process for any technology that will process their data.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Formal Technology Request (FTR):</strong> The standardized process and required documentation for requesting approval of any new technology, initiated via the official IT Service Management (ITSM) portal.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h2>4.0 Policy Statement</h2>
<p>Intellfence strictly prohibits the use of Shadow IT. The unauthorized acquisition, configuration, subscription to, installation, or connection of any hardware, software, cloud service, or application to corporate networks, endpoints, or for the processing, storage, or transmission of corporate data constitutes a direct violation of this policy.</p>
<p>All technology intended for business use must be procured, implemented, and managed exclusively through the formal IT governance and procurement channels outlined herein. This mandate is critical for maintaining our security posture, ensuring compliance with client agreements and regulations, and preserving the integrity of our cybersecurity service offerings.</p>
<h2>5.0 Detailed Policy Requirements &amp; Controls</h2>
<h3>5.1 Mandatory Pre-Approval Process</h3>
<p>No technology, including free trials, proof-of-concept deployments, or open-source tools intended for production use, may be procured, subscribed to, installed, or configured prior to receiving written approval from the Authorizing Official. All requests must originate as a Formal Technology Request (FTR) within the official ITSM portal. The request must include a clear business justification, identified Data Owner, expected data classification involved, and any known integration points with existing sanctioned systems.</p>
<h3>5.2 Formal Security, Architecture, and Compliance Assessment</h3>
<p>All proposed technology must undergo a rigorous, multi-faceted review prior to approval. The IT and Security Departments are responsible for conducting this assessment, which shall include, at a minimum:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Vendor Risk Assessment (VRA):</strong> Evaluation of the provider''s security posture, including review of independent audit reports (SOC 2 Type II, ISO 27001), penetration test results, and their information security policies.</li>
<li><strong>Data Privacy Impact Assessment (DPIA):</strong> Analysis of data processing activities, data residency requirements, sub-processor dependencies, and alignment with GDPR, CCPA/CPRA, and other applicable privacy laws.</li>
<li><strong>Technical Security Review:</strong> Evaluation of authentication mechanisms (SSO/SAML 2.0/OIDC mandatory where possible), encryption standards for data at rest (AES-256 minimum) and in transit (TLS 1.2+), logging and audit capabilities, and secure API design.</li>
<li><strong>Architectural Fit Review:</strong> Assessment of the solution''s alignment with Intellfence''s cloud security architecture, network segmentation model, and identity and access management (IAM) framework.</li>
</ul>
<h3>5.3 Centralized Inventory and Continuous Discovery</h3>
<p>The IT Department shall maintain a definitive, accurate, and current Configuration Management Database (CMDB) or IT Asset Management (ITAM) system for all sanctioned IT assets. This inventory must include asset owner, department, data classification, vendor contract details, security configuration status, and end-of-life/end-of-support dates. Furthermore, the use of automated Cloud Access Security Broker (CASB) tools, network traffic analysis (NTA), and agent-based discovery on endpoints is mandated to continuously monitor for and identify unauthorized assets and services.</p>
<h3>5.4 Technical Control Enforcement</h3>
<p>Technical controls shall be implemented to provide defense-in-depth against Shadow IT proliferation:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Network Enforcement:</strong> Next-generation firewall (NGFW) and Secure Web Gateway (SWG) policies must be configured to block access to known high-risk and unauthorized SaaS/cloud application categories. Network segmentation shall limit lateral movement from potentially compromised unauthorized services.</li>
<li><strong>Endpoint Enforcement:</strong> Endpoint Detection and Response (EDR) or unified endpoint management (UEM) agents on all corporate-managed devices must enforce application allow-listing or block-listing policies. Local administrator privileges required for software installation are strictly prohibited for standard users.</li>
<li><strong>Identity-Centric Controls:</strong> The principle of least privilege shall be enforced through the corporate Identity Provider (IdP). Where possible, access to sanctioned SaaS applications will be brokered exclusively through the IdP to centralize authentication and de-provisioning.</li>
</ul>
<h3>5.5 Data Exfiltration Prevention</h3>
<p>The use of unsanctioned file-sharing, collaboration, communication, or cloud storage services (e.g., personal Google Drive, Dropbox, WhatsApp, or personal email) for transmitting, storing, or processing Intellfence Confidential or Restricted data is expressly forbidden. Data Loss Prevention (DLP) tools, integrated with email, web gateways, and endpoint agents, shall be configured to monitor, alert on, and block unauthorized transfers of sensitive data based on predefined classification labels and policies.</p>
<h3>5.6 Exception Handling and Risk Acceptance</h3>
<p>Time-bound exceptions to this policy for legitimate, urgent business needs may be granted only by the CISO or designated Authorizing Official. The exception process is not a bypass but a formal risk management procedure:</p>
<ol>
<li>A formal exception request must be submitted via the ITSM portal.</li>
<li>The request must include a compelling business case, proposed mitigation controls, and a maximum expiration date (not to exceed 90 days without executive review).</li>
<li>A formal risk acceptance form must be completed and signed by the relevant Data Owner and the requesting department head, acknowledging the residual risks.</li>
<li>All exceptions are logged in a central register reviewed quarterly by the CISO. Prior to expiration, the technology must be either fully decommissioned and all data migrated, or it must enter the formal sanctioning process.</li>
</ol>
<h2>6.0 Compliance &amp; Legal Obligations</h2>
<p>All technology used at Intellfence must comply with applicable laws, regulations, industry standards, and client contractual obligations. These may include, but are not limited to, the General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR), California Consumer Privacy Act (CCPA) as amended by CPRA, and contractual commitments requiring adherence to frameworks like ISO 27001, SOC 2, NIST SP 800-53, or the CIS Critical Security Controls.</p>
<p>The use of unvetted Shadow IT that leads to a data breach, compliance violation, or client data exposure may result in severe financial penalties, loss of certification, reputational damage, and legal liability. Vendor agreements for all sanctioned IT must include robust security and compliance clauses, such as guarantees of data processing compliance, mandatory security incident notification within 72 hours, right-to-audit clauses, and clear data return/destruction procedures upon contract termination.</p>
<h2>7.0 Roles and Responsibilities</h2>
<ul>
<li><p><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Are responsible for using only sanctioned IT for business activities, for understanding the data classification of information they handle, for reporting suspected instances of Shadow IT to the IT Service Desk or via the anonymous reporting channel, and for submitting all technology requests through the official FTR process.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Department Heads / Data Owners:</strong> Are accountable for the enforcement of this policy within their teams. They must promote a culture of security, authorize the business need for technology requests, and are the final authority for signing risk acceptance forms for technologies that will process their department''s data.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Information Technology (IT) Department:</strong> Is responsible for operating and maintaining the FTR and exception processes, conducting technical and architectural reviews, managing the CMDB/ITAM system, implementing and monitoring technical enforcement controls, and providing secure, sanctioned alternatives to common Shadow IT use cases.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Has ultimate authority for the enforcement, interpretation, and exception approval of this policy. The CISO is responsible for defining the security assessment criteria, reporting quarterly to executive management on Shadow IT risks and compliance metrics, and ensuring the policy aligns with the ISMS and audit requirements.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>IT Governance Board:</strong> Provides strategic oversight, reviews high-impact or high-cost technology requests, and resolves disputes regarding technology approval. The board includes the CISO, Head of IT, and relevant business executives.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h2>8.0 Policy Enforcement and Violations</h2>
<p>Compliance with this policy is mandatory. Violations will be treated seriously and may result in disciplinary action proportionate to the severity and impact of the violation, up to and including termination of employment or contract.</p>
<p>The IT and Security Departments are authorized and obligated to take immediate action to contain any risk posed by Shadow IT. This includes, but is not limited to: technically blocking network access to unauthorized services, remotely uninstalling unauthorized software from corporate endpoints, disabling user accounts involved in policy violations, and quarantining affected systems.</p>
<p>Individuals who knowingly violate this policy may be held personally accountable for any direct damages, regulatory fines, incident response costs, or client penalties incurred by Intellfence as a result of their actions.</p>
<h2>9.0 Policy Review and Maintenance</h2>
<p>This policy shall be reviewed and validated by the CISO at least annually. Additional reviews will be triggered by significant security incidents, material changes in the regulatory landscape, major shifts in business operations, or updates to the corporate ISMS. All revisions must be approved by the CISO and communicated to all personnel. The effectiveness of this policy and its associated controls will be measured through key risk indicators (KRIs) and assessed during internal and external audits for ISO 27001, SOC 2, and other relevant frameworks.</p>
', 'draft', 1, '', '2026-02-19 20:04:55.125588', '2026-02-19 20:04:55.125588', 'general', true, '"{}"', NULL, NULL, 'pending', NULL, NULL),
	(1616, 3, 33, NULL, 'Password Policy', '<h1>Password Policy</h1>
<p><strong>Document ID:</strong> IS-POL-001
<strong>Effective Date:</strong> October 26, 2023
<strong>Approved By:</strong> Chief Information Security Officer
<strong>Classification:</strong> Internal Use</p>
<h2>1.0 Purpose</h2>
<p>This Password Policy establishes mandatory technical and administrative controls for the creation, management, protection, and secure disposal of all authentication credentials at Intellfence. As a cybersecurity firm, our operational integrity and client trust depend on demonstrable security practices. The primary objective is to safeguard information assets, intellectual property, and client data by ensuring credentials are cryptographically robust, confidential, and resistant to modern attack vectors. This protects the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of corporate systems and supports compliance with industry standards and regulations.</p>
<h2>2.0 Scope</h2>
<p>This policy applies to all Intellfence personnel, including full-time and part-time employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, interns, and third-party agents with access to Intellfence information systems. It governs all individuals who interact with any Intellfence network, infrastructure, workstation, server, cloud environment, application, database, code repository, or security tool.</p>
<p>The policy covers all authentication credentials, including user account passwords, privileged administrative passwords, service account passwords, application secrets, API keys, cryptographic keys for authentication, and tokens. It applies regardless of system location (on-premises, public/private cloud, or hybrid architecture). Exceptions require a formally documented, CISO-approved risk-based exemption.</p>
<h2>3.0 Definitions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Authentication Credential:</strong> Any secret piece of information used to verify the identity of a user, system, or service. This includes passwords, passphrases, API keys, tokens, and cryptographic keys used for authentication.</li>
<li><strong>Cryptographic Hash:</strong> A one-way mathematical function that transforms input data (e.g., a password) into a fixed-size string of characters (a hash), from which the original data cannot be feasibly derived.</li>
<li><strong>Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA):</strong> An authentication method that requires the user to provide two or more verification factors from distinct categories (e.g., knowledge factor like a password, possession factor like a hardware token, inherence factor like a fingerprint).</li>
<li><strong>Passphrase:</strong> A sequence of random or unrelated words used as a memorized secret, typically longer and more secure than a traditional password (e.g., &quot;crystal-hamster-bracket-tangible&quot;).</li>
<li><strong>Privileged Administrative Account:</strong> Any user account with elevated permissions beyond a standard user, used for system administration, configuration, or security management (e.g., root, Administrator, Domain Admin).</li>
<li><strong>Service Account:</strong> A non-human account used by an application, service, or system to interact with other systems, databases, or network resources.</li>
<li><strong>Salt (Cryptographic):</strong> A random data string that is uniquely generated and combined with a password before hashing to defend against precomputed hash attacks (e.g., rainbow tables).</li>
</ul>
<h2>4.0 Policy Statement</h2>
<p>Intellfence mandates that all authentication credentials be created, managed, transmitted, stored, and disposed of according to the requirements defined herein. The use of weak, default, shared, or known-compromised passwords is strictly prohibited and constitutes a security violation. All personnel are accountable for adhering to these controls. Security is a shared responsibility integral to our role as cybersecurity professionals.</p>
<h2>5.0 Detailed Requirements &amp; Controls</h2>
<h3>5.1 Password Composition and Complexity</h3>
<p>All user-created passwords must be sufficiently complex to resist modern cracking techniques. The following minimum requirements are enforced technically where possible and mandated administratively elsewhere.</p>
<p><strong>Minimum Length:</strong> Standard user account passwords: minimum 14 characters. Privileged administrative account passwords: minimum 20 characters. Longer passwords are strongly encouraged.</p>
<p><strong>Character Complexity:</strong> Passwords must include characters from at least three (3) of the following four categories:</p>
<ol>
<li>Uppercase letters (A through Z)</li>
<li>Lowercase letters (a through z)</li>
<li>Base 10 numerals (0 through 9)</li>
<li>Non-alphanumeric special characters (e.g., ! @ # $ % ^ &amp; * ( ) ` ~ - _ = + [ ] { } \ | ; : &#39; &quot; , . &lt; &gt; / ?)</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Prohibited Elements:</strong> Passwords must NOT contain:</p>
<ol>
<li>The user&#39;s account name, full name, or any significant part thereof.</li>
<li>Common dictionary words in any language, including simple substitutions (e.g., &quot;P@ssw0rd&quot;).</li>
<li>Repetitive (e.g., &quot;aaaaaa&quot;) or sequential characters (e.g., &quot;123456&quot;, &quot;qwerty&quot;).</li>
<li>Publicly available personal information (e.g., birthdates, family names, pet names).</li>
<li>Company-related information that is easily guessable (e.g., &quot;Intellfence2024&quot;).</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Passphrases:</strong> The use of strong, random passphrases—a sequence of unrelated words totaling over 20 characters—is recommended for memorized secrets (e.g., &quot;crystal-hamster-bracket-tangible&quot;). They must avoid predictable patterns or phrases from popular culture.</p>
<h3>5.2 Password Lifecycle Management</h3>
<p><strong>Initial Provisioning &amp; First-Time Use:</strong> All initial or reset passwords provisioned by IT or Security shall be system-generated, unique, and meet complexity requirements. Temporary passwords must be transmitted via a secure, out-of-band method. Users must change this password immediately upon first login.</p>
<p><strong>Password Expiration &amp; Rotation:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Standard User Accounts:</strong> Passwords must be changed at least every 90 days.</li>
<li><strong>Privileged Administrative Accounts:</strong> Passwords must be changed at least every 60 days.</li>
<li><strong>Service Accounts:</strong> Passwords or secrets must be rotated at least every 180 days, and immediately upon personnel departure or suspected compromise.</li>
<li><strong>Just-in-Time (JIT) &amp; PAM:</strong> Where possible, privileged access should be managed through a Privileged Access Management (PAM) solution or Just-in-Time elevation mechanisms.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Password History:</strong> Authentication systems shall enforce a password history, preventing reuse of the previous 10 passwords.</p>
<h3>5.3 Password Protection and Storage</h3>
<p><strong>Secure Storage:</strong> Passwords shall never be stored in plaintext. All passwords at rest must be hashed using a strong, modern, industry-approved cryptographic hashing algorithm (e.g., bcrypt, scrypt, Argon2id, or PBKDF2 with iteration count &gt; 100,000). All hashes must be salted with a unique, cryptographically random salt per password.</p>
<p><strong>Secure Transmission:</strong> Passwords shall only be transmitted over encrypted channels. All authentication interfaces must use strong transport layer encryption (TLS 1.2 or higher). Sending passwords via unencrypted email, instant messaging, or other clear-text protocols is prohibited.</p>
<p><strong>Prohibition on Insecure Local Storage:</strong> Writing down passwords on sticky notes, notebooks, or whiteboards is prohibited. Storing passwords in unencrypted files on local drives, network shares, or cloud storage is prohibited. Browser-based password managers not integrated with the corporate-approved solution are prohibited for work-related credentials.</p>
<p><strong>Approved Password Managers:</strong> Personnel must use the corporate-approved enterprise password manager for storing and managing all work-related credentials. The master password must adhere to Section 5.1 requirements and be protected with MFA.</p>
<h3>5.4 Authentication and Access Controls</h3>
<p><strong>Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA):</strong> MFA is mandatory for the following access scenarios without exception:</p>
<ol>
<li>All remote network access (VPN, RDP gateways, Citrix).</li>
<li>All access to cloud service administration consoles (e.g., AWS, Azure, GCP).</li>
<li>All users with privileged access rights on any system.</li>
<li>Access to any system or application containing sensitive data per Intellfence&#39;s Data Classification Policy.</li>
<li>The approved enterprise password manager.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Account Lockout:</strong> Systems shall implement an account lockout mechanism. After 5 consecutive failed authentication attempts within 15 minutes, the account shall be locked for a minimum of 15 minutes. Lockout events must be logged and generate an alert to the Security Operations Center (SOC).</p>
<p><strong>Session Management:</strong> Interactive user sessions shall be protected by automatic termination after 15 minutes of inactivity. Session tokens must be cryptographically strong, randomly generated, and invalidated upon logout. Re-authentication is required to resume a terminated session.</p>
<h3>5.5 Administrative and Technical Enforcement</h3>
<p><strong>Centralized Identity Management:</strong> All user authentication shall be managed through a centralized Identity and Access Management (IAM) system to ensure consistent policy application.</p>
<p><strong>Automated Compliance Checking:</strong> The Information Security Team shall deploy automated tools to periodically scan for and alert on non-compliant password practices.</p>
<p><strong>Logging and Monitoring:</strong> All authentication events must be logged with sufficient detail for forensic analysis. Logs must be centrally aggregated, protected from tampering, and retained for a minimum of 90 days (365 days for privileged access events).</p>
<h2>6.0 Roles and Responsibilities</h2>
<p><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Are responsible for understanding and complying with this policy, creating and managing strong passwords, using approved password managers, and reporting any suspected password compromise or policy violation to the Information Security Team.</p>
<p><strong>Information Technology (IT) Department:</strong> Is responsible for the technical implementation and enforcement of password controls on systems under their management, provisioning initial credentials securely, and assisting users with password-related issues.</p>
<p><strong>Information Security Team:</strong> Is responsible for developing, maintaining, and communicating this policy; selecting and managing the enterprise password manager; monitoring for compliance and attacks; conducting security awareness training; and investigating policy violations.</p>
<p><strong>System and Application Owners:</strong> Are responsible for ensuring their systems are configured to comply with this policy&#39;s technical requirements, including password complexity, storage, MFA, and logging.</p>
<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Has ultimate authority and accountability for this policy, including granting formal exceptions and approving significant changes.</p>
<h2>7.0 Procedures</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Password Creation/Reset:</strong> Users must create passwords meeting Section 5.1 requirements. For password resets, contact the IT Help Desk, which will follow a secure, identity-verified process to issue a temporary password.</li>
<li><strong>Password Change:</strong> Users will receive automated notifications prior to password expiration. Changes must be made through the official company portal or IAM system.</li>
<li><strong>Suspected Compromise:</strong> Immediately report any suspected credential compromise to the SOC via the designated incident reporting channel. Affected passwords must be changed immediately on all systems where they were used.</li>
<li><strong>Use of Password Manager:</strong> All work-related credentials must be stored in the approved enterprise password manager. Users must complete mandatory training on its use.</li>
<li><strong>Offboarding:</strong> Upon termination or role change, the IT Department will immediately disable all associated accounts and initiate credential rotation for any related service accounts.</li>
</ol>
<h2>8.0 Exceptions</h2>
<p>Exceptions to this policy are strongly discouraged and will only be granted where a legitimate technical or business constraint exists that cannot be mitigated. The process is as follows:</p>
<ol>
<li>A formal written request must be submitted to the CISO, detailing the specific requirement from which an exception is needed, the justification, the systems/accounts involved, the duration, and a proposed set of compensating controls to mitigate the associated risk.</li>
<li>The Information Security Team will conduct a risk assessment of the exception request.</li>
<li>Exceptions require written approval from the CISO. All approved exceptions will be documented, assigned a unique ID, and include an expiration date (not to exceed one year).</li>
<li>Exception owners are responsible for ensuring compensating controls remain effective and for requesting a renewal prior to expiration if the condition persists.</li>
</ol>
<h2>9.0 Compliance &amp; Audit Alignment</h2>
<p>This policy aligns with key cybersecurity frameworks and regulations relevant to Intellfence&#39;s operations.</p>
<p><strong>ISO/IEC 27001:2022:</strong> Supports controls including A.5.15 (Identity management), A.5.16 (Identity, authentication, and access), A.5.17 (Authentication information), A.8.2 (Privileged access rights), and A.8.9 (Logging).</p>
<p><strong>SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria:</strong> Provides control framework for Security, Availability, and Confidentiality criteria, specifically CC6.1, CC6.3, and CC6.7.</p>
<p><strong>General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR):</strong> Supports compliance with Article 32 (Security of processing).</p>
<p><strong>NIST Cybersecurity Framework (CSF) &amp; NIST SP 800-63B:</strong> Requirements are informed by NIST digital identity guidelines.</p>
<p>Non-compliance may result in audit findings, regulatory penalties, legal liability, and reputational damage.</p>
<h2>10.0 References</h2>
<ol>
<li>Intellfence Information Security Policy</li>
<li>Intellfence Data Classification and Handling Policy</li>
<li>Intellfence Acceptable Use Policy</li>
<li>Intellfence Incident Response Plan</li>
<li>NIST Special Publication 800-63B: Digital Identity Guidelines</li>
<li>ISO/IEC 27001:2022 Standard</li>
</ol>
<h2>11.0 Review and Maintenance</h2>
<p>This policy shall be reviewed by the Information Security Team at least annually, or more frequently in response to significant security incidents, changes in technology, or updates to relevant laws and standards. Proposed changes will be circulated to relevant stakeholders for comment and require final approval from the CISO. The version history below documents all revisions.</p>
<h2>Version History</h2>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th align="left">Version</th>
<th align="left">Effective Date</th>
<th align="left">Approved By</th>
<th align="left">Summary of Changes</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td align="left">1.0</td>
<td align="left">October 26, 2023</td>
<td align="left">CISO</td>
<td align="left">Initial policy release.</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
', 'draft', 1, '', '2026-02-20 06:13:22.535017', '2026-02-20 06:13:22.535017', 'general', true, '"{}"', NULL, NULL, 'pending', '2027-02-20 00:00:00', NULL),
	(1617, 3, 32, NULL, 'Remote Access Policy', '# Intellfence Remote Access Security Policy

## 1.0 Purpose and Objectives

This Remote Access Policy establishes the mandatory security controls, governance framework, and operational procedures for all remote access connections to Intellfence''s internal networks, systems, applications, and data repositories. As a cybersecurity enterprise, Intellfence handles highly sensitive intellectual property, threat intelligence, client security data, and proprietary analysis tools. The primary purpose of this policy is to protect the confidentiality, integrity, and availability (CIA triad) of these critical information assets by ensuring all remote access is provisioned, authenticated, encrypted, monitored, and managed in a secure and compliant manner.

The policy objectives are to: 1) Define a standardized and secure method for remote connectivity that minimizes the attack surface; 2) Enforce the principle of least privilege and need-to-know for all remote users; 3) Ensure all remote access activities are logged and auditable to meet stringent compliance requirements; 4) Mitigate risks associated with endpoint compromise, credential theft, and network-based attacks; and 5) Provide a clear framework that aligns with industry best practices and certifications including ISO/IEC 27001:2022, SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria (Security, Availability, Confidentiality), and the NIST Cybersecurity Framework.

## 2.0 Scope

This policy applies universally within Intellfence and to all external parties granted access to corporate resources. Its scope is comprehensive and includes:

**Personnel Scope:** All Intellfence employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, interns, and authorized third-party agents (e.g., vendors, partners) who require access to internal resources from any external location. Collectively, these individuals are termed "Authorized Users."

**Technical Scope:** All methods and technologies used to establish a remote connection to Intellfence assets. This includes, but is not limited to:
*   Virtual Private Networks (VPNs) for network-layer access.
*   Remote desktop and application protocols (e.g., RDP, VNC, Citrix, HTML5 portals).
*   Secure shell (SSH) for administrative access to servers and network devices.
*   Cloud-based secure access service edge (SASE) or zero-trust network access (ZTNA) solutions.
*   Direct access to cloud-hosted applications (e.g., CRM, ERP, development platforms).

**Asset Scope:** All corporate-owned, personally-owned (BYOD), and third-party-provided devices (e.g., contractor laptops) that are used to initiate a remote access connection to Intellfence resources. This includes workstations, laptops, tablets, and mobile devices.

**Data and System Scope:** All internal networks, data centers, cloud environments (IaaS/PaaS/SaaS), servers, workstations, applications, and data classified as "Internal," "Confidential," or "Restricted" according to the Intellfence Data Classification Policy.

## 3.0 Policy Definitions

**Authorized User:** An individual who has been formally granted permission to access Intellfence resources, following the procedures in Section 5.1.

**Corporate Endpoint:** Any device that is procured, managed, and fully controlled by Intellfence''s IT department, with mandatory security agents installed.

**BYOD (Bring Your Own Device):** A personally-owned device authorized for limited corporate use under the terms of a separate BYOD Agreement, which incorporates the security requirements of this policy.

**Endpoint Detection and Response (EDR):** A mandatory security agent that provides advanced monitoring, threat detection, and incident response capabilities on an endpoint device.

**Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA):** An authentication mechanism that requires the user to present two or more distinct factors from the categories: knowledge (something you know, e.g., password), possession (something you have, e.g., hardware token, smartphone app), and inherence (something you are, e.g., biometrics).

**Privileged Access:** Access rights that elevate a user''s capabilities beyond those of a standard user, allowing for the installation, configuration, management, or significant alteration of systems, applications, networks, or security controls. This includes administrative, root, and service accounts.

**Virtual Private Network (VPN):** A service that creates a secure, encrypted tunnel over a public network (e.g., the Internet) to connect a remote device to the corporate network as if it were locally attached.

**Zero-Trust Network Access (ZTNA):** A security model that grants access to specific applications or services based on strict identity verification and contextual factors, without placing the user on the broader network.

## 4.0 Policy Statement

Intellfence mandates that all remote access to corporate information resources shall be strictly controlled, centrally managed, and continuously monitored. Remote access is a privilege granted based on demonstrable business necessity and contingent upon strict adherence to the security requirements defined in this policy. The default stance is to deny remote access; it is only permitted where explicitly authorized and configured in accordance with these controls.

Unauthorized establishment of remote access connections, the use of unapproved tools or methods (including "shadow IT" solutions), or any deliberate circumvention of defined security controls (such as disabling endpoint agents or attempting to bypass MFA) are strictly prohibited. Such actions constitute a serious violation of this policy and will result in immediate corrective action.

## 5.0 Detailed Security Requirements and Controls

### 5.1 Authorization, Provisioning, and Lifecycle Management

All requests for remote access must follow a formal workflow. A completed "Remote Access Request Form" must be submitted by the user''s direct manager or department head, detailing the business justification, required resources, and requested access level. This form requires subsequent review and approval by the Information Security team to validate security compliance and enforce the principle of least privilege.

Access rights shall be provisioned only after approval is granted. The IT department will configure access in accordance with the approved scope. All remote access privileges, including VPN accounts and application-specific remote access, must be reviewed and re-certified by the user''s manager and the InfoSec team on a quarterly basis. Access must be immediately revoked upon termination of employment, contract conclusion, role change, or when business need no longer exists.

### 5.2 Authentication and Identity Controls

Password-only authentication for remote access is categorically forbidden. All remote access sessions, regardless of method (VPN, ZTNA, cloud app), must be protected by Intellfence-approved Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA). The primary MFA method shall be a time-based one-time password (TOTP) via a dedicated authenticator app (e.g., Duo, Microsoft Authenticator) or a FIDO2-compliant security key. SMS-based OTP is prohibited for privileged access due to known vulnerabilities.

For privileged remote access (e.g., administrative SSH, RDP to servers), MFA is required at both the initial network/access gateway *and* again at the point of destination (the server/application). All service accounts used for automated remote access must utilize certificate-based authentication or managed identities, with credentials stored in a privileged access management (PAM) vault.

### 5.3 Connection and Network Security

All remote network-layer access must be routed through the corporate-approved VPN gateway or a modern SASE/ZTNA provider. The VPN must utilize strong, industry-accepted cryptographic protocols: encryption shall be AES-256-GCM or stronger, and key exchange shall use IKEv2 with perfect forward secrecy (PFS) enabled.

Split-tunneling, which allows some device traffic to bypass the VPN, is prohibited for all general users. All client traffic must be forced through the corporate VPN tunnel while connected to ensure all traffic is inspected and protected by corporate security controls. Limited exceptions for split-tunneling may be granted by the CISO for specific, justified technical reasons and must be documented.

### 5.4 Endpoint Security Posture Requirements

Any device used to access Intellfence resources remotely must meet minimum security posture standards, verified before or during connection. Corporate-owned endpoints must have the following mandatory controls, managed by a Mobile Device Management (MDM) or Unified Endpoint Management (UEM) platform:
*   **Endpoint Detection and Response (EDR):** Agent must be installed, running, and reporting to the central console.
*   **Next-Generation Antivirus (NGAV):** Must be enabled, with signatures updated within 24 hours of release.
*   **Host-Based Firewall:** Configured in deny-by-default mode for inbound connections.
*   **Full-Disk Encryption:** Enabled using BitLocker (Windows) or FileVault (macOS).
*   **Operating System:** Must be a currently supported version, with all critical and high-severity security patches applied within 7 days of release. Automatic updates must be enabled.
*   **Screen Lock:** Automatic screen lock with a password/PIN required after a maximum of 5 minutes of inactivity.

For BYOD devices, a separate BYOD agreement governs use. At a minimum, a company-managed secure container or workspace app must be used, which enforces equivalent security policies for corporate data and applications accessed. Direct network-level VPN access from BYOD devices is typically prohibited.

### 5.5 Session Management, Monitoring, and Timeouts

To mitigate risks from unattended sessions and credential reuse, strict session controls are enforced:
*   Remote desktop and application sessions shall automatically lock after **10 minutes** of user inactivity.
*   All remote access sessions (VPN, ZTNA) shall be terminated after a maximum of **8 hours** of continuous connectivity, requiring full re-authentication.
*   Concurrent sessions from the same user account may be limited based on role.
*   All session establishment, successful/unsuccessful authentication attempts, duration, and termination events must be logged centrally. For privileged sessions, keystroke logging and video recording may be enabled for audit and forensic purposes.

### 5.6 Prohibited Activities, Behaviors, and Environments

Authorized Users must exercise heightened vigilance when working remotely. The following activities and scenarios are explicitly prohibited:
*   Accessing "Confidential" or "Restricted" Intellfence data or systems from public, unsecured Wi-Fi networks (e.g., coffee shops, airports, hotels) without an active VPN connection. The use of a personal mobile hotspot is preferred over public Wi-Fi.
*   Using corporate credentials (username/password) for personal online services or non-corporate systems.
*   Storing sensitive corporate data on the local hard drive, USB drives, or unsynchronized cloud storage (e.g., personal Dropbox, Google Drive) of a remote device unless the data is encrypted using corporate-approved encryption tools and such storage is explicitly authorized for the task.
*   Allowing family members, friends, or other unauthorized individuals to use a device that is configured for corporate remote access.
*   Performing high-risk security testing, threat hunting, or malware analysis on a device connected via remote access without prior written authorization from the InfoSec team and appropriate network segmentation.

## 6.0 Compliance and Audit Alignment

This policy is explicitly designed to implement and satisfy controls from key industry frameworks:
*   **ISO/IEC 27001:2022:** Annex A controls including A.5.15 (Access Control), A.5.16 (Identity Management), A.8.22 (Segregation of Networks), A.8.23 (Web Filtering), A.8.24 (Use of Cryptography), A.8.25 (Secure Development Lifecycle), and A.8.31 (Separation of Development, Test, and Production Environments).
*   **SOC 2:** Relevant Trust Services Criteria for Security (CC6.1, CC6.3, CC6.7, CC6.8), Availability (A1.2), and Confidentiality (C1.2).
*   **General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR)/Data Protection:** Ensures remote access processing of personal data incorporates appropriate technical and organizational measures (Article 32).

All security logs related to remote access (authentication, authorization, network flows, endpoint compliance) must be forwarded to the centralized Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) system. These logs must be maintained in a non-alterable format for a minimum of 90 days for routine analysis, with all security incident-related logs retained for a period of one year to support forensic investigations and audit requirements.

## 7.0 Roles and Responsibilities

**Information Security Team:** Holds primary responsibility for the development, maintenance, and enforcement of this policy. Duties include designing technical controls, conducting periodic vulnerability assessments of the remote access infrastructure, reviewing access logs for anomalies, and leading incident response related to remote access breaches.

**IT Department / Network Engineering Team:** Responsible for the operational provisioning, maintenance, hardening, availability, and technical support of the remote access infrastructure (e.g., VPN concentrators, ZTNA proxies, RADIUS servers). They implement the technical controls as directed by the InfoSec team.

**Authorized Users:** Are personally responsible for understanding and adhering to all stipulations of this policy. This includes securing their physical workspace, maintaining the security posture of their endpoint device, using strong unique passwords with MFA, locking sessions when away, and immediately reporting any lost/stolen devices or suspected security incidents (e.g., phishing attempts, credential leaks) to the InfoSec team.

**Department Heads / People Managers:** Are responsible for validating the business necessity of remote access requests for their team members, ensuring their staff complete required security awareness training, and enforcing compliance with this policy within their departments.

**Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):** Bears ultimate accountability for the effectiveness of the remote access security program, approves significant changes to this policy, and reports on its status to executive management.

## 8.0 Policy Enforcement and Violations

Compliance with this policy is mandatory. The Information Security team is authorized to perform periodic and random technical audits to verify compliance. Any user or device found to be in violation of this policy may be subject to immediate remedial action, including but not limited to: disabling of remote access privileges, revocation of network access, or isolation of the endpoint device.

Violations of this policy will be treated with appropriate severity based on the circumstances and impact. Disciplinary action for employees may range from a formal warning to termination of employment. For contractors and third parties, violations may result in termination of the business relationship and potential legal action. All violations will be investigated, and lessons learned will be used to improve the policy and controls.

## 9.0 Policy Review and Maintenance

This Remote Access Policy is a living document that must evolve with the threat landscape and business needs. It shall be formally reviewed and assessed by the Information Security team at least annually. Additionally, an ad-hoc review must be triggered following any significant security incident involving remote access, major changes to the IT infrastructure, or the adoption of new remote access technologies.

All proposed revisions must be documented, reviewed by relevant stakeholders (IT, Legal, HR), and approved by the Chief Information Security Officer (CISO) before publication and communication to all Authorized Users.', 'draft', 1, 'emmanuel', '2026-02-20 09:11:54.048233', '2026-02-20 09:11:54.048233', 'general', true, '"{}"', NULL, NULL, 'pending', NULL, NULL),
	(1619, 3, 33, NULL, 'Password Policy', '# Intellfence Password Policy

## 1.0 Purpose

This Password Policy establishes the mandatory technical and administrative controls for the creation, management, protection, and secure disposal of all authentication credentials at Intellfence. As a cybersecurity organization, our credibility and operational integrity are intrinsically linked to the robustness of our own security posture. The primary objective of this policy is to safeguard corporate information assets, client data, and intellectual property by ensuring that passwords and other secrets are cryptographically strong, kept confidential, and resistant to modern attack vectors. This directly protects the confidentiality, integrity, and availability (CIA triad) of our systems and data, forming a critical component of our defense-in-depth strategy and underpinning trust with our clients and partners.

## 2.0 Scope

This policy applies universally and without exception to all individuals and entities that interact with Intellfence information systems. This includes, but is not limited to, full-time and part-time employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, interns, third-party vendors, and service providers. It governs all forms of authentication credentials used to access Intellfence resources, including user account passwords, privileged administrative passwords, service account credentials, application secrets, API keys, database passwords, and encryption key passphrases. The policy applies irrespective of the resource''s location: on-premises infrastructure, cloud-hosted platforms (IaaS, PaaS, SaaS), hybrid environments, corporate-owned devices, and Bring Your Own Device (BYOD) scenarios where corporate data is accessed.

## 3.0 Definitions

**Password:** A confidential sequence of characters, known only to the user and the system, used to authenticate an identity and authorize access to information resources. In this policy, "password" is often used synonymously with passphrase, though composition rules differ.

**Passphrase:** A longer sequence of words or other text used for authentication, often easier to remember than a complex password while providing significant entropy (e.g., "Cerulean-Dolphin-7-Synchronize!").

**Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA):** An authentication method that requires the presentation of two or more distinct verification factors from the following categories: knowledge (something you know, like a password), possession (something you have, like a hardware token or smartphone app), and inherence (something you are, like a biometric). Also referred to as Two-Factor Authentication (2FA).

**Privileged Account:** Any user account with elevated permissions beyond those of a standard user, including but not limited to: Domain/System Administrators, Database Administrators (DBA), Network Administrators, Cloud Subscription Owners, and Application Administrators.

**Service Account:** A non-human account used by an operating system, application, or service to interact with other systems, databases, or network resources. These accounts often have high-level permissions and are not associated with an individual person.

**Password Hash:** A cryptographically scrambled, fixed-length representation of a password generated via a one-way function. The hash is stored instead of the plaintext password, and during authentication, the input password is hashed and compared to the stored hash.

**Password Manager:** A software application designed to securely store, generate, and manage online credentials in an encrypted vault, protected by a single master password and MFA.

**Brute-Force Attack:** An attack method that attempts to discover a password by systematically checking all possible combinations of characters.

**Credential Stuffing:** An attack that uses automated tools to inject vast numbers of username/password pairs (typically obtained from breaches on other sites) into login forms to fraudulently gain access to user accounts.

## 4.0 Policy Statement

Intellfence mandates that all authentication credentials shall be created, managed, transmitted, stored, and disposed of according to the stringent, risk-based requirements defined in this policy. The use of weak, default, shared, or known-compromised passwords is strictly prohibited and constitutes a serious security violation. All personnel are personally accountable for understanding and adhering to these controls. The technical enforcement of this policy is non-negotiable and is integrated into all relevant systems. Adherence is not merely an IT requirement but a fundamental condition of employment and engagement with Intellfence, essential for maintaining our security-first culture and market reputation.

## 5.0 Detailed Requirements and Controls

### 5.1 Password Composition and Complexity

All user-created passwords must be constructed to maximize entropy and resist both online guessing and offline cracking attacks. The following minimum requirements are enforced:

**Minimum Length:** For standard user accounts, the minimum password length is 14 characters. For all privileged administrative accounts (as defined in Section 3.0), the minimum length is 20 characters. A passphrase structure is strongly encouraged for privileged accounts.

**Character Diversity:** Passwords must include characters from at least three (3) of the following four character categories:
*   Uppercase letters (A through Z)
*   Lowercase letters (a through z)
*   Base 10 numerals (0 through 9)
*   Non-alphanumeric special characters (e.g., ! @ # $ % ^ & * ( ) ` ~ - _ = + [ ] { } \ | ; : '' " , . < > / ?).

**Prohibited Elements:** Passwords must NOT contain:
*   The user''s account name (username, email alias, or significant parts thereof).
*   Simple, repetitive, or sequential characters (e.g., "12345678", "abcdefg", "aaaaaaa", "qwerty").
*   Common dictionary words in any language, used in isolation.
*   Context-specific words easily associated with the user or Intellfence (e.g., "Intellfence2024", "CyberSecurity", "Password").
*   Any password known to have been compromised in a public data breach. Systems shall integrate with services (e.g., haveibeenpwned API) to check new passwords against known breach corpora where technically feasible.

### 5.2 Password Lifecycle Management

**Initial Provisioning & First Use:** All initial or reset passwords shall be system-generated, unique, and meet the complexity requirements in Section 5.1. They must be transmitted to the user via a secure, out-of-band method (e.g., encrypted email with a separate passcode sent via SMS, secure enterprise messaging platform, or in-person delivery). The user is forced to change this temporary password immediately upon first successful login.

**Password Expiration & Rotation:**
*   **Standard User Accounts:** Passwords must be changed at least every 90 days.
*   **Privileged Administrative Accounts:** Passwords must be changed at least every 60 days. More frequent rotation (e.g., 30 days) may be mandated for accounts with access to critical infrastructure or sensitive client data.
*   **Service Accounts:** Passwords or secrets must be rotated at least every 180 days. Additionally, they must be rotated immediately upon the departure of any personnel who had knowledge of the credential, or upon suspicion of compromise.
*   **Exception - Federated Accounts:** For accounts using federated identity (e.g., SSO via Okta, Azure AD), password expiration is governed by the identity provider''s policy, which must meet or exceed the standards set herein.

**Password History:** Systems shall enforce a password history policy that prevents the reuse of a user''s previous 10 passwords.

### 5.3 Password Protection and Storage

**Cryptographic Storage:** Passwords shall never be stored, transmitted in logs, or displayed in plaintext. All passwords at rest must be hashed using a strong, modern, and industry-approved cryptographic hashing algorithm designed for password storage. Acceptable algorithms include bcrypt, scrypt, Argon2id, or PBKDF2 with a sufficiently high work factor (iteration count > 100,000). The use of deprecated algorithms like MD5 or SHA-1 is expressly forbidden.

**Secure Transmission:** Passwords shall only be transmitted over encrypted channels using strong cryptographic protocols. All authentication interfaces (web, API, etc.) must use TLS 1.2 or higher (preferably TLS 1.3) with recommended cipher suites.

**Prohibition of Insecure Storage:** Personnel are strictly forbidden from:
*   Writing down passwords on sticky notes, notebooks, or whiteboards.
*   Storing passwords in unencrypted files (e.g., Word documents, Excel spreadsheets, text files).
*   Saving passwords in a web browser''s built-in password manager that is not part of the corporate-approved and managed solution.
*   Sharing passwords via email, instant messaging, or other unencrypted communication channels.

**Approved Password Managers:** The use of the corporate-approved enterprise password manager is mandatory for storing any work-related credentials, including personal accounts used for work purposes. The master password for the password manager must adhere to the composition rules in 5.1 and be protected with MFA.

### 5.4 Authentication and Access Controls

**Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA):** MFA is mandatory for the following access scenarios without exception:
*   All remote access to the Intellfence network (VPN, RDP gateways, etc.).
*   Access to all cloud service administration consoles (e.g., AWS Management Console, Azure Portal, GCP Console).
*   All users with privileged access rights, regardless of access method (local or remote).
*   Access to any system or application containing sensitive data as classified by the Data Classification Policy.
*   The preferred MFA factor is a cryptographic authenticator app (e.g., Microsoft Authenticator, Duo) or a FIDO2/WebAuthn security key. SMS-based one-time passwords (OTP) are permitted only where no other method is available and are considered a lower-assurance factor.

**Account Lockout:** To mitigate online brute-force attacks, systems shall automatically lock a user account after 5 consecutive failed authentication attempts within a 15-minute rolling window. The lockout duration shall be a minimum of 15 minutes, after which the account automatically unlocks, or requires manual intervention by the IT Help Desk. All lockout events must be logged and generate an alert to the Security Operations Center (SOC) for analysis.

**Session Management:**
*   **Inactivity Timeout:** Interactive user sessions for all applications and systems shall automatically terminate after a maximum of 15 minutes of inactivity. For highly sensitive systems, a shorter timeout (e.g., 5 minutes) may be enforced.
*   **Session Tokens:** Session identifiers must be cryptographically random, transmitted securely (HTTPS), and invalidated both on the server and client upon user logout, browser closure, or session timeout.
*   **Concurrent Sessions:** The number of concurrent sessions per user may be limited based on role and system criticality.

### 5.5 Administrative and Technical Enforcement

**Centralized Identity Management:** All user authentication shall be managed through a centralized Identity and Access Management (IAM) system or a federated identity provider (IdP) to ensure consistent policy application, provisioning, and de-provisioning.

**Automated Compliance Checking:** The Information Security Team shall deploy automated tools to periodically scan infrastructure, source code repositories, and configuration files for hard-coded credentials, default passwords, and weak password policies. Regular audits of service account usage and password strength will be conducted.

**Logging, Monitoring, and Alerting:** All authentication-related events must be logged with sufficient detail to support forensic investigation and audit requirements. Logs must include, at minimum: timestamp, source IP address, user identifier (username), target system/service, event type (success, failure, password change, lockout), and, for failures, the reason. These logs must be aggregated into the Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) system, retained for a minimum of 90 days (with 1 year recommended for privileged access events), and monitored for anomalous patterns indicative of attack.

## 6.0 Compliance and Audit Alignment

This policy is designed to provide clear, auditable controls that align with major information security and privacy frameworks relevant to Intellfence''s operations in North America and the European Union.

**ISO/IEC 27001:2022:** Directly supports Annex A controls, including A.5.15 (Identity management), A.5.16 (Identity, authentication, and access), A.5.17 (Authentication information), A.8.2 (Privileged access rights), and A.8.9 (Logging).

**SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria:** Provides the control evidence for Security (CC6.1 - Logical and Physical Access Controls), specifically around credential management, MFA, and session termination, which also supports the Confidentiality and Privacy criteria.

**NIST Cybersecurity Framework (CSF) & NIST SP 800-63B:** The password and MFA requirements are aligned with NIST''s latest digital identity guidelines, promoting phishing-resistant authentication and sane password policies.

**General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR):** Supports compliance with Article 32 (Security of Processing) by implementing appropriate technical measures, including pseudonymization and encryption, to ensure a level of security appropriate to the risk of processing personal data.

**Industry Best Practice:** Aligns with recommendations from cybersecurity authorities such as CISA (Cybersecurity & Infrastructure Security Agency) and ENISA (European Union Agency for Cybersecurity) regarding strong authentication and credential hygiene.

Non-compliance with this policy may result in significant audit findings, failure to obtain or maintain security certifications, regulatory penalties, legal liability, and severe reputational damage for Intellfence.

## 7.0 Roles and Responsibilities

**Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):** Holds ultimate authority and accountability for the approval, implementation, communication, and overall enforcement of this policy. The CISO is responsible for ensuring the policy remains current with the threat landscape and compliance requirements.

**Information Security Team:** Responsible for the development, maintenance, dissemination, and technical oversight of this policy. This includes configuring IAM/IdP systems, reviewing authentication logs and alerts, conducting periodic audits, and investigating potential violations.

**IT Infrastructure & Operations Teams:** Responsible for implementing and maintaining the technical controls specified in Section 5 across all on-premises and cloud-based systems, servers, networks, and applications under their management. This includes enforcing password policies in Active Directory, Azure AD, and other directory services.

**All Personnel (Employees, Contractors, Vendors):** Individually responsible for reading, understanding, and complying with all aspects of this policy. This includes creating strong, unique passwords, safeguarding them using approved methods, diligently using MFA, and immediately reporting any suspected password compromise or policy violation to the Information Security Team.

**Human Resources (HR):** Responsible for integrating policy acknowledgment into the employee and contractor onboarding process, and for ensuring the IT department is promptly notified of terminations to facilitate account deactivation as part of the offboarding procedure.

**Department Heads and Managers:** Responsible for promoting a culture of security within their teams, ensuring their staff have the resources and training to comply, and supporting enforcement actions.

## 8.0 Policy Violations and Enforcement

Adherence to this policy is a condition of employment and engagement with Intellfence. Any individual found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, the severity of which will be commensurate with the nature and impact of the violation. Actions may include, but are not limited to: formal written warning, mandatory security re-training, suspension of system access, termination of employment or contract, and pursuit of legal action in cases involving gross negligence or malicious intent.

The Information Security Team is explicitly authorized to immediately disable or quarantine any account found to be in violation of this policy (e.g., using a compromised password, sharing credentials) to mitigate imminent risk to the organization. All suspected and confirmed violations shall be documented, investigated, and reported in accordance with the Intellfence Incident Response Policy.

## 9.0 Policy Review and Maintenance

This Password Policy is a living document that must evolve with the changing threat landscape, technology, and regulatory environment. It shall be formally reviewed by the Information Security Team, in consultation with key stakeholders, at least annually. An ad-hoc review must also be triggered following any significant security incident involving credentials, a major change in the compliance landscape, or a fundamental shift in authentication technology.

All proposed revisions must be documented, assessed for risk and impact, and submitted to the CISO for formal approval. Upon approval, the revised policy shall be communicated to all personnel, and the previous version shall be archived. The latest approved version of this policy shall be made readily accessible to all personnel through the official corporate policy repository.', 'draft', 1, 'emmanuel', '2026-02-20 09:14:50.338828', '2026-02-20 09:14:50.338828', 'general', true, '"{}"', NULL, NULL, 'pending', NULL, NULL),
	(1618, 3, 56, NULL, 'Data Protection Policy', '# Intellfence Data Protection Policy

## 1.0 Purpose and Objectives

This Data Protection Policy establishes the mandatory framework for protecting the confidentiality, integrity, and availability (CIA triad) of all data assets owned, processed, or controlled by Intellfence. As a cybersecurity enterprise, our foundational trust with clients, partners, and employees is predicated on our ability to exemplify the highest standards of data stewardship. This policy defines the principles, administrative, technical, and physical controls, and organizational responsibilities required to safeguard data against unauthorized access, disclosure, alteration, destruction, or loss. The primary objectives are to ensure business resilience, protect intellectual property and client assets, maintain regulatory compliance, and uphold our contractual commitments. This document is designed to satisfy core requirements of major compliance frameworks including ISO 27001:2022, SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria, the EU General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR), and the California Consumer Privacy Act (CCPA), providing a unified control baseline.

## 2.0 Scope

This policy applies universally and without exception to all Intellfence employees, contractors, consultants, interns, temporary staff, and any other third-party individuals or entities (collectively referred to as "Personnel") who access, process, store, or transmit any data owned by or entrusted to Intellfence. It encompasses all data, irrespective of its format (electronic, paper, magnetic, optical, etc.) or state (data-at-rest, data-in-transit, data-in-use). The policy governs all company-owned or managed systems, networks, applications, endpoints, and physical facilities. This scope explicitly includes data processed in cloud environments (IaaS, PaaS, SaaS), and extends to personal devices (BYOD) when authorized for business purposes, which must comply with the corporate Mobile Device Management (MDM) and Acceptable Use policies.

## 3.0 Definitions

**Personal Data / Personally Identifiable Information (PII):** Any information relating to an identified or identifiable natural person (''data subject''), as defined by the GDPR and analogous regulations. This includes direct identifiers (name, ID number, location data) and indirect identifiers which, when combined, can lead to identification.
**Protected Health Information (PHI):** A subset of PII defined by the Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act (HIPAA), concerning health status, provision of healthcare, or payment for healthcare.
**Confidential Data:** Non-public proprietary and business-critical information, including trade secrets, source code, vulnerability research, unpublished financial data, strategic business plans, merger/acquisition details, and pre-release product information.
**Sensitive Data:** A special category of Personal Data requiring heightened protection due to its nature, as defined by GDPR Article 9. This includes data revealing racial or ethnic origin, political opinions, religious or philosophical beliefs, trade union membership, genetic data, biometric data for uniquely identifying a person, data concerning health, or data concerning a natural person''s sex life or sexual orientation.
**Data Processing:** Any operation or set of operations performed on data, whether or not by automated means, including but not limited to: collection, recording, organization, structuring, storage, adaptation, alteration, retrieval, consultation, use, disclosure by transmission, dissemination, alignment, combination, restriction, erasure, or destruction.
**Data Controller:** The natural or legal person, public authority, agency, or other body which, alone or jointly with others, determines the purposes and means of the processing of Personal Data.
**Data Processor:** A natural or legal person, public authority, agency, or other body which processes Personal Data on behalf of the Controller.
**Encryption at Rest:** The cryptographic protection of stored data using a symmetric algorithm with a minimum key strength of AES-256. This applies to databases, file systems, backups, and portable media.
**Encryption in Transit:** The cryptographic protection of data traversing a network using a secure protocol, mandating TLS 1.2 or higher with strong cipher suites. Use of SSLv3, TLS 1.0, or TLS 1.1 is explicitly prohibited.
**Data Breach:** A security incident that leads to the accidental or unlawful destruction, loss, alteration, unauthorized disclosure of, or access to, data transmitted, stored, or otherwise processed.

## 4.0 Policy Statement

Intellfence is committed to a principle-based approach to data protection, adhering to the core tenets of lawfulness, fairness, transparency, purpose limitation, data minimization, accuracy, storage limitation, integrity, confidentiality, and accountability. All data shall be protected by a defense-in-depth strategy with controls commensurate to its classification, sensitivity, criticality, and associated legal and contractual obligations. Personnel are strictly prohibited from accessing, using, or disclosing any data except as expressly necessary to perform their authorized job functions. The organization will implement robust technical safeguards, enforce stringent administrative procedures, and foster a culture of security awareness to ensure the resilient protection of all data assets under our stewardship.

## 5.0 Detailed Control Requirements

### 5.1 Data Classification and Handling

All data assets must be classified at the point of creation or receipt according to the corporate **Data Classification Standard**. The four-tier classification scheme is:
*   **Public:** Data approved for general public disclosure (e.g., marketing brochures, public blog posts).
*   **Internal:** Data not sensitive but intended for internal use only (e.g., internal memos, non-sensitive operational policies).
*   **Confidential:** Sensitive business data requiring protection (e.g., client proposals, internal financial reports, product roadmaps, most PII).
*   **Restricted:** Highly sensitive data where unauthorized disclosure would cause severe damage (e.g., source code, vulnerability data pre-disclosure, cryptographic keys, Sensitive Data as defined by GDPR).

Handling procedures, enforced through technical controls and user training, mandate:
*   **Labeling:** Electronic documents and emails containing Confidential or Restricted data must be clearly labeled (e.g., headers/footers, subject line tags).
*   **Storage:** Restricted data must be stored only on approved, centrally managed systems with encryption-at-rest. Storage on local workstation drives is prohibited unless encrypted and authorized.
*   **Transmission:** Confidential data may be transmitted internally over corporate networks. Transmission of Restricted data and any Confidential data externally requires encryption (e.g., secure file transfer, encrypted email).
*   **Disposal:** Data must be disposed of securely per its classification. Digital data requires secure deletion tools; physical media requires degaussing or physical destruction following NIST SP 800-88 guidelines.

### 5.2 Access Control and Authentication

Access to data and systems is governed by the principle of least privilege and need-to-know.
*   **Provisioning:** All access requests must be formally submitted, approved by the relevant Data Owner or system custodian, and implemented by the IT team. Access rights must be reviewed quarterly.
*   **Authentication:** Multi-factor authentication (MFA) is mandatory for all personnel accessing any corporate system, with particular emphasis on systems housing Confidential or Restricted data, administrative interfaces, and remote access (VPN, cloud consoles).
*   **Privileged Access Management (PAM):** Privileged accounts (e.g., root, Administrator, Domain Admin) are subject to heightened controls including just-in-time access provisioning, session monitoring and recording, and regular credential rotation. Shared privileged accounts are prohibited.
*   **Segregation of Duties:** Conflicts of interest are prevented by ensuring no single individual has end-to-end control over a critical process (e.g., the same person cannot request, approve, and implement a payment).

### 5.3 Encryption and Cryptographic Controls

Cryptography is a foundational control for protecting data confidentiality and integrity.
*   **Encryption at Rest:** All Restricted and Confidential data at rest must be encrypted using a minimum of AES-256. This applies to databases, file servers, cloud storage (e.g., S3, Blob Storage), backups, and endpoint hard drives (via Full Disk Encryption).
*   **Encryption in Transit:** All data transmissions over public or untrusted networks must be protected using TLS 1.2 or higher with forward secrecy. Internal transmissions between sensitive systems should also use encrypted channels.
*   **Key Management:** Cryptographic keys are treated as Restricted data. Keys must be generated, stored, rotated, and destroyed using a dedicated Key Management Service (KMS) or Hardware Security Module (HSM). Private keys and secret keys must never be stored in source code or configuration files.

### 5.4 Data Retention and Secure Disposal

Data shall not be retained indefinitely.
*   **Retention Schedule:** A formal Data Retention Schedule, developed in consultation with Legal and Data Owners, defines minimum and maximum retention periods for all data categories based on business need and regulatory requirements (e.g., tax law, GDPR storage limitation principle).
*   **Secure Disposal:** At the end of the retention period, data must be irreversibly destroyed. For electronic media, this requires cryptographic erasure (using NIST 800-88 Purge standards) or physical destruction. Paper documents containing Confidential or Restricted data must be shredded via cross-cut shredder (DIN 66399 Level P-4 or higher) or professionally incinerated.

### 5.5 Logging, Monitoring, and Incident Response

Proactive monitoring and a prepared response capability are critical.
*   **Logging:** All access to, and significant actions on, systems handling Confidential and Restricted data must be logged. Logs must capture at minimum: user identity, timestamp, source IP, action performed, target object/identifier, and success/failure status.
*   **Log Management:** Logs must be centrally aggregated in a Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) system, protected from tampering (e.g., write-once storage), and retained for a minimum of 90 days, with critical logs retained for one year or longer as required for compliance.
*   **Security Monitoring:** The Security Operations Center (SOC) or designated team shall monitor logs and security tool alerts for anomalous or malicious activity.
*   **Incident Response:** The formal **Security Incident Response Plan (SIRP)** governs the response to all data breaches and security incidents. Any suspected incident must be reported immediately to the CISO and/or Data Protection Officer (DPO), triggering the SIRP. The plan includes containment, eradication, recovery, notification procedures (to authorities and affected data subjects as mandated by law), and post-incident review.

### 5.6 Third-Party and Cloud Security

Intellfence remains accountable for data processed by its vendors.
*   **Vendor Risk Management:** A security assessment is mandatory prior to engaging any third-party vendor (Data Processor) that will handle, store, or process Intellfence data. Assessments evaluate the vendor''s security posture against our requirements.
*   **Data Processing Agreements (DPA):** A legally binding DPA that outlines security obligations, audit rights, sub-processor governance, and breach notification timelines is required for all Data Processors, as mandated by GDPR Article 28 and other regulations.
*   **Cloud Security:** For cloud services (AWS, Azure, GCP, SaaS), the Shared Responsibility Model is documented and understood. Configuration baselines (e.g., CIS Benchmarks) are enforced using Infrastructure as Code (IaC) and continuous compliance tools. Regular configuration audits and external penetration tests are conducted on cloud environments.

## 6.0 Compliance and Legal Obligations

Intellfence shall comply with all applicable data protection laws, regulations, and contractual obligations in every jurisdiction where it operates or processes data. This includes, but is not limited to:
*   **Global:** EU General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR), including international transfer mechanisms (Standard Contractual Clauses, UK Addendum).
*   **United States:** California Consumer Privacy Act (CCPA) as amended by the CPRA, and other state-level privacy laws.
*   **Industry Frameworks:** Adherence to control objectives from ISO 27001:2022 (particularly Annex A controls A.5-A.8, A.9, A.12, A.13, A.14, A.17, A.18), SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria (Security, Availability, Confidentiality, Privacy), and the NIST Cybersecurity Framework.
*   **Assessments:** Data Protection Impact Assessments (DPIAs) shall be conducted for any new high-risk processing activities (e.g., large-scale profiling, systematic monitoring of public areas). Records of Processing Activities (RoPA) are maintained as a GDPR Article 30 requirement.

## 7.0 Roles and Responsibilities

*   **Board of Directors / Executive Management:** Hold ultimate accountability for data protection governance, setting the tone from the top, and ensuring adequate resources are allocated to the security program.
*   **Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):** Responsible for the development, implementation, maintenance, and oversight of this policy and the overarching Information Security Management System (ISMS). Acts as the primary point of contact for security strategy and major incidents.
*   **Data Protection Officer (DPO):** Formally designated role required under GDPR Article 37. The DPO oversees compliance with data protection laws, advises on DPIAs, serves as the contact point for data subjects and supervisory authorities, and provides independent oversight.
*   **Data Owners (Business Function Heads / Product Managers):** Business leaders who are accountable for specific data sets. They classify data, authorize access requests, define retention periods, and approve acceptable use.
*   **Data Custodians (IT, DevOps, Security Teams):** Responsible for implementing and maintaining the technical and operational controls (access management, encryption, backups, patching) as specified by the Data Owners and security policies.
*   **All Personnel:** Mandated to complete annual data protection and security awareness training, understand and adhere to all policies and procedures, exercise due care in handling data, and report any security incidents, policy violations, or suspicious activity immediately through designated channels.

## 8.0 Policy Enforcement and Violations

Compliance with this policy is a condition of employment and engagement with Intellfence. The Information Security Department is authorized to monitor systems, networks, and traffic to ensure compliance and detect policy violations. Any violation may result in disciplinary action, proportionate to the severity and intent, up to and including termination of employment or contract, and pursuit of legal remedies. Deliberate acts such as circumventing security controls, unauthorized data exfiltration, failure to report a breach, or mishandling of client data constitute gross misconduct. All violations will be investigated, documented, and may be reported to law enforcement or regulatory bodies as required.

## 9.0 Policy Review and Maintenance

This policy shall be reviewed and approved by the CISO in conjunction with the Legal Department and the DPO at least annually. Reviews will also be triggered by significant changes in the threat landscape, business operations, technology architecture, or applicable laws and regulations. All revisions shall be formally version-controlled. Upon approval, changes will be communicated to all Personnel, and archived versions will be maintained. The effectiveness of the controls mandated by this policy shall be validated through a program of internal audits, external assessments (e.g., for ISO 27001 and SOC 2), and regular penetration testing.', 'draft', 1, 'emmanuel', '2026-02-20 09:13:00.914259', '2026-02-20 09:13:00.914259', 'general', true, '"{}"', NULL, NULL, 'pending', NULL, NULL),
	(1620, 3, 34, NULL, 'Wireless Security Policy', '# Intellfence Wireless Security Policy

## 1.0 Purpose

This Wireless Security Policy establishes the mandatory requirements for the secure deployment, configuration, management, and monitoring of all wireless networks and devices that access, transmit, or store Intellfence information assets. The policy is designed to protect the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of corporate data and systems from threats inherent in wireless communications, in alignment with Intellfence''s overall Information Security Management System (ISMS) and its commitment to cybersecurity excellence. It aims to mitigate risks including unauthorized network access, data interception, man-in-the-middle attacks, credential theft, and the introduction of malware via unsecured wireless channels.

## 2.0 Scope

This policy applies universally across Intellfence, encompassing all subsidiaries, business units, and physical locations globally. It governs all individuals, including employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, interns, and any other third-party affiliates. The policy covers all corporate-owned, leased, or managed wireless infrastructure (e.g., Wi-Fi access points, wireless controllers, bridges, sensors) and all endpoint devices—whether corporate-issued or personal (under a BYOD arrangement)—that connect to Intellfence wireless networks or that create wireless connections (e.g., ad-hoc networks, mobile tethering, Bluetooth pairings) to access corporate resources. This includes, but is not limited to, technologies such as Wi-Fi (all 802.11 standards), Bluetooth, Zigbee, cellular data modems (MiFi), and any subsequent emerging wireless technologies utilized for business purposes.

## 3.0 Definitions

**Access Point (AP) / Wireless Access Point (WAP):** A networking hardware device that allows Wi-Fi compliant client devices to connect to a wired network and to each other.

**Enterprise Wireless Network:** The centrally managed, authorized, and secure wireless network infrastructure deployed and maintained by the Intellfence Information Technology (IT) Department for use by authorized personnel and managed devices.

**Guest Wireless Network:** A logically and physically segregated wireless network provisioned for visitors, vendors, clients, or other non-employees. Access is strictly controlled via a captive portal and is limited to internet access only, with no direct pathway to internal corporate resources.

**Rogue Access Point:** Any wireless access point installed on the corporate network or operating within corporate facilities without the explicit written authorization of the Intellfence IT Department. This includes malicious devices, as well as unauthorized but well-intentioned devices such as personal wireless routers.

**WPA3-Enterprise (Wi-Fi Protected Access 3 Enterprise):** The current mandatory security certification and protocol for securing enterprise-grade Wi-Fi networks. It provides 256-bit Galois/Counter Mode Protocol (GCMP-256) encryption, forward secrecy, and robust authentication via the 802.1X/EAP framework.

**Wireless Intrusion Detection/Prevention System (WIDS/WIPS):** A dedicated security solution that monitors the radio frequency (RF) spectrum to detect, classify, and respond to rogue devices, misconfigured clients, policy violations, and malicious attack patterns.

## 4.0 Policy Statement

Intellfence mandates that all wireless network access serving corporate functions shall be implemented and maintained with security as the paramount design principle, reflecting our position within the cybersecurity industry. The use of wireless technologies is a privilege granted under strict administrative and technical controls to mitigate risks of unauthorized access, data interception, and network compromise. All wireless communications involving Intellfence data, systems, or intellectual property must be encrypted using strong, industry-vetted algorithms, authenticated via robust identity management, and continuously monitored for anomalous activity. The deployment of unauthorized wireless infrastructure, the circumvention of established security controls, or the connection of unauthorized devices is strictly prohibited and will be treated as a serious security violation.

## 5.0 Policy Requirements

### 5.1 Authorization and Architecture

5.1.1 All wireless infrastructure, including but not limited to access points, wireless controllers, management appliances, and wireless bridges, must be approved, procured, standardized, and deployed solely by the Intellfence Information Technology (IT) Department. No other department or individual is permitted to install wireless networking equipment.

5.1.2 A formal, up-to-date network architecture diagram detailing all wireless infrastructure must be maintained by the Network Team. This diagram must include physical and logical placement, coverage areas, channel assignments, transmit power settings, VLAN mappings, and upstream network connections. This architecture document shall be reviewed and validated at least annually, or after any significant network change.

5.1.3 The implementation of personal wireless access points, wireless routers, ad-hoc wireless networks, or wireless-enabled network switches within corporate facilities (including home offices used for corporate work) is expressly forbidden. This prohibition extends to the use of USB wireless adapters to create software-based access points on corporate machines.

### 5.2 Encryption and Authentication Standards

5.2.1 All enterprise wireless networks providing access to corporate resources shall utilize WPA3-Enterprise with AES-256-GCM encryption as the mandatory standard. Transitional use of WPA2-Enterprise is permitted only for legacy device support that is incapable of WPA3, and each such instance must be documented with a formal risk assessment and a time-bound migration plan approved by the CISO.

5.2.2 The use of deprecated and insecure protocols, including Wired Equivalent Privacy (WEP), WPA-Personal (Pre-Shared Key), or open/unencrypted networks for any corporate purpose, including testing or lab environments, is strictly forbidden. Lab environments must be physically or logically air-gapped from the production corporate network.

5.2.3 Authentication for the primary enterprise network must leverage a centralized identity provider (IdP), such as a RADIUS server, tightly integrated with the corporate directory service (e.g., Microsoft Active Directory, Azure Active Directory). The preferred authentication method is 802.1X/EAP-TLS using client certificates, with EAP-PEAP (MSCHAPv2) as a secondary, strictly configured option.

5.2.4 Guest network access shall be secured via a customizable captive portal system. Authentication shall require unique, time-limited credentials generated for each guest or a separate, complex Pre-Shared Key (PSK) that is rotated at least quarterly. Guest network traffic must be routed through a dedicated internet breakout point, segregated from corporate traffic by a firewall, and shall have no routing path to the internal corporate network.

### 5.3 Device Configuration and Hardening

5.3.1 All authorized wireless access points and controllers shall be hardened according to a standardized baseline configuration. This includes changing all default administrative credentials, disabling unnecessary services (e.g., Telnet, HTTP management), SNMP if not required, and ensuring firmware is maintained at a vendor-supported version. Critical security patches must be applied within 30 days of release, following a defined change management process.

5.3.2 Service Set Identifiers (SSIDs) shall be generic and must not contain information that could identify Intellfence, its specific business units, physical location, or the function of the network (e.g., "Intellfence_Corp," "Building1_Finance"). SSID broadcasting may be disabled for the enterprise network if client device management supports secure, pre-configured connections.

5.3.3 Wireless signal strength and antenna placement shall be professionally tuned to provide adequate coverage within required business areas while actively minimizing propagation beyond the physical and logical boundaries of Intellfence premises. This reduces the opportunity for war-driving and external eavesdropping.

### 5.4 Monitoring and Intrusion Detection

5.4.1 A dedicated Wireless Intrusion Detection and Prevention System (WIDS/WIPS) shall be deployed to provide continuous, 24/7 monitoring of the 2.4 GHz and 5 GHz (and other relevant) radio frequency (RF) spectrums at all Intellfence facilities. The system must be configured to automatically detect and alert on rogue devices, misconfigured clients, ad-hoc networks, evil twin attacks, and other malicious activity patterns.

5.4.2 Comprehensive logging is mandatory. All authentication successes and failures, administrative access events, configuration changes, and WIDS/WIPS alerts related to the wireless infrastructure must be forwarded to a centralized Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) system. Logs must be retained for a minimum period of 90 days to support incident investigation and compliance audits.

5.4.3 The Information Security Team, in coordination with the IT Department, shall conduct periodic wireless vulnerability assessments and authorized penetration tests at least annually. These assessments must include RF spectrum analysis, configuration reviews, and simulated attack vectors to identify and remediate security weaknesses proactively.

### 5.5 Endpoint and Client Security

5.5.1 All corporate-managed devices (laptops, smartphones, tablets) must have their wireless interfaces configured and controlled via a Mobile Device Management (MDM) or Unified Endpoint Management (UEM) solution. Policies must enforce automatic connection only to approved Intellfence SSIDs and prevent connections to untrusted or open networks.

5.5.2 Users are prohibited from configuring corporate devices to connect to untrusted or public wireless networks (e.g., coffee shops, airports, hotels) for the purpose of accessing sensitive corporate data, including email, source code repositories, or customer information, unless a mandated, company-provided and configured Virtual Private Network (VPN) with always-on or forced-tunnel capabilities is used to encrypt all traffic.

5.5.3 The use of personal devices (Bring Your Own Device - BYOD) to connect to the enterprise wireless network is permitted only through formal enrollment in the Intellfence BYOD program. Enrollment mandates device registration, agreement to security policies, installation of a management profile for basic health checks (e.g., OS version, passcode presence), and the use of containerization or secure workspace applications to isolate corporate data from personal data.

## 6.0 Compliance & Audit

This policy is designed to support and demonstrate compliance with relevant legal, regulatory, and contractual obligations, including but not limited to the General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR), the California Consumer Privacy Act (CCPA), and client-specific security requirements. The controls specified herein directly map to and support the following standards:

*   **ISO/IEC 27001:2022 Annex A:** Primarily A.8.9 (Wireless Access Control), A.8.15 (Logging), A.8.16 (Monitoring), A.5.7 (Threat Intelligence), A.8.1 (Asset Management), and A.8.12 (Data Leakage Prevention).
*   **SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria:** Security (CC6.1, CC6.3, CC6.6, CC6.7), Confidentiality, and Availability.
*   **NIST Cybersecurity Framework:** Protect (PR.AC-1, PR.AC-3, PR.DS-2), Detect (DE.AE-1, DE.CM-4), and Respond (RS.MI-1).

Any deviation from this policy for legitimate business or technical necessity requires the submission of a formal risk assessment and a Policy Exception Request, which must be reviewed and approved in writing by the Chief Information Security Officer (CISO). All exceptions will be documented, tracked, and reviewed periodically.

## 7.0 Roles and Responsibilities

**Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):** Bears ultimate responsibility for the approval, dissemination, oversight, and enforcement of this policy. The CISO is the final authority for granting policy exceptions.

**Information Technology (IT) Department / Network Team:** Responsible for the secure design, implementation, maintenance, patching, and operational monitoring of all authorized wireless infrastructure. This team manages the WIDS/WIPS, applies configuration standards, and executes the initial response to technical incidents involving wireless systems.

**Information Security Team:** Responsible for the periodic review and update of this policy, conducting and overseeing security assessments (vulnerability scans, penetration tests), analyzing escalated WIDS/WIPS alerts, investigating policy violations, and managing the security aspects of the BYOD program.

**All Users (Employees, Contractors, Third Parties):** Required to read, understand, and comply with all aspects of this policy. Users must utilize only authorized wireless networks for business activities, secure their devices as mandated, and immediately report any suspected security incidents, policy violations, or the presence of unauthorized wireless devices to the IT Help Desk or Information Security Team.

## 8.0 Enforcement and Violations

Compliance with this policy will be enforced through a combination of technical controls (MDM, WIDS/WIPS, network access control), automated monitoring (SIEM), and administrative procedures. Violations of this policy may result in disciplinary action, the nature of which will be commensurate with the severity of the violation. Actions may include, but are not limited to, formal written warnings, revocation of network access privileges, termination of employment, or termination of contractual agreements. Severe violations may also lead to civil or criminal liability.

The IT and Information Security Departments are explicitly authorized to immediately and without prior notice disconnect, disable, or quarantine any unauthorized wireless device or network that is found to be posing a credible security risk to Intellfence assets. All users shall be required to formally acknowledge receipt and understanding of this policy upon hire, upon any major revision, and at least annually thereafter.

## 9.0 Policy Review and Maintenance

This Wireless Security Policy shall be reviewed by the Information Security Team at least annually. Reviews may be conducted more frequently in response to significant security incidents, material changes in the threat landscape, the introduction of new wireless technologies, or updates to relevant compliance standards (e.g., ISO 27001, NIST publications). Proposed revisions must be documented and submitted to the CISO for final review and approval. The latest approved version of this policy shall be made accessible to all personnel through the official corporate policy repository or intranet.', 'draft', 1, 'emmanuel', '2026-02-20 09:15:48.023842', '2026-02-20 09:15:48.023842', 'general', true, '"{}"', NULL, NULL, 'pending', NULL, NULL),
	(1621, 3, 36, NULL, 'Secure SDLC Policy', '# Intellfence Secure Software Development Lifecycle (SDLC) Policy

## 1.0 Purpose

This Secure Software Development Lifecycle (SDLC) Policy establishes the mandatory security controls, processes, and governance framework for all software development activities at Intellfence. As a cybersecurity company, our products and internal systems are high-value targets and must embody the security principles we advocate. This policy mandates the systematic integration of security and privacy requirements throughout all phases of the software lifecycle to ensure the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of our applications, services, and data. This proactive approach is fundamental to managing risk, protecting our intellectual property and customer data, meeting contractual obligations, and maintaining the trust essential to our business. The policy is designed to provide a structured, repeatable, and auditable process that aligns with industry best practices and regulatory requirements.

## 2.0 Scope

This policy applies to all Intellfence personnel, including full-time and part-time employees, contractors, interns, and temporary staff, as well as third-party vendors and service providers who are involved in any aspect of the design, development, testing, deployment, maintenance, procurement, or operation of software applications, systems, platforms, or services on behalf of Intellfence. This encompasses, but is not limited to, software engineers, architects, site reliability engineers (SREs), quality assurance (QA) personnel, product managers, DevOps engineers, and security practitioners.

The policy covers all software assets, including:
*   Internally developed commercial and proprietary software products.
*   Internal business applications and automation tools.
*   Software-as-a-Service (SaaS) offerings developed and operated by Intellfence.
*   Modifications, configurations, and integrations of Commercial Off-The-Shelf (COTS) software.
*   APIs, microservices, and associated infrastructure-as-code (IaC).
*   Container images, virtual machine templates, and serverless function code.

## 3.0 Definitions

**Secure SDLC:** A structured and institutionalized process that integrates specific security activities, checkpoints, and governance into each phase of the software development lifecycle, from initial concept to decommissioning.

**Static Application Security Testing (SAST):** The analysis of application source code, bytecode, or binary code for security vulnerabilities, coding flaws, and compliance with secure coding standards without executing the program. SAST is typically performed early in the development cycle.

**Dynamic Application Security Testing (DAST):** The analysis of a running application in a test or staging environment for security vulnerabilities by simulating external attacks. DAST identifies runtime and environment-specific issues that SAST may miss.

**Interactive Application Security Testing (IAST):** A hybrid analysis technique that combines elements of SAST and DAST by using instrumentation within the running application to identify vulnerabilities in real-time during automated or manual testing.

**Software Composition Analysis (SCA):** The process of automatically identifying, inventorying, and managing open-source and third-party software components within an application. Its primary purposes are to detect known vulnerabilities (CVEs) and ensure license compliance.

**Software Bill of Materials (SBOM):** A formal, machine-readable inventory of software components, libraries, and dependencies, including their versions and hierarchical relationships, used to build an application. An SBOM is a critical output of SCA.

**Threat Modeling:** A structured, systematic process for identifying, quantifying, and addressing the security risks associated with an application. It involves identifying assets, defining security objectives, enumerating potential threats (e.g., using the STRIDE model), and specifying mitigations.

**Production Environment:** The live, customer-facing operational environment where end-users access and interact with the application and its data. This environment is subject to the highest level of security and availability controls.

**Secrets:** Any sensitive digital authentication credential, including passwords, API keys, cryptographic private keys, tokens, certificates, and connection strings.

## 4.0 Policy Statement

Intellfence is committed to building security into its products and systems by design and by default. Security and privacy are non-negotiable, first-class requirements that shall be defined, implemented, verified, and maintained throughout the entire software development lifecycle. The ad-hoc introduction of security controls as an afterthought or as a reaction to a security incident is strictly prohibited. All development teams are responsible for adhering to the principles and procedures outlined in this policy, ensuring that security is an integral part of our engineering culture and delivery process.

## 5.0 Secure SDLC Process Requirements

Security activities must be integrated into each phase of the SDLC. The following sections detail the mandatory requirements for each phase.

### 5.1 Requirements and Design Phase

Security must be considered from the inception of any project. This phase focuses on "shifting security left" by identifying and designing for security requirements before a single line of code is written.

5.1.1 **Security Requirements Gathering:** Functional and non-functional security requirements shall be explicitly documented in formal requirement specifications, user stories, and acceptance criteria. These requirements must be traceable through the development lifecycle and include considerations for authentication, authorization, data protection, audit logging, and regulatory compliance (e.g., data residency, privacy by design).

5.1.2 **Threat Modeling:** A formal threat modeling exercise is mandatory for all new applications, major new features, and significant architectural changes to existing systems. The exercise shall utilize a recognized methodology (e.g., STRIDE, PASTA) and be documented in a standardized threat model report. This report must identify assets, trust boundaries, potential threats, and corresponding security controls. The report requires approval from the development lead and a designated security representative (e.g., Application Security team member or Security Champion) before development commences.

5.1.3 **Secure Architecture Review:** All high-level and detailed application architectures must be reviewed for security implications. This review must validate the use of secure design principles (e.g., least privilege, defense in depth, fail securely). The use of deprecated, weak, or insecure cryptographic protocols and algorithms (e.g., SSLv3, TLS 1.0/1.1, RC4, MD5, SHA-1) is expressly forbidden for any new development.

5.1.4 **Data Protection by Design:** Data classification must be performed for all data elements processed by the application. Handling, storage, transmission, and disposal requirements for sensitive data (e.g., Personally Identifiable Information (PII), financial data, intellectual property, customer security data) must be explicitly defined and designed into the system. Encryption standards for data at rest and in transit must be specified.

### 5.2 Implementation Phase

This phase governs the actual writing of code and emphasizes secure coding practices to prevent the introduction of vulnerabilities.

5.2.1 **Secure Coding Standards:** All developers shall adhere to the Intellfence Secure Coding Standards, which are based on industry-recognized guidelines (e.g., OWASP Application Security Verification Standard (ASVS), CWE/SANS Top 25) and explicitly prohibit common vulnerability classes such as those listed in the OWASP Top Ten.

5.2.2 **Peer Code Review:** All code commits must undergo a mandatory peer review process with a specific focus on security logic, in addition to functional correctness. Reviewers must be different from the author. Security-focused checklists shall be used to guide reviews. All review comments, approvals, and discussions must be documented within the version control system (e.g., Git) to provide an audit trail.

5.2.3 **Software Composition Analysis (SCA):** The use of open-source and third-party libraries is governed by a formal SCA process. An SCA tool must be integrated into the build pipeline to continuously scan for and inventory components. A Software Bill of Materials (SBOM) must be generated for each release. Components with known critical or high-severity vulnerabilities, as defined by the Common Vulnerability Scoring System (CVSS), shall not be approved for use in new development. Existing vulnerable dependencies must be prioritized for remediation.

5.2.4 **Secrets Management:** Hard-coding secrets of any kind (e.g., passwords, API keys, private keys, database connection strings) within source code, configuration files, or container images is strictly prohibited. All secrets must be managed through a dedicated, approved enterprise secrets management solution (e.g., HashiCorp Vault, AWS Secrets Manager, Azure Key Vault). Application code must retrieve secrets dynamically at runtime from the sanctioned secrets store.

### 5.3 Verification and Testing Phase

This phase ensures that security requirements have been correctly implemented and that no vulnerabilities have been introduced.

5.3.1 **Static Application Security Testing (SAST):** SAST shall be performed automatically on all application source code for every build or pull request. The SAST tool must be configured with rules aligned to the Secure Coding Standards. Findings of critical or high severity must be remediated prior to the merge of code into the main branch. Findings of medium or lower severity must be documented in a risk-accepted backlog with planned remediation dates.

5.3.2 **Dynamic/Interactive Application Security Testing (DAST/IAST):** DAST or IAST shall be performed against all staging or pre-production application builds. These tests simulate attacker behavior against a running application. Critical and high-severity vulnerabilities discovered must be remediated before a release candidate is approved for production deployment.

5.3.3 **Software Composition Analysis (SCA) Verification:** SCA scans shall be run as a gating step in the CI/CD pipeline. No application build may be promoted to a production-ready artifact if it contains unresolved critical or high-severity vulnerabilities in its direct dependencies. Exceptions require a formal risk assessment and CISO approval.

5.3.4 **Penetration Testing:** For all internet-facing applications and applications handling highly sensitive data (as defined by data classification), an independent, qualified third-party penetration test must be conducted at least annually, or following any major release or significant architectural change. Findings from penetration tests are treated as highest priority for remediation.

### 5.4 Release and Deployment Phase

This phase controls the secure and auditable transition of software from development to production.

5.4.1 **Security Approval Gate:** A formal security approval gate is mandatory for all production deployments. This gate requires explicit sign-off from the Application Security team or a designated Security Champion, confirming that all security testing requirements (SAST, DAST/IAST, SCA) have been met and that critical/high findings are resolved or have an approved risk exception.

5.4.2 **Automated and Auditable Deployment:** Deployment to production environments must be performed exclusively through automated, version-controlled CI/CD pipelines. Manual deployments, direct access to production servers by developers, or "hotfixes" applied directly in production are strictly prohibited. Break-glass procedures for emergency access must be documented, require dual approval, and are subject to immediate post-incident review and audit.

5.4.3 **Infrastructure Security:** All application infrastructure (e.g., containers, virtual machines, serverless functions, Kubernetes clusters) must be provisioned from hardened, security-approved templates or images (e.g., CIS-hardened images). Infrastructure-as-Code (IaC) must itself be scanned for security misconfigurations prior to deployment.

### 5.5 Operations and Maintenance Phase

Security is an ongoing responsibility that continues after deployment.

5.5.1 **Continuous Security Monitoring:** A continuous monitoring program for applications in production shall be implemented. This includes, but is not limited to, monitoring for anomalous behavior (via SIEM), unauthorized access attempts, application performance anomalies indicative of an attack, and alerts from vulnerability intelligence feeds related to the application''s technology stack.

5.5.2 **Vulnerability Management:** A formal vulnerability management process governs the triage, prioritization, and remediation of security vulnerabilities discovered in production applications. This includes vulnerabilities found via monitoring, bug bounty programs, customer reports, or new CVEs affecting deployed components. Critical vulnerabilities must be remediated or mitigated within 7 calendar days of notification. High-severity vulnerabilities must be addressed within 30 calendar days.

5.5.3 **Change Management:** All changes to production applications, including security patches, dependency updates, and configuration modifications, must follow the formal change management process. Changes must be tested in a non-production environment that closely mirrors production before deployment. Rollback plans must be documented for all significant changes.

### 5.6 Decommissioning Phase

Secure disposal of applications and data is as important as secure creation.

5.6.1 **Decommissioning Plan:** A formal decommissioning plan must be created and approved for any application or service being retired. The plan must detail the steps for the secure archival or destruction of all associated data, credentials, and cryptographic keys, in accordance with Intellfence''s Data Retention and Destruction Policy.

5.6.2 **Secure Data Destruction:** All persistent data storage (e.g., databases, file stores, object storage, backups, log archives) associated with the decommissioned application shall be securely wiped using industry-standard sanitization methods (e.g., NIST 800-88) or physically destroyed. Confirmation of destruction must be documented.

## 6.0 Compliance and Audit

All software development activities must comply with relevant laws, regulations, industry standards, and contractual obligations applicable to Intellfence and its clients. This includes, but is not limited to, data protection regulations (GDPR, CCPA/CPRA), cybersecurity frameworks (NIST CSF), and audit standards (ISO 27001, SOC 2 Type II, PCI DSS where applicable). The processes, artifacts, and audit logs generated by this Secure SDLC policy (e.g., threat models, code review logs, SAST/DAST reports, SBOMs, approval gates) constitute the primary evidence of due care and compliance. The Information Security team will facilitate internal and external audits by providing this evidence.

## 7.0 Roles and Responsibilities

**Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):** Holds ultimate accountability for this policy, its enforcement, its periodic review, and its alignment with the corporate risk appetite. The CISO is the final authority for policy exceptions.

**Head of Software Development/Engineering:** Bears operational responsibility for implementing and enforcing the Secure SDLC processes within all development teams. This includes ensuring adequate resource allocation, training, and tooling for security activities.

**Application Security Team:** Responsible for defining and maintaining secure coding standards, managing and tuning security testing tools (SAST, DAST, SCA), conducting in-depth security reviews and threat modeling sessions, providing expert guidance to development teams, and operating the security approval gate.

**Development Team Leads/Managers:** Responsible for the day-to-day execution of this policy within their teams. They must facilitate threat modeling sessions, enforce peer review and secure coding practices, and ensure the timely remediation of security findings in their product backlog.

**Software Developers:** Individually responsible for writing secure code in accordance with company standards, completing mandatory security training, actively participating in peer code reviews, and promptly remediating security vulnerabilities identified in their code.

**Quality Assurance (QA) / Test Engineers:** Responsible for integrating security test cases (e.g., negative testing, fuzzing) into QA processes and validating the functional remediation of security defects.

**DevOps / Platform Engineers:** Responsible for implementing secure CI/CD pipelines, integrating security tooling, managing the secrets management infrastructure, and ensuring the security of deployment environments and infrastructure-as-code.

## 8.0 Policy Enforcement and Exceptions

Compliance with this policy is mandatory. Any personnel, including contractors and third-party vendors, found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contractual agreement. The CISO and the Application Security team are explicitly authorized to block the deployment of any application or feature that does not satisfy the security gates and requirements defined herein.

Exceptions to this policy are permitted only under extraordinary circumstances and require a formal, documented risk assessment. The risk assessment must detail the business justification, the specific controls being bypassed, compensating controls, and a mitigation timeline. All exceptions require written approval from the CISO or their designated delegate and must be reviewed at least quarterly.

## 9.0 Review and Revision

This policy shall be reviewed by the Information Security Governance Committee at least annually. Additional reviews shall be triggered by significant security incidents, major changes in the threat landscape, adoption of new technology stacks, or changes in relevant laws and regulations. Proposed revisions must be formally documented, reviewed for risk and operational impact, and approved by the CISO. The latest approved version of this policy shall be made accessible to all personnel and third parties to whom it applies through the company''s official policy repository.', 'draft', 1, 'emmanuel', '2026-02-20 10:03:02.984066', '2026-02-20 10:03:02.984066', 'general', true, '"{}"', NULL, NULL, 'pending', NULL, NULL),
	(1622, 3, 34, NULL, 'Wireless Security Policy', '# Wireless Security Policy

## 1.0 Purpose

This Wireless Security Policy establishes the mandatory requirements for the secure deployment, configuration, management, and monitoring of all wireless networks and devices that access, transmit, or store Intellfence information assets. As a cybersecurity enterprise, Intellfence handles sensitive client data, proprietary threat intelligence, and internal communications that are attractive targets for adversaries. This policy is designed to protect the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of these assets from threats inherent in wireless communications, including eavesdropping, unauthorized access, data interception, and network-based attacks. It provides a formalized control framework that is integral to Intellfence''s overall Information Security Management System (ISMS) and risk management strategy.

## 2.0 Scope

This policy applies universally across all of Intellfence''s operations, encompassing its headquarters, satellite offices, research labs, and any temporary physical locations (e.g., conference venues). It governs all individuals, including full-time and part-time employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, interns, and any other third-party affiliates.

The policy covers all corporate-owned, leased, or managed wireless infrastructure equipment, such as wireless access points (APs), wireless controllers, bridges, and wireless management systems. It also applies to all endpoint devices, whether corporate-issued (laptops, smartphones, tablets, IoT devices) or personally owned (under a BYOD arrangement), that connect to Intellfence wireless networks or that create wireless connections (e.g., ad-hoc networks, mobile tethering) to access corporate resources.

This includes, but is not limited to, Wi-Fi networks (all 802.11 standards), Bluetooth connections used for business peripherals or data transfer, cellular data modems (e.g., Mi-Fi), and any future or emerging wireless technologies adopted for business purposes. The policy extends to both the enterprise production network and any guest or lab networks operated by Intellfence.

## 3.0 Definitions

**Access Point (AP) / Wireless Access Point (WAP):** A networking hardware device that allows Wi-Fi compliant devices to connect to a wired network. It serves as the central transmitter and receiver of wireless radio signals.

**Enterprise Wireless Network:** The centrally managed, authorized wireless network infrastructure deployed and maintained by the Intellfence Information Technology (IT) Department for general corporate use by authorized personnel. This network provides authenticated access to the corporate intranet and approved cloud resources.

**Guest Wireless Network:** A segregated, firewalled, and tightly controlled wireless network provisioned explicitly for visitors, vendors, contractors, or other non-employees. Access is restricted to the public internet only, with no direct pathway to internal corporate resources.

**Rogue Access Point:** Any wireless access point installed on the corporate network or within corporate facilities without the explicit written authorization of the Intellfence IT Department. This includes unauthorized physical hardware, as well as software-based "soft APs" created on endpoints.

**WPA3-Enterprise (Wi-Fi Protected Access 3 Enterprise):** The current mandatory security certification and protocol for securing enterprise-grade Wi-Fi networks, as defined by the Wi-Fi Alliance. It utilizes Simultaneous Authentication of Equals (SAE) to provide robust protection against offline dictionary attacks and mandates the use of 256-bit Galois/Counter Mode Protocol (GCMP-256) encryption. It integrates with 802.1X/EAP for strong, certificate-based user and device authentication.

**Wireless Intrusion Detection/Prevention System (WIDS/WIPS):** A dedicated security solution that monitors the radio frequency (RF) spectrum to detect and, in prevention mode, block rogue access points, misconfigured devices, client mis-associations, and known wireless attack patterns (e.g., evil twin, deauthentication attacks).

## 4.0 Policy Statement

Intellfence mandates that all wireless network access serving corporate functions shall be implemented and maintained with security as the paramount design principle. The use of wireless technologies within the corporate environment is a privilege granted under strict administrative and technical controls to mitigate the significant risks of unauthorized access, data interception, and network compromise inherent in radio-based communications.

All wireless communications involving Intellfence data, systems, or applications must be encrypted using strong, industry-vetted cryptographic standards. All connections must be authenticated based on the principle of least privilege, and all wireless activity must be subject to continuous monitoring and logging. The deployment of unauthorized wireless infrastructure, the circumvention of established security controls, or the connection of unauthorized devices to corporate networks is strictly prohibited and will be treated as a serious security violation.

## 5.0 Detailed Requirements

### 5.1 Authorization and Architecture

5.1.1 All wireless infrastructure, including access points, wireless controllers, management appliances, and associated software, must be approved, procured, and deployed solely by the Intellfence Information Technology (IT) Department. No other department or individual is permitted to install wireless networking equipment on company premises or on the corporate network.

5.1.2 A formal, up-to-date network architecture diagram detailing all wireless infrastructure must be maintained by the Network Team. This diagram must include physical and logical placement of all APs, controller locations, RF coverage maps, channel planning, and transmit power settings. This architecture document shall be reviewed and validated at least annually, or following any significant network change.

5.1.3 The implementation of personal wireless access points, wireless routers, wireless range extenders, or ad-hoc wireless networks within corporate facilities is expressly forbidden. This prohibition extends to the use of USB wireless adapters configured to create software-based access points on corporate endpoints.

5.1.4 All wireless infrastructure must be logically segmented from the core wired network using VLANs and firewall policies to contain potential breaches and enforce access control policies between network zones.

### 5.2 Encryption and Authentication Standards

5.2.1 All enterprise-grade wireless networks operated by Intellfence shall utilize WPA3-Enterprise as the mandatory security protocol. The use of AES-256-GCM encryption is non-negotiable for all data in transit. Transitional use of WPA2-Enterprise is permitted only for legacy devices that cannot support WPA3 and must be documented in a formal registry. Each legacy device must have an associated risk acceptance form and a defined migration or retirement plan signed by the system owner and the CISO.

5.2.2 The use of deprecated and insecure protocols, including Wired Equivalent Privacy (WEP), Wi-Fi Protected Access with Pre-Shared Key (WPA-Personal/WPA2-Personal), or open/unencrypted networks for any corporate purpose is strictly forbidden. Exceptions for specific lab or testing environments require CISO approval and must be physically and logically isolated from production networks.

5.2.3 Authentication for the primary enterprise network must leverage a centralized identity provider. A dedicated RADIUS server (or equivalent cloud service) integrated with the corporate directory (e.g., Microsoft Entra ID / Active Directory) must be used. The preferred authentication method is 802.1X/EAP-TLS using machine or user certificates, providing mutual authentication. EAP-PEAP with MSCHAPv2 is acceptable only if certificate deployment is not feasible, but requires strong password policies.

5.2.4 Guest network access shall be secured via a captive portal system. Access credentials must be unique, one-time-use, or time-limited (maximum validity of 24 hours for generated codes). If a pre-shared key (PSK) must be used for guest access, it shall be distinct from any other network key, changed at least quarterly, and disclosed only upon visitor registration at reception. Guest traffic must be routed through a dedicated internet breakout point with no routing back to the internal corporate network.

### 5.3 Device Configuration and Hardening

5.3.1 All authorized wireless access points and controllers shall be hardened before deployment. This includes changing all default administrative credentials (username and password), disabling unnecessary services (e.g., Telnet, HTTP management), and enabling management via encrypted protocols only (e.g., SSH, HTTPS). Firmware must be maintained at a vendor-supported version, with critical and high-severity security patches applied within 30 calendar days of their release by the vendor.

5.3.2 Service Set Identifiers (SSIDs) for corporate networks shall be generic and shall not contain information that could identify Intellfence, its specific business units, physical location, or the network''s function (e.g., "Corp-Secure," "Guest-Internet"). Broadcasting the SSID (beaconing) may be disabled for the enterprise network if client device management supports secure, pre-configured connections.

5.3.3 Wireless signal strength (transmit power) shall be carefully tuned to provide adequate coverage within the intended physical premises while minimizing propagation beyond controllable boundaries such as building walls. Site surveys shall be conducted to optimize coverage and reduce the risk of signal leakage into public areas.

### 5.4 Monitoring and Intrusion Detection

5.4.1 A dedicated Wireless Intrusion Detection and Prevention System (WIDS/WIPS) shall be deployed to provide continuous, 24/7 monitoring of the 2.4 GHz and 5 GHz (and 6 GHz if used) radio frequency spectrum. The system must be configured to automatically detect and alert on rogue access points, unauthorized clients, misconfigured devices, ad-hoc networks, and known attack patterns like deauthentication floods and evil twin APs.

5.4.2 Comprehensive logging is mandatory. All authentication successes and failures from the RADIUS server, all administrative access sessions to wireless infrastructure, and all alert events from the WIDS/WIPS must be forwarded in real-time to the centralized Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) system. These logs must be retained for a minimum of 90 days for operational purposes and one year for compliance and forensic investigations.

5.4.3 The Information Security Team, in coordination with the IT Department, shall conduct periodic wireless security assessments. This includes vulnerability scans of wireless infrastructure and authorized wireless penetration testing at least annually. Tests will include assessments for weak encryption, rogue AP discovery from outside the facility, and client susceptibility to attacks.

### 5.5 Endpoint and Client Security

5.5.1 All corporate-managed devices (laptops, mobile phones, tablets) must have their wireless interfaces configured via a Mobile Device Management (MDM) or Unified Endpoint Management (UEM) solution. Configuration profiles must enforce policies that automatically connect the device only to pre-approved, trusted Intellfence SSIDs and prevent connections to untrusted or ad-hoc networks.

5.5.2 Users are prohibited from configuring corporate devices to connect to untrusted public wireless networks (e.g., coffee shops, airports, hotels) for the purpose of accessing sensitive corporate data, including email, internal wikis, or client portals, unless a mandated, company-provided and configured Virtual Private Network (VPN) is used. The VPN must establish an encrypted tunnel before any corporate traffic is transmitted.

5.5.3 The use of personal devices (Bring Your Own Device - BYOD) to connect to the enterprise wireless network is permitted only under the formal Intellfence BYOD Policy. This requires enrollment in the MDM/UEM solution, which will enforce device health checks (e.g., OS version, screen lock), and will containerize corporate email, applications, and data separately from personal data. Access may be revoked automatically for non-compliant devices.

## 6.0 Compliance & Audit

This policy is designed to support and demonstrate compliance with relevant legal, regulatory, and contractual obligations pertinent to a cybersecurity firm operating in NA/EU regions. This includes data protection regulations such as the General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR) and the California Consumer Privacy Act (CCPA), where wireless controls protect the confidentiality of personal data.

The policy''s controls directly map to and satisfy key requirements of major security frameworks:
*   **ISO/IEC 27001:2022:** Primarily addresses Annex A controls A.8.9 (Wireless Access Control), A.8.15 (Logging), A.8.16 (Monitoring), and supports A.8.1, A.8.2, A.8.12, and A.8.31 (Separation of Development, Test, and Production Environments for lab networks).
*   **SOC 2:** Supports the Trust Services Criteria for Security (CC6.1, CC6.3, CC6.6, CC6.7), Availability, and Confidentiality through its defined logical access, monitoring, and change management controls.
*   **NIST Cybersecurity Framework:** Aligns with Protect (PR.AC-1, PR.AC-3, PR.DS-2) and Detect (DE.AE-1, DE.CM-4) functions.

Any requested deviation from this policy for business necessity requires the submission of a formal risk assessment and an Exception Request Form. Exceptions must be reviewed by the Information Security Team and require written approval from the Chief Information Security Officer (CISO). All exceptions are time-bound and subject to periodic review.

## 7.0 Roles and Responsibilities

**Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):** Bears ultimate responsibility for the approval, oversight, periodic review, and enforcement of this policy. The CISO is the final authority on policy interpretation and exception approvals.

**Information Technology (IT) Department / Network Team:** Responsible for the secure design, implementation, maintenance, and operational monitoring of all authorized wireless infrastructure. This includes managing the WIDS/WIPS, applying patches and configuration changes, maintaining architecture documentation, and providing first-line response to wireless-related incidents.

**Information Security Team:** Responsible for the development and periodic review of this policy, conducting wireless vulnerability assessments and penetration tests, analyzing escalated alerts from the WIDS/WIPS and SIEM, and leading the investigation of significant policy violations or wireless security incidents.

**All Users (Employees, Contractors, Third Parties):** Required to read, understand, and comply with all aspects of this policy. Users must use only authorized wireless networks for business activities, must not attempt to install unauthorized equipment, and must immediately report any suspected security incidents, lost/stolen devices, or sightings of unauthorized wireless hardware to the IT Help Desk or Information Security Team.

## 8.0 Enforcement and Violations

Compliance with this policy will be enforced through a combination of technical controls (MDM, WIDS/WIPS, NAC), automated monitoring (SIEM), and administrative procedures. Violations of this policy will be investigated and may result in disciplinary action commensurate with the severity of the violation.

Actions may include, but are not limited to, formal written warnings, revocation of network access privileges, mandatory security training, termination of employment, or termination of contractual agreements. Violations may also lead to civil or criminal liability, particularly if they result in a data breach or network compromise.

The IT and Information Security Departments are explicitly authorized to immediately disconnect, disable, or quarantine any unauthorized wireless device or network that is detected and poses an imminent security risk to Intellfence assets, without prior notice to the owner.

All personnel shall be required to formally acknowledge receipt and understanding of this policy during the onboarding process and re-acknowledge it annually as part of security awareness training.

## 9.0 Review and Revision

This Wireless Security Policy shall be reviewed by the Information Security Team at least annually. Reviews may be triggered more frequently in response to significant security incidents, material changes in the threat landscape, the introduction of new wireless technologies, or updates to relevant compliance standards (e.g., new ISO 27001 controls, NIST guidelines).

Proposed revisions must be documented with a change log and submitted to the CISO for final review and approval. Upon approval, the revised policy shall be communicated to all relevant stakeholders, and the previous version shall be archived. The latest approved version of this policy shall be made accessible to all personnel through the corporate intranet policy repository.', 'draft', 1, 'emmanuel', '2026-02-20 10:03:14.896796', '2026-02-20 10:03:14.896796', 'general', true, '"{}"', NULL, NULL, 'pending', NULL, NULL),
	(1624, 3, 33, NULL, 'Password Policy', '# Intellfence Password Policy

## 1.0 Purpose

This Password Policy establishes the mandatory technical and administrative controls for the creation, management, protection, and secure disposal of all authentication credentials at Intellfence. As a cybersecurity firm, our credibility and operational integrity are intrinsically linked to our security posture. The primary objective of this policy is to safeguard our information assets, intellectual property, and client data by ensuring that passwords and other secrets are cryptographically robust, kept confidential, and resistant to modern attack vectors. This directly protects the confidentiality, integrity, and availability (CIA triad) of corporate systems, development environments, threat intelligence platforms, and sensitive client information, thereby upholding our contractual obligations and industry reputation.

## 2.0 Scope

This policy applies universally to all Intellfence personnel, including full-time and part-time employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, interns, and authorized third-party agents who are granted access to any Intellfence information system, network, application, database, cloud service, API, or data repository. This encompasses all corporate-owned and Bring Your Own Device (BYOD) devices used for business purposes.

The policy governs all forms of authentication credentials, including but not limited to: user account passwords, privileged administrative passwords, service account credentials, application secrets, API keys, database connection strings, encryption key passphrases, and SSH keys. It applies irrespective of the system''s location, whether hosted on-premises, in public or private cloud infrastructure (e.g., AWS, Azure, GCP), or in a hybrid environment.

## 3.0 Definitions

**Password:** A confidential sequence of characters, known only to the user and the system, used as one factor to authenticate an identity and authorize access to information resources. It is a shared secret that must be protected.

**Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA):** An authentication method that requires the user to provide two or more distinct verification factors from the categories of knowledge (something you know, like a password), possession (something you have, like a hardware token or smartphone app), and inherence (something you are, like a biometric). MFA significantly reduces the risk of account compromise via credential theft.

**Privileged Account:** Any user account with elevated permissions beyond a standard user, used for system administration, security configuration, database management, or network operations. Examples include Domain Administrators, root accounts, AWS IAM users with administrative policies, and application service accounts with write/delete permissions.

**Service Account:** A non-human account used by an application, operating system, or automated process to interact with other systems, databases, or network resources. These accounts often have persistent credentials that require stringent management.

**Password Manager:** A software application designed to securely store, generate, and manage online credentials in an encrypted vault, protected by a single strong master password and MFA. Intellfence mandates the use of the corporate-approved enterprise password manager for storing business-related credentials.

**Password Hash:** A cryptographically scrambled, fixed-length representation of a password, generated via a one-way function. Storing password hashes (with a salt) instead of plaintext passwords is a fundamental security control. Approved algorithms include bcrypt, scrypt, Argon2id, and PBKDF2 with a sufficient work factor.

**Brute-Force Attack:** An automated attempt to discover a password by systematically trying every possible combination of characters within a given keyspace.

**Credential Stuffing:** An attack that uses automated tools to inject large volumes of username/password pairs (typically obtained from breaches on other sites) into login forms to fraudulently gain access to user accounts.

## 4.0 Policy Statement

Intellfence mandates that all authentication credentials shall be created, managed, and protected according to the stringent, risk-based requirements defined in this policy. The use of weak, default, shared, or known-compromised passwords is strictly prohibited and constitutes a serious security violation. All personnel are personally accountable for understanding and adhering to these controls. The technical implementation of these controls is the responsibility of the Information Security and IT Infrastructure teams. This policy is considered a foundational element of our security program and is essential for compliance with industry standards and client agreements.

## 5.0 Detailed Requirements & Controls

### 5.1 Password Composition and Complexity

All user-created passwords must be constructed to resist both online brute-force and offline cracking attempts. The following minimum complexity requirements are enforced globally:

*   **Minimum Length:** Standard user account passwords must be a minimum of 14 characters. Privileged administrative accounts (e.g., system admins, network engineers, cloud architects) must use passwords of at least 20 characters. Length is the primary determinant of password strength.
*   **Character Diversity:** Passwords must include characters from at least three of the following four categories: Uppercase letters (A-Z), Lowercase letters (a-z), Numerals (0-9), and Special characters (e.g., ! @ # $ % ^ & * ( ) - _ = + [ ] { } | ; : , . < > / ? ` ~). The use of spaces is permitted and encouraged to create passphrases.
*   **Prohibited Elements:** Passwords must not contain:
    *   The user''s account name, full name, or a significant portion thereof.
    *   Common dictionary words in any language, used in isolation.
    *   Simple, predictable sequences (e.g., "12345678", "qwertyuiop", "abcdefg").
    *   Repetitive or sequential characters (e.g., "aaaaaaa", "1234abcd").
    *   Company names, product names, or publicly known internal project codenames.
    *   Seasonally or contextually predictable passwords (e.g., "Summer2024!").
*   **Passphrase Encouragement:** Intellfence strongly encourages the use of **random-word passphrases** (e.g., "correct-horse-battery-staple-42!") as they are often longer, more memorable, and cryptographically strong.

### 5.2 Password Lifecycle Management

Credentials must be managed throughout their entire lifecycle to mitigate the risk of long-term exposure and compromise.

*   **Initial Provisioning & First Use:** All initial passwords for new accounts shall be system-generated, unique, and compliant with this policy''s complexity rules. They must be transmitted to the user via a secure, out-of-band method (e.g., temporary link via a separate email, secure messaging platform, or in-person delivery). The user is forced to change this temporary password immediately upon first successful login.
*   **Password Expiration & Rotation:**
    *   **Standard User Accounts:** Passwords must be changed at least every 90 days. Systems should provide warnings starting 14 days prior to expiration.
    *   **Privileged User Accounts:** Passwords must be changed at least every 60 days due to the higher risk associated with these credentials.
    *   **Service & Application Accounts:** Credentials must be rotated at least every 180 days using an automated secrets management tool. Additionally, they must be rotated immediately upon the departure of any personnel who had knowledge of the credential, or upon suspicion of compromise.
*   **Password History:** All systems must enforce a password history, preventing the reuse of a user''s previous 10 passwords. This prevents cyclical rotation of a small set of passwords.
*   **Password Change Process:** Users must create a new compliant password during change events. Reusing any part of the previous password with minor alterations (e.g., incrementing a number) is prohibited.

### 5.3 Password Protection and Storage

The confidentiality of passwords is paramount. The following controls ensure they are protected at rest and in transit.

*   **Cryptographic Storage:** Passwords shall **never** be stored in plaintext, reversible encryption, or weakly hashed formats (e.g., MD5, SHA1). All passwords at rest must be hashed using a strong, memory-hard, and industry-approved algorithm such as **bcrypt, scrypt, or Argon2id**. If PBKDF2 is used, the iteration count must be ≥ 310,000. A cryptographically random salt unique to each credential must be used.
*   **Secure Transmission:** Passwords shall only be transmitted over encrypted channels. All authentication interfaces (web, API, VPN) must use TLS 1.3 or, at a minimum, TLS 1.2 with strong cipher suites. Sending passwords via unencrypted email, instant messaging, or other clear-text protocols is strictly forbidden.
*   **Secure Storage by Users:** Memorization of strong, unique passwords for each system is impractical. Therefore:
    *   The use of the corporate-approved enterprise password manager is **mandatory** for storing all business-related credentials.
    *   Writing down passwords on sticky notes, notebooks, or whiteboards is prohibited.
    *   Storing passwords in unencrypted files (e.g., Word documents, Excel spreadsheets, text files on the desktop) is prohibited.
    *   Using a browser''s built-in password manager for business accounts is prohibited unless it is explicitly integrated with and backed by the corporate-approved password manager.
*   **Sharing Prohibition:** Passwords are non-transferable and must not be shared with anyone, including colleagues, assistants, or family members. The only exception is for emergency break-glass accounts, which are governed by a separate, stricter procedure and must be logged and audited.

### 5.4 Authentication and Access Controls

Technical systems must implement robust controls to verify identity and limit session exposure.

*   **Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA):** MFA is **mandatory** for:
    *   All remote network access (VPN, RDP gateways, Citrix).
    *   Access to all cloud service administration consoles (AWS Management Console, Azure Portal, GCP Console).
    *   All users with privileged access rights.
    *   Access to any system containing sensitive client data, source code repositories, or financial information.
    *   The preferred MFA factors are time-based one-time passwords (TOTP) via an authenticator app (e.g., Duo, Microsoft Authenticator) or FIDO2/WebAuthn security keys. SMS-based codes are discouraged for high-risk accounts due to SIM-swapping risks.
*   **Account Lockout:** To mitigate online brute-force attacks, user accounts shall be automatically locked after **5 consecutive failed authentication attempts** within a 15-minute window. The lockout duration shall be a minimum of **30 minutes**, after which the account auto-unlocks, or requires manual intervention by the IT Help Desk. All lockout events must generate an alert to the Security Operations Center (SOC).
*   **Session Management:**
    *   Interactive user sessions (desktop, web applications) shall automatically terminate after a maximum of **15 minutes** of inactivity. For highly sensitive systems, a 5-minute timeout may be enforced.
    *   Session tokens must be cryptographically secure, randomly generated, and invalidated both on the server and client upon explicit user logout.
    *   Re-authentication is required for re-establishing a session after timeout and before performing highly sensitive actions (e.g., changing account email, modifying financial settings).

### 5.5 Administrative and Technical Enforcement

Policy effectiveness depends on consistent technical enforcement and monitoring.

*   **Centralized Identity Management:** Where technically and architecturally feasible, all user authentication shall be federated through a centralized Identity and Access Management (IAM) system, such as Microsoft Entra ID (Azure AD) or Okta. This provides a single point for policy enforcement, provisioning, and de-provisioning.
*   **Automated Compliance Checking:** Automated tools shall be deployed to periodically scan infrastructure (including cloud IAM, directories, and configuration files) for the use of default, weak, or hard-coded passwords, with a focus on service accounts and DevOps pipelines. Findings must be tracked to remediation.
*   **Secrets Management:** All non-human access (applications, scripts, CI/CD pipelines) must use a dedicated secrets management solution (e.g., HashiCorp Vault, AWS Secrets Manager, Azure Key Vault) to automate the generation, rotation, and auditing of credentials and API keys.
*   **Logging and Monitoring:** All authentication-related events must be logged centrally. Logs must include, at a minimum: timestamp, source IP address, user identifier, event type (success/failure/lockout/password change), and target system/service. These logs must be retained for a minimum of **90 days** for operational purposes and **at least one year** for compliance and forensic investigation. Anomalous authentication patterns must trigger alerts for SOC investigation.

## 6.0 Compliance & Audit Alignment

This policy is designed to provide the control framework necessary to demonstrate compliance with major industry standards and regulations relevant to a cybersecurity services provider.

*   **ISO/IEC 27001:2022:** Directly supports Annex A controls A.5.15 (Identity management), A.5.16 (Identity, authentication & access), A.5.17 (Authentication information), A.8.2 (Privileged access rights), and A.8.9 (Logging).
*   **SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria:** Provides the basis for meeting the Security criterion CC6.1 (Logical and physical access controls) and the Confidentiality criterion, specifically around protecting information designated as confidential.
*   **NIST Cybersecurity Framework (CSF):** Aligns with the Protect (PR.AC) function, specifically PR.AC-1 (Identities and credentials are issued, managed, verified, revoked, and audited) and PR.AC-7 (Users, devices, and other assets are authenticated).
*   **General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR):** Supports compliance with Article 32 (Security of processing), which requires implementing appropriate technical measures, including the ability to ensure the ongoing confidentiality, integrity, and availability of processing systems.
*   **Cybersecurity Maturity Model Certification (CMMC):** Maps to practices within Level 2 (e.g., IA.2.076 - Authenticate to systems before accessing them) and Level 3 (e.g., IA.3.084 - Employ replay-resistant authentication mechanisms).

Non-compliance with this policy may result in significant audit findings, failure to achieve or maintain certifications, breach of client contracts, regulatory penalties, and increased legal liability for Intellfence.

## 7.0 Roles and Responsibilities

*   **Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):** Holds ultimate authority and accountability for the approval, implementation, communication, and enforcement of this policy. The CISO is responsible for its periodic review and updates.
*   **Information Security Team:** Responsible for the development, maintenance, and monitoring of this policy. This includes configuring IAM and SSO systems, reviewing authentication logs and alerts, conducting periodic access reviews, and investigating potential violations.
*   **IT Infrastructure & DevOps Teams:** Responsible for implementing the technical controls specified in Section 5 across all on-premises systems, cloud environments, and applications under their management. This includes configuring password policies in Active Directory, enforcing MFA, managing secrets vaults, and ensuring secure coding practices that do not hardcode credentials.
*   **All Personnel:** Every individual within the scope of this policy is responsible for reading, understanding, and adhering to all its requirements. This includes creating strong, unique passwords, using the corporate password manager, enabling MFA, and immediately reporting any suspected password compromise or policy violation to the Information Security Team.
*   **Human Resources (HR):** Responsible for integrating policy acknowledgment into the employee onboarding and offboarding processes. HR works with IT to ensure accounts are provisioned and de-provisioned in a timely manner in accordance with this policy.

## 8.0 Policy Violations & Enforcement

Adherence to this policy is a condition of employment and continued access to Intellfence systems. Any personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, the severity of which will be commensurate with the nature and impact of the violation. Actions may include, but are not limited to: formal written warning, mandatory security training, suspension of system access, termination of employment or contractual agreement, and pursuit of legal action in cases involving gross negligence or malicious intent.

The Information Security Team is explicitly authorized to immediately disable or lock any user account found to be in violation of this policy (e.g., using a compromised password, sharing credentials) to mitigate ongoing risk to the organization. All suspected and confirmed violations shall be documented, investigated, and reported in accordance with the Intellfence Incident Response Policy.

## 9.0 Policy Review and Maintenance

This Password Policy is a living document that must evolve with the threat landscape and technological change. It shall be formally reviewed by the Information Security Team, in consultation with IT and legal stakeholders, **at least annually**. An ad-hoc review must also be triggered following any significant security incident involving credentials, a major change in the regulatory or compliance landscape, or a fundamental shift in authentication technology.

All proposed revisions must be documented, assessed for risk and impact, and submitted to the CISO for formal approval. Upon approval, the revised policy shall be communicated to all personnel, and the previous version shall be archived. The latest approved version of this policy shall be made permanently accessible to all personnel through the corporate intranet policy repository.', 'draft', 1, 'emmanuel', '2026-02-20 10:04:25.757214', '2026-02-20 10:04:25.757214', 'general', true, '"{}"', NULL, NULL, 'pending', NULL, NULL),
	(1623, 3, 34, NULL, 'Wireless Security Policy', '# Wireless Security Policy

## 1.0 Purpose

This Wireless Security Policy establishes the mandatory requirements for the secure deployment, configuration, management, and monitoring of all wireless networks and devices that access, transmit, or store Intellfence information assets. As a cybersecurity enterprise, Intellfence handles sensitive client data, proprietary threat intelligence, and internal communications that are attractive targets for adversaries. This policy is designed to protect the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of these assets from threats inherent in wireless communications, including eavesdropping, unauthorized access, data interception, and network-based attacks. It forms a critical component of Intellfence''s overall Information Security Management System (ISMS) and risk management framework, ensuring that wireless access does not become a vector for compromise of our own security services or client environments.

## 2.0 Scope

This policy applies universally across all of Intellfence''s operations, encompassing its headquarters, remote offices, research labs, and any subsidiary or business unit. It governs all individuals, including full-time and part-time employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, interns, and any other third parties granted access to Intellfence facilities or information systems.

The policy covers all forms of wireless communication used for business purposes, including but not limited to: Wi-Fi networks (802.11a/b/g/n/ac/ax/be), Bluetooth and Bluetooth Low Energy (BLE) connections, cellular data modems (e.g., LTE/5G hotspots), Near Field Communication (NFC), and any subsequent emerging wireless technologies. It applies to all corporate-owned, leased, or managed wireless infrastructure hardware (e.g., access points, wireless controllers, bridges, sensors) and all endpoint devices—whether corporate-issued or personal (under a BYOD arrangement)—that connect to Intellfence wireless networks or that create wireless connections (e.g., ad-hoc networks, smartphone tethering) to access corporate resources, data, or applications.

## 3.0 Definitions

**Access Point (AP) / Wireless Access Point (WAP):** A networking hardware device that allows Wi-Fi compliant client devices to connect to a wired network and to each other.

**Enterprise Wireless Network:** The centrally managed, authorized, and secure wireless network infrastructure deployed and maintained by the Intellfence Information Technology (IT) Department for use by authorized personnel to access corporate resources.

**Guest Wireless Network:** A strictly segregated and controlled wireless network provisioned for visitors, clients, contractors, or other non-employees. This network provides controlled internet access while enforcing stringent isolation from the internal corporate network and data resources.

**Rogue Access Point:** Any wireless access point or router installed on the corporate network or within corporate facilities without the explicit written authorization of the Intellfence IT Department. This includes unauthorized devices connected to the wired network and unauthorized personal hotspots operating within the facility.

**WPA3-Enterprise (Wi-Fi Protected Access 3 Enterprise):** The current mandatory security certification and protocol for securing enterprise-grade Wi-Fi networks. It provides 256-bit Galois/Counter Mode Protocol (GCMP-256) encryption, Simultaneous Authentication of Equals (SAE) to protect against offline dictionary attacks, and mandates the use of 802.1X/EAP for robust, certificate-based authentication.

**Wireless Intrusion Detection/Prevention System (WIDS/WIPS):** A dedicated security solution that monitors the radio frequency (RF) spectrum to detect and respond to unauthorized wireless devices, misconfigurations, policy violations, and malicious attack patterns such as evil twin attacks, deauthentication floods, and honeypot APs.

## 4.0 Policy Statement

Intellfence mandates that all wireless network access serving corporate functions shall be implemented and maintained with security as the paramount design principle, reflecting our position as a trusted cybersecurity provider. The use of wireless technologies within Intellfence is a privilege granted under strict administrative and technical controls designed to mitigate risks of unauthorized access, data interception, espionage, and network compromise. All wireless communications involving Intellfence data, systems, or client information must be encrypted using strong, industry-vetted cryptographic standards, authenticated through robust, identity-centric mechanisms, and continuously monitored for anomalous activity. The deployment of unauthorized wireless infrastructure, the circumvention of established security controls, or the negligent use of wireless connectivity is strictly prohibited and will be treated as a serious security violation.

## 5.0 Policy Requirements

### 5.1 Authorization and Architecture

5.1.1 All wireless infrastructure, including access points, wireless controllers, management appliances, and wireless bridges, must be approved, procured, standardized, and deployed solely by the Intellfence Information Technology (IT) Department. No other department or individual is permitted to install wireless networking equipment.

5.1.2 A formal, up-to-date network architecture diagram detailing all wireless infrastructure must be maintained by the Network Team. This diagram must include physical and logical placement of all APs, controller locations, RF coverage maps, channel planning, transmit power settings, and VLAN segmentation schemas. This architecture document shall be reviewed and validated at least annually, or after any significant network change.

5.1.3 The implementation of personal wireless access points, wireless routers, ad-hoc wireless networks, or wireless-enabled network switches within corporate facilities is expressly forbidden. This prohibition extends to the use of USB wireless adapters to create software-based access points on corporate workstations.

5.1.4 All wireless infrastructure must be logically segmented from the core corporate network using dedicated Virtual Local Area Networks (VLANs) with firewall policies enforcing the principle of least privilege for traffic traversing between segments.

### 5.2 Encryption and Authentication Standards

5.2.1 All enterprise wireless networks providing access to corporate resources shall utilize WPA3-Enterprise with AES-256-GCM encryption as the mandatory standard. Transitional use of WPA2-Enterprise is permitted only for legacy devices that lack WPA3 support and must be documented in a formal risk register with a defined migration or retirement plan approved by the CISO.

5.2.2 The use of deprecated and insecure protocols, including Wired Equivalent Privacy (WEP), WPA-Personal (Pre-Shared Key), or open/unencrypted networks for any corporate purpose, including testing or lab environments, is strictly forbidden. Lab environments must be physically and logically air-gapped or employ equivalently strong, dedicated security controls.

5.2.3 Authentication for the primary enterprise network must leverage a centralized identity provider. This will be implemented via a RADIUS server (or equivalent) tightly integrated with the corporate directory service (e.g., Microsoft Active Directory, Azure Active Directory) using the 802.1X framework. The preferred EAP method is EAP-TLS using client certificates for mutual authentication. EAP-PEAP (MSCHAPv2) may be used where certificate deployment is not feasible, but must be configured to validate server certificates to prevent man-in-the-middle attacks.

5.2.4 Guest network access shall be secured via a captive portal system. Access shall be granted via unique, single-use or time-limited credentials generated by the portal, or via a sponsor-provided passcode. If a Pre-Shared Key (PSK) must be used for guest access, it must be distinct from any other network and rotated at least quarterly, or immediately following a high-visibility event. Guest traffic must be routed through a dedicated internet breakout point with no routing path to the internal corporate network.

### 5.3 Device Configuration and Hardening

5.3.1 All authorized wireless access points and controllers shall be hardened according to a standardized baseline configuration. This includes changing all default administrative credentials, disabling unnecessary services (e.g., Telnet, HTTP management, SNMP with default communities), and disabling unused physical ports. Administrative access must be via encrypted protocols (SSH, HTTPS) from designated management networks only.

5.3.2 Firmware for all wireless infrastructure must be kept up-to-date. Critical security patches released by the vendor must be applied within 30 calendar days of release. A formal patch management process, including pre-deployment testing in a lab environment, must be followed.

5.3.3 Service Set Identifiers (SSIDs) shall be generic and shall not contain information that could identify Intellfence, its specific business units, physical location, or the function of the network (e.g., "Intellfence_Corp," "BuildingA_Secure"). Standard SSIDs such as "Corporate" and "Guest" shall be used.

5.3.4 Wireless signal strength (transmit power) for all APs shall be tuned to provide adequate coverage within the intended physical premises while minimizing propagation into publicly accessible areas such as parking lots, streets, and adjacent buildings. Site surveys shall be conducted to validate coverage boundaries.

### 5.4 Monitoring and Intrusion Detection

5.4.1 A dedicated Wireless Intrusion Detection and Prevention System (WIDS/WIPS) shall be deployed to provide continuous, 24/7 monitoring of the 2.4 GHz and 5 GHz (and 6 GHz, if used) radio frequency spectrum. The system must be capable of detecting and alerting on rogue access points, misconfigured clients, ad-hoc networks, evil twin attacks, denial-of-service attempts, and other malicious activity.

5.4.2 All authentication and authorization events (successes and failures) on the enterprise wireless network, as well as all administrative access and configuration changes to the wireless infrastructure, must be logged. These logs must be forwarded in real-time to the centralized Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) system with a minimum immutable retention period of 90 days to support incident investigation and compliance audits.

5.4.3 The Information Security Team, in coordination with the IT Department, shall conduct periodic wireless security assessments. This includes vulnerability scans of the wireless infrastructure and authorized penetration tests that simulate attacker techniques against the wireless environment. These assessments must be performed at least annually, or after any major architectural change.

### 5.5 Endpoint and Client Security

5.5.1 All corporate-managed devices (laptops, smartphones, tablets) must have their wireless interfaces configured and controlled via a Mobile Device Management (MDM) or Unified Endpoint Management (UEM) solution. Policies must enforce automatic connection only to approved Intellfence SSIDs and prevent connections to remembered or untrusted networks while on corporate premises.

5.5.2 Users are prohibited from configuring corporate devices to connect to untrusted or public wireless networks (e.g., coffee shops, airports, hotels) for the purpose of accessing sensitive corporate data, source code, or client systems unless a mandated, company-provided Virtual Private Network (VPN) with always-on capability and strong encryption (e.g., IPsec, WireGuard) is utilized for the entire session.

5.5.3 The use of personal devices (Bring Your Own Device - BYOD) to connect to the enterprise wireless network is permitted only through the formal BYOD program managed by the IT Department. Enrollment is mandatory and requires installation of a management profile that enforces device health checks (e.g., OS version, screen lock), enables remote wipe capability, and containerizes corporate email, data, and applications separate from personal content.

## 6.0 Compliance and Audit

This policy is designed to support and demonstrate compliance with relevant legal, regulatory, and contractual obligations, including but not limited to the General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR), the California Consumer Privacy Act (CCPA), and client-specific security requirements.

The controls specified herein directly address and provide evidence for the following standards:
*   **ISO/IEC 27001:2022 Annex A:** A.8.1 (Asset Management), A.8.2 (Information Classification), A.8.9 (Information Access Restriction), A.8.10 (Information Transfer), A.8.12 (Data Leakage Prevention), A.8.15 (Logging), A.8.16 (Monitoring), and A.8.23 (Network Security).
*   **SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria:** CC6.1 (Logical Access), CC6.3 (Security Incident Management), CC6.6 (Logical Access Security), CC6.7 (Network Security), and the Confidentiality criterion.
*   **NIST Cybersecurity Framework:** PR.AC-1, PR.AC-3, PR.DS-2, DE.CM-1, DE.CM-3.

Any deviation from this policy for legitimate business or technical necessity requires a formal risk assessment, documented justification, and a written exception approved by the Chief Information Security Officer (CISO). All exceptions shall have a defined expiration date and be reviewed periodically.

## 7.0 Roles and Responsibilities

**Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):** Bears ultimate responsibility for the approval, dissemination, oversight, and enforcement of this policy. The CISO is responsible for granting policy exceptions and ensuring the policy aligns with the overall risk appetite of Intellfence.

**Information Technology (IT) Department / Network Team:** Responsible for the secure design, implementation, maintenance, patching, and operational monitoring of all authorized wireless infrastructure. This team manages the WIDS/WIPS, applies configuration standards, responds to technical incidents, and maintains the wireless architecture documentation.

**Information Security Team:** Responsible for the periodic review and update of this policy, conducting wireless security assessments and penetration tests, analyzing alerts from the WIDS/WIPS and SIEM, investigating potential policy violations, and providing security guidance for wireless projects.

**All Users (Employees, Contractors, Third Parties):** Required to read, understand, and comply with all aspects of this policy. Users must utilize only authorized wireless networks for business activities, secure their devices as mandated, and immediately report any suspected security incidents, lost/stolen devices, or sightings of unauthorized wireless equipment to the IT Help Desk or Information Security Team.

## 8.0 Enforcement and Violations

Compliance with this policy will be enforced through a combination of technical controls (MDM, WIDS/WIPS, NAC), automated monitoring (SIEM), and administrative procedures. Violations of this policy may result in disciplinary action commensurate with the severity of the infraction, up to and including termination of employment or contractual agreement. Violations may also lead to civil or criminal liability, particularly if they result in a data breach or compromise of client systems.

The IT and Information Security teams are explicitly authorized to immediately disconnect, disable, or contain any unauthorized wireless device or network that poses an active or suspected security risk to Intellfence assets, without prior notice. All users shall be required to formally acknowledge receipt and understanding of this policy upon hire, during annual security awareness training, and upon any major policy revision.

## 9.0 Review and Revision

This Wireless Security Policy shall be reviewed by the Information Security Team, in consultation with the IT Department, at least annually. Reviews shall also be triggered by significant security incidents, material changes in the threat landscape, the introduction of new wireless technologies, or updates to relevant compliance standards (e.g., ISO 27001, NIST SP 800-153).

Proposed revisions must be documented, including a rationale for change, and submitted to the CISO for final approval. The latest approved version of this policy shall be made accessible to all personnel through the corporate intranet policy repository. A version history shall be maintained to track all changes.', 'draft', 1, 'emmanuel', '2026-02-20 10:04:05.559586', '2026-02-20 10:04:05.559586', 'general', true, '"{}"', NULL, NULL, 'pending', NULL, NULL),
	(1625, 3, 33, NULL, 'Password Policy', '# Intellfence Password Policy

## 1.0 Purpose

This Password Policy establishes the mandatory technical and administrative controls for the creation, management, protection, and secure disposal of all authentication credentials at Intellfence. As a cybersecurity enterprise, our credibility and operational integrity are intrinsically linked to the robustness of our internal security practices. The primary objective of this policy is to safeguard all information assets—including intellectual property, client data, threat intelligence, and critical systems—by ensuring that passwords and other secrets are cryptographically strong, kept confidential, and resistant to modern attack vectors. This directly protects the confidentiality, integrity, and availability (CIA triad) of corporate data and systems, forming a foundational defense layer against unauthorized access and credential-based attacks.

## 2.0 Scope

This policy applies universally and without exception to all individuals and entities that interact with Intellfence information systems. This includes, but is not limited to, full-time and part-time employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, interns, third-party vendors, and service providers. It governs all forms of authentication credentials used to access any Intellfence information system, network, application (SaaS, PaaS, IaaS), database, API, or data repository, irrespective of its deployment model (on-premises, cloud-hosted, or hybrid). This policy extends to user account passwords, privileged administrative passwords, service account credentials, application secrets, API keys, database connection strings, and encryption passphrases.

## 3.0 Definitions

**Password:** A confidential sequence of characters, known only to the user and the authentication system, used to verify identity and authorize access to information resources. It serves as a "something you know" authentication factor.

**Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA):** An authentication method that requires the presentation of two or more distinct verification factors from the following categories: knowledge (something you know, e.g., password), possession (something you have, e.g., hardware token, smartphone app), and inherence (something you are, e.g., biometrics).

**Privileged Account:** Any user account with elevated permissions beyond those of a standard user, including but not limited to system/network/database administrators, domain administrators, root accounts, and accounts with access to security tools or sensitive data repositories.

**Service Account:** A non-human account used by an operating system, application, or service to interact with other systems, databases, or network resources. These accounts often have persistent privileges and are a high-value target for attackers.

**Password Manager:** A software application, formally approved by the Information Security Team, designed to generate, store, and manage online credentials in a centrally encrypted vault, protected by a strong master password and MFA.

**Password Hash:** A cryptographically scrambled, fixed-length representation of a password generated via a one-way mathematical function. The hash is stored instead of the plaintext password, and during authentication, the input is hashed and compared to the stored hash.

**Brute-Force Attack:** An attack method where an adversary systematically submits a high volume of password guesses, often using automated tools, to discover a valid credential.

**Credential Stuffing:** An attack that leverages previously breached username/password pairs from other services, relying on password reuse across multiple sites.

## 4.0 Policy Statement

Intellfence mandates that all authentication credentials shall be created, managed, transmitted, stored, and disposed of according to the stringent, risk-based requirements defined in this policy. The use of weak, default, shared, reused, or known-compromised passwords is strictly prohibited and constitutes a serious security violation. All personnel are personally accountable for understanding, implementing, and adhering to these controls. The organization is committed to providing the necessary tools, such as enterprise password managers and MFA solutions, to facilitate secure compliance. Upholding this policy is a non-negotiable condition of system access and is critical to maintaining the advanced security posture expected of a leading cybersecurity firm.

## 5.0 Detailed Requirements & Controls

### 5.1 Password Composition and Complexity

All user-created passwords must be constructed to resist both automated brute-force and intelligent guessing attacks. The following minimum complexity requirements are enforced technically where possible and mandated administratively elsewhere.

*   **Minimum Length:** Passwords for standard user accounts must be a minimum of 14 characters. Passwords for all privileged administrative accounts, as defined in Section 3.0, must be a minimum of 20 characters. Length is the primary determinant of password strength against brute-force attacks.
*   **Character Diversity:** Passwords must include characters from at least three (3) of the following four character categories: Uppercase letters (A-Z), Lowercase letters (a-z), Numerals (0-9), and Special characters (e.g., ! @ # $ % ^ & * ( ) - _ = + [ ] { } | ; : , . < > / ? ` ~). The use of spaces is permitted and encouraged to create passphrases.
*   **Prohibited Elements:** Passwords must not contain:
    *   The user''s account name, full name, or any easily derivable variation.
    *   Common dictionary words in any language, used in isolation.
    *   Simple, predictable sequences (e.g., "12345678", "qwertyuiop", "abcdefgh").
    *   Repetitive or sequential characters (e.g., "aaaaaaa", "123456", "fedcba").
    *   Company names, product names, or internal project codenames.
    *   Any publicly available personal information (e.g., birthdates, family names, pet names).

### 5.2 Password Lifecycle Management

Credentials must be managed throughout their entire lifecycle to mitigate the risk of long-term exposure and reuse.

*   **Initial Provisioning:** All initial or reset passwords shall be system-generated, unique, and of sufficient complexity as per Section 5.1. These temporary credentials must be transmitted to the user via a secure, out-of-band method (e.g., a temporary link delivered via a separate communication channel). The user is forced to change this password immediately upon first successful login.
*   **Password Expiration & Rotation:**
    *   Standard User Accounts: Passwords must be changed at least every 90 days.
    *   Privileged Administrative Accounts: Passwords must be changed at least every 60 days.
    *   Service & Application Accounts: Credentials must be rotated at least every 180 days, using automated orchestration tools where possible. Additionally, they must be rotated immediately upon the departure of any personnel who had knowledge of the credential or upon suspicion of compromise.
*   **Password History:** Authentication systems shall be configured to enforce a password history of at least the previous 10 passwords to prevent cyclical reuse. Users cannot revert to a recently used password.

### 5.3 Password Protection and Storage

Protecting passwords from exposure is as critical as their initial strength.

*   **Cryptographic Storage:** Passwords shall never be stored, logged, or cached in plaintext. All passwords at rest within Intellfence systems must be hashed using a strong, memory-hard, and industry-approved cryptographic hashing algorithm designed for password protection. Approved algorithms include bcrypt, scrypt, Argon2id, or PBKDF2 with a work factor (iteration count) appropriate to resist modern hardware-assisted attacks. Salting is mandatory.
*   **Secure Transmission:** Passwords shall only be transmitted over encrypted channels. All authentication interfaces (web, API, etc.) must use TLS 1.3 or, at a minimum, TLS 1.2 with strong cipher suites. The transmission of passwords via unencrypted email, instant messaging, or other clear-text protocols is strictly forbidden.
*   **Secure User Storage – Approved Methods:** Memorization of complex passwords is impractical. Therefore, the use of the corporate-approved enterprise password manager is the mandated and primary method for storing and managing credentials. The master password for this vault must adhere to the requirements in Section 5.1 and be protected by MFA.
*   **Prohibited User Storage:** The following practices are explicitly forbidden:
    *   Writing passwords down on sticky notes, notebooks, or whiteboards.
    *   Storing passwords in unencrypted files (e.g., Word documents, Excel spreadsheets, text files).
    *   Using unapproved browser-based password managers or "save password" features.
    *   Sharing passwords via any means other than the secure sharing function within the approved password manager, and only when absolutely necessary for business operations with prior managerial approval.

### 5.4 Authentication and Access Controls

System-level controls must complement strong passwords to create a defense-in-depth strategy.

*   **Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA):** MFA is mandatory for all access scenarios, without exception. This includes:
    *   All remote network access (VPN, RDP gateways, Citrix).
    *   Access to all cloud service administration consoles (e.g., AWS Management Console, Azure Portal, GCP Console).
    *   All users with privileged access rights.
    *   Access to any system containing sensitive data as classified by the Data Classification Policy.
    *   The use of phishing-resistant MFA factors (e.g., FIDO2/WebAuthn security keys, certificate-based authentication) is strongly encouraged and required for the highest privilege tiers.
*   **Account Lockout:** To mitigate online brute-force attacks, user accounts shall be automatically locked after 5 consecutive failed authentication attempts within a 15-minute window. The lockout duration shall be a minimum of 15 minutes or require manual intervention by the IT Help Desk. All lockout events must be logged and generate an alert to the Security Operations Center (SOC) for investigation.
*   **Session Management:**
    *   Inactivity Timeout: All interactive user sessions (local, remote, web) shall automatically terminate after a maximum of 15 minutes of inactivity. For highly sensitive administrative sessions, a 10-minute timeout is enforced.
    *   Session Tokens: Must be cryptographically random, of sufficient length, and invalidated both on the client and server upon user logout, browser closure, or session timeout.
    *   Re-authentication: Re-authentication (including MFA) is required for re-establishing a session after timeout and before performing highly sensitive transactions (e.g., wire transfers, major configuration changes).

### 5.5 Administrative and Technical Enforcement

Policy effectiveness depends on consistent and automated enforcement.

*   **Centralized Identity Management:** All user authentication and authorization shall be managed through a centralized Identity and Access Management (IAM) system, such as Azure Active Directory or Okta, to ensure consistent policy application, streamline provisioning/deprovisioning, and provide a single audit trail.
*   **Automated Compliance Checking:** The Information Security Team shall deploy automated tools to periodically scan infrastructure (including cloud environments) for the existence of default, weak, or hard-coded passwords, with a particular focus on service accounts, IoT devices, and network equipment. Findings are tracked to remediation.
*   **Logging, Monitoring, and Auditing:** All authentication-related events are considered critical security events and must be logged. Logs must include, at a minimum: timestamp, source IP address, user identifier (UID), target system/service, event type (success, failure, password change, lockout), and any relevant error codes. These logs must be aggregated into the Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) system, retained for a minimum of 90 days (with 1 year recommended for privileged access), and protected from tampering. Regular audits of authentication logs are performed to detect anomalous patterns.

## 6.0 Compliance & Regulatory Alignment

This policy is designed to provide the control framework necessary to demonstrate compliance with major information security standards and regulations relevant to Intellfence''s operations in North America and the European Union.

*   **ISO/IEC 27001:2022:** Directly supports Annex A controls, including A.5.15 (Identity Management), A.5.16 (Identity, Authentication & Access), A.5.17 (Authentication Information), A.5.18 (Access Rights), and A.8.2 (Privileged Access Rights).
*   **SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria:** Provides the foundational controls for the Security criterion, specifically CC6.1 (Logical and Physical Access Controls) and CC6.2 (System Operations), by governing how access is identified, authenticated, and authorized.
*   **General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR):** Supports compliance with Article 32 ("Security of Processing"), which requires the implementation of appropriate technical and organizational measures to ensure a level of security appropriate to the risk, including the ability to ensure the ongoing confidentiality, integrity, and availability of processing systems.
*   **NIST Cybersecurity Framework (CSF) & NIST SP 800-63B:** The password and MFA requirements are aligned with the latest NIST digital identity guidelines, which emphasize password length over complexity and mandate phishing-resistant MFA.
*   **Industry Best Practice:** Aligns with recommendations from cybersecurity authorities such as CISA (Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency) and ENISA (European Union Agency for Cybersecurity) regarding credential hardening.

Non-compliance with this policy not only creates internal risk but may also lead to significant audit findings, regulatory penalties, loss of client trust, and legal liability for Intellfence.

## 7.0 Roles and Responsibilities

**Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):** Holds ultimate authority and accountability for the approval, implementation, communication, and overall enforcement of this policy. The CISO sponsors annual reviews and updates.

**Information Security Team:** Responsible for the development, maintenance, dissemination, and technical oversight of this policy. Duties include configuring IAM systems, reviewing authentication logs and alerts, conducting penetration tests that include credential attacks, and investigating policy violations.

**IT Infrastructure & Operations Teams:** Responsible for implementing the technical controls specified in Section 5 across all systems, servers, network devices, and applications under their management. This includes enforcing password policies in Active Directory, configuring MFA, and managing service account rotations.

**All Personnel (Employees, Contractors, Vendors):** Responsible for reading, understanding, and adhering to all aspects of this policy. This includes creating strong, unique passwords, safeguarding them using the approved password manager, diligently using MFA, and immediately reporting any suspected password compromise or policy violation to the Information Security Team.

**Human Resources (HR):** Responsible for integrating policy acknowledgment into the employee and contractor onboarding process and ensuring that access revocation and credential disabling are integral parts of the offboarding process.

## 8.0 Policy Violations & Enforcement

Adherence to this policy is a fundamental condition of employment and system access at Intellfence. Any personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, commensurate with the severity of the violation. Actions may include, but are not limited to, formal written warnings, mandatory security training, suspension of system access, termination of employment or contractual agreement, and pursuit of legal action in cases involving gross negligence or malicious intent.

The Information Security Team is explicitly authorized to immediately disable or force a password reset for any account found to be in violation of this policy (e.g., using a weak password, sharing credentials) to mitigate imminent risk to the organization. All suspected and confirmed violations shall be documented, investigated, and reported in accordance with the Intellfence Incident Response Policy.

## 9.0 Review and Revision

This Password Policy is a living document that must evolve with the threat landscape and technological advancements. It shall be formally reviewed by the Information Security Team, in consultation with IT and legal stakeholders, at least annually. An ad-hoc review must also be triggered following any significant security incident involving credentials, a major change in the regulatory or compliance landscape, or a fundamental shift in authentication technology.

Proposed revisions must be documented, including a rationale for the change and a risk assessment, and submitted to the CISO for formal approval. Upon approval, the revised policy shall be communicated to all personnel, and the previous version shall be archived. The latest approved version of this policy is always made accessible to all personnel through the official corporate policy repository.', 'draft', 1, 'emmanuel', '2026-02-20 10:05:12.590531', '2026-02-20 10:05:12.590531', 'general', true, '"{}"', NULL, NULL, 'pending', NULL, NULL),
	(1626, 3, 33, NULL, 'Password Policy', '# Intellfence Password Policy

## 1.0 Purpose

This Password Policy establishes the mandatory technical and administrative controls for the creation, management, protection, and secure disposal of all authentication credentials at Intellfence. Its primary objective is to safeguard the organization''s information assets, intellectual property, client data, and critical infrastructure by ensuring that passwords and other secrets are cryptographically strong, kept confidential, and resistant to modern attack vectors. As a cybersecurity firm, Intellfence must exemplify the highest standards of security hygiene. This policy directly supports the protection of the confidentiality, integrity, and availability (CIA triad) of corporate systems and data, forming a foundational element of our defense-in-depth strategy and trust relationship with clients and partners.

## 2.0 Scope

This policy applies universally to all Intellfence personnel, including full-time and part-time employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, interns, and authorized third-party agents who are provisioned access to any Intellfence information system, network, application, database, data repository, or cloud service. It governs all forms of authentication credentials, including but not limited to user passwords, privileged account passwords, service account passwords, application secrets, API keys, cryptographic keys used for authentication, and tokens. This policy is enforceable irrespective of the asset''s location—whether on-premises, in corporate-managed cloud environments (e.g., AWS, Azure, GCP), in software-as-a-service (SaaS) applications, or on personally owned devices used for business purposes (BYOD) where such access is authorized.

## 3.0 Definitions

**Authentication:** The process of verifying the identity of a user, process, or device.
**Password:** A confidential sequence of characters, known only to the user and the system, used as one factor to authenticate identity and authorize access to information resources.
**Passphrase:** A longer sequence of words or other text used similarly to a password, often favored for memorability while maintaining high entropy.
**Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA):** An authentication method that requires the user to provide two or more distinct verification factors from the categories of knowledge (something you know), possession (something you have), and inherence (something you are).
**Privileged Account:** Any user account with elevated permissions beyond a standard user, including but not limited to system/network/database administrators, domain administrators, root accounts, and application service accounts with broad access.
**Service Account:** A non-human account used by an operating system, application, or service to interact with other systems, databases, or network resources.
**Password Manager:** A software application designed to securely store, generate, and manage online credentials in an encrypted vault, protected by a master password and MFA.
**Password Hash:** A cryptographically scrambled, fixed-length representation of a password, generated via a one-way function, used for secure storage and verification.
**Salting:** The practice of appending a random string (salt) to a password before hashing to defend against precomputed rainbow table attacks.
**Brute-Force Attack:** An attack method that attempts to discover a password by systematically trying every possible combination of characters.
**Credential Stuffing:** An attack that uses automated tools to inject large volumes of previously breached username/password pairs into website login forms.
**Password Spraying:** An attack that attempts a single, common password against many different user accounts before trying a second password, thus avoiding account lockout thresholds.

## 4.0 Policy Statement

Intellfence mandates that all authentication credentials shall be created, managed, transmitted, stored, and disposed of according to the stringent, risk-based requirements defined in this policy. The use of weak, default, shared, reused, or known-compromised passwords is strictly prohibited and constitutes a serious security violation. All personnel are personally accountable for understanding, implementing, and adhering to these controls. The technical enforcement of this policy is the responsibility of the Information Security and IT Infrastructure teams, who must configure all systems to comply with these standards. This policy is considered a minimum baseline; specific systems handling confidential or regulated data may require enhanced controls.

## 5.0 Detailed Requirements & Controls

### 5.1 Password Composition and Complexity

All user-created passwords must be constructed to maximize entropy and resist both online guessing and offline cracking attacks. The following minimum requirements are enforced:

*   **Minimum Length:** All standard user account passwords must be a minimum of 14 characters. All passwords for privileged administrative accounts (e.g., domain admin, root, AWS IAM admin) must be a minimum of 20 characters. The use of longer passphrases (e.g., `CorrectHorseBatteryStaple!`) is strongly encouraged for standard accounts and may be required for privileged accounts.
*   **Character Diversity:** Passwords must include characters from at least three of the following four categories: 1) Uppercase letters (A-Z), 2) Lowercase letters (a-z), 3) Numerals (0-9), and 4) Special characters (e.g., ! @ # $ % ^ & * ( ) - _ = + [ ] { } | ; : , . < > / ? ` ~). Systems must be configured to validate this complexity upon password creation and change.
*   **Prohibited Elements:** Passwords must not contain:
    *   The user''s account name, full name, or significant portions thereof.
    *   Common dictionary words in any language, used in isolation.
    *   Simple, predictable sequences (e.g., "12345678", "qwertyuiop", "abcdefgh").
    *   Repetitive or sequential characters (e.g., "aaaaaaa", "123456", "fedcba").
    *   Context-specific words related to Intellfence, such as the company name, product names, or default naming conventions for systems.
    *   Any password known to have been involved in a public data breach. Systems should integrate with compromised password checking services where feasible.

### 5.2 Password Lifecycle Management

Credentials must be managed throughout their entire lifecycle to limit the window of opportunity if a password is compromised.

*   **Initial Provisioning:** All initial passwords for new user accounts shall be system-generated, unique, and meet the complexity requirements of Section 5.1. These temporary passwords must be transmitted to the user via a secure, out-of-band method (e.g., encrypted email with a separate phone call, secure enterprise messaging platform, or physical handover). The user is forced to change this temporary password immediately upon first successful login.
*   **Password Expiration & Rotation:**
    *   **Standard User Accounts:** Passwords must be changed at least every 90 days. Systems should provide warnings starting 14 days prior to expiration.
    *   **Privileged User Accounts:** Passwords must be changed at least every 60 days.
    *   **Service & Application Accounts:** Passwords or secrets must be rotated at least every 180 days, or immediately upon the departure of any personnel who had knowledge of the credential, or following any suspected compromise. Automated secret management tools (e.g., HashiCorp Vault, AWS Secrets Manager) are mandated for this purpose where possible.
*   **Password History:** Authentication systems shall enforce a password history of at least the previous 10 passwords to prevent cyclical reuse. Users cannot revert to any password within this history.

### 5.3 Password Protection and Storage

The protection of passwords at rest and in transit is critical to preventing unauthorized disclosure.

*   **Cryptographic Storage:** Passwords shall never be stored, logged, or cached in plaintext. All passwords at rest must be hashed using a strong, memory-hard, and industry-approved cryptographic hashing algorithm designed for password storage. Acceptable algorithms include bcrypt, scrypt, Argon2id, or PBKDF2 with a sufficiently high work factor (iteration count > 100,000). A unique cryptographically random salt must be used for each password.
*   **Secure Transmission:** Passwords shall only be transmitted over encrypted channels using strong cryptographic protocols. All web-based authentication must use TLS 1.2 or higher. For other protocols, SSH (v2) or IPsec/VPN encryption is required.
*   **Prohibition of Insecure Storage:** The following practices are strictly forbidden:
    *   Writing down passwords on sticky notes, notebooks, or whiteboards.
    *   Storing passwords in unencrypted files (e.g., Word documents, Excel spreadsheets, text files).
    *   Saving passwords in a web browser''s built-in password manager that is not governed by corporate policy.
    *   Sharing passwords via email, instant messaging, or other unencrypted communication channels.
*   **Approved Password Managers:** The corporate-approved enterprise password manager (e.g., 1Password Business, LastPass Enterprise, Keeper) is the required mechanism for storing and managing personal and shared team credentials. Its use is mandatory for all personnel.

### 5.4 Authentication and Access Controls

Technical controls must be implemented to detect and prevent unauthorized access attempts.

*   **Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA):** MFA is mandatory for:
    *   All remote network access (VPN, RDP gateways, Citrix).
    *   Access to all cloud service administration consoles (AWS Management Console, Azure Portal, GCP Console).
    *   All users with privileged access rights.
    *   Access to any system containing sensitive data as classified by the Data Classification Policy.
    *   The approved enterprise password manager.
    *   MFA factors should be phishing-resistant where possible (e.g., FIDO2 security keys, WebAuthn) and time-based one-time passwords (TOTP) as a minimum.
*   **Account Lockout:** To mitigate brute-force and password spraying attacks, user accounts shall be automatically locked after 5 consecutive failed authentication attempts within a 15-minute window. The lockout duration shall be a minimum of 15 minutes, after which the account auto-unlocks, or requires manual intervention by the IT Help Desk. All lockout events must be logged and generate an alert to the Security Operations Center (SOC).
*   **Session Management:**
    *   Interactive user sessions for administrative interfaces and sensitive applications shall automatically terminate after a maximum of 15 minutes of inactivity.
    *   Standard user sessions may have a longer timeout, not to exceed 4 hours of inactivity.
    *   All session tokens must be cryptographically secure, randomly generated, and invalidated both server and client-side upon user logout.
    *   Re-authentication is required for re-establishing a terminated session.

### 5.5 Administrative and Technical Enforcement

System configurations must actively enforce this policy.

*   **Centralized Identity Management:** All authentication should be federated through a centralized Identity and Access Management (IAM) system, such as Microsoft Entra ID (Azure AD) or Okta, to ensure consistent policy application, single sign-on (SSO), and centralized logging.
*   **Automated Compliance Checking:** Automated tools and scripts shall be deployed to periodically scan infrastructure (servers, network devices, databases) for the use of default, weak, or shared passwords, with a particular focus on service accounts. Findings must be remediated according to the Vulnerability Management Policy.
*   **Logging and Monitoring:** All authentication events—successes, failures, password changes, resets, account lockouts, and MFA registrations—must be logged. Logs must contain, at a minimum: timestamp (UTC), source IP address, user identifier (ID), target system/service, event type (success/failure), and any relevant error codes. These logs must be aggregated into the Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) system and retained for a minimum of 90 days for operational purposes and at least one year for audit and forensic investigations.

## 6.0 Compliance & Audit Alignment

This policy is designed to align with and provide demonstrable evidence for compliance with key industry frameworks and regulations relevant to Intellfence''s operations and client commitments.

*   **ISO/IEC 27001:2022:** Directly supports Annex A controls A.5.15 (Identity Management), A.5.16 (Identity, Authentication & Access), A.5.17 (Authentication Information), A.8.2 (Privileged Access Rights), and A.8.9 (Logging).
*   **SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria:** Provides controls for the Security criterion (CC6.1 - Logical and Physical Access Controls), specifically CC6.1.1 (Logical Access Security), CC6.1.2 (Access to Data), CC6.1.3 (Identification and Authentication), and CC6.1.5 (Logical Access Removal).
*   **NIST Cybersecurity Framework (CSF) & NIST SP 800-63B:** Aligns with the Protect (PR.AC) function and implements guidelines for Digital Identity, specifically authenticator requirements for memorized secrets.
*   **General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR):** Supports Article 32 (Security of Processing) by implementing appropriate technical measures, including the ability to ensure the ongoing confidentiality, integrity, and availability of processing systems.
*   **Cloud Security Alliance (CSA) STAR Attestation:** Demonstrates strong identity and access management controls for cloud environments.
Non-compliance with this policy may result in significant audit findings, failure to obtain or maintain certifications, regulatory penalties, legal liability, and severe reputational damage for Intellfence.

## 7.0 Roles and Responsibilities

**Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):** Holds ultimate authority and accountability for the approval, implementation, communication, and overall enforcement of this policy. The CISO is responsible for ensuring the policy remains current with the threat landscape.

**Information Security Team:** Responsible for the development, maintenance, and monitoring of this policy. This includes configuring IAM and SSO systems for policy enforcement, reviewing authentication and security logs, managing the enterprise password manager, and investigating potential violations.

**IT Infrastructure & Operations Teams:** Responsible for implementing the technical controls specified in Section 5 across all on-premises and cloud-based systems, servers, network devices, and applications under their management. This includes enforcing password policies in Active Directory, Linux systems, and cloud IAM policies.

**All Personnel (Employees, Contractors, Third Parties):** Responsible for reading, understanding, and adhering to all aspects of this policy. This includes creating strong, unique passwords, safeguarding them using the approved password manager, never sharing credentials, and promptly using MFA when prompted. Personnel must report any suspected policy violations or compromised credentials immediately to the Information Security Team.

**Human Resources (HR):** Responsible for integrating policy acknowledgment into the employee and contractor onboarding process and ensuring that access revocation and credential disabling are part of the offboarding checklist.

## 8.0 Policy Violations & Enforcement

Adherence to this policy is a condition of employment and continued access to Intellfence systems. Any personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, commensurate with the severity of the violation, up to and including termination of employment or contractual agreement. Violations may also lead to civil or criminal legal action if they result in a data breach or system compromise.

The Information Security Team is explicitly authorized to immediately disable or suspend any user account found to be in violation of this policy (e.g., using a compromised password, sharing credentials) to mitigate imminent risk to the organization. All suspected and confirmed violations shall be documented, investigated, and reported in accordance with the Intellfence Incident Response Policy.

## 9.0 Policy Review and Maintenance

This Password Policy is a living document that must evolve with the threat landscape, technological advancements, and changes in the regulatory environment. It shall be formally reviewed by the Information Security Team, in consultation with IT and Legal, at least annually. An ad-hoc review must also be triggered following any significant security incident involving credentials, a major change in compliance requirements, or a fundamental shift in authentication technology.

Proposed revisions must be documented, assessed for risk and impact, and submitted to the CISO for formal approval. Upon approval, the revised policy shall be communicated to all personnel, and the previous version shall be archived. The latest approved version of this policy is maintained in the corporate policy repository and is accessible to all personnel.', 'draft', 1, 'emmanuel', '2026-02-20 10:19:49.495283', '2026-02-20 10:19:49.495283', 'general', true, '"{}"', NULL, NULL, 'pending', NULL, NULL),
	(1627, 3, 34, NULL, 'Wireless Security Policy', '# Wireless Security Policy

## 1.0 Purpose

This Wireless Security Policy establishes the mandatory requirements for the secure deployment, configuration, management, and monitoring of all wireless networks and devices that access, transmit, or store Intellfence information assets. As a cybersecurity enterprise, Intellfence handles sensitive client data, proprietary threat intelligence, and internal communications that are attractive targets for adversarial actors. The inherent broadcast nature of wireless communications introduces significant risks, including unauthorized network access, data interception, session hijacking, and the deployment of rogue infrastructure for lateral movement.

This policy is designed to protect the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of corporate data and systems from threats inherent in wireless communications. It provides a structured framework to ensure that wireless technology is implemented as a controlled and defensible extension of the corporate network, in strict alignment with Intellfence''s overall Information Security Management System (ISMS) and risk appetite. The policy mandates cryptographic controls, continuous monitoring, and strict governance to mitigate risks to a level acceptable for a trusted cybersecurity service provider.

## 2.0 Scope

This policy applies universally across all Intellfence entities, including all subsidiaries, business units, and physical offices within the NA and EU regions. Its jurisdiction extends to all individuals who interact with Intellfence systems, including full-time and part-time employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, interns, and any authorized third-party personnel.

The policy governs all corporate-owned, leased, or managed wireless network infrastructure, such as wireless access points (APs), wireless controllers, bridges, and management systems. It equally applies to all endpoint devices—whether corporate-issued or personal (under a BYOD arrangement)—that connect to Intellfence wireless networks or that create wireless connections (e.g., ad-hoc networks, personal mobile hotspots, Bluetooth pairings) to access corporate resources, data, or applications.

This encompasses all wireless technologies used for business purposes, including but not limited to: Wi-Fi (all 802.11 standards, including 802.11a/b/g/n/ac/ax), Bluetooth, cellular data modems (e.g., LTE/5G hotspots), and any subsequent emerging wireless technologies (e.g., Wi-Fi 7, Ultra-Wideband). The policy covers both the centrally managed "Enterprise" network and any provisioned "Guest" or specialized wireless networks.

## 3.0 Definitions

**Access Point (AP) / Wireless Access Point (WAP):** A networking hardware device that allows Wi-Fi compliant client devices to connect to a wired network. It serves as the central transmitter and receiver of wireless radio signals.

**Enterprise Wireless Network:** The primary, centrally managed, and authorized wireless network infrastructure deployed and maintained by the Intellfence Information Technology (IT) Department. This network provides authenticated and encrypted access to the corporate intranet and authorized cloud resources.

**Guest Wireless Network:** A logically segregated and tightly controlled wireless network provisioned for visitors, vendors, contractors, or other non-employees. Access is restricted to the public internet only, with no direct routing to internal corporate resources, enforced through firewall rules and a captive portal.

**Rogue Access Point:** Any wireless access point, router, or similar device installed on the corporate network or within corporate facilities without the explicit written authorization of the Intellfence IT Department. This includes unauthorized devices connected to Ethernet ports that provide wireless connectivity.

**WPA3-Enterprise (Wi-Fi Protected Access 3 Enterprise):** The current mandatory security certification and protocol suite for securing enterprise-grade Wi-Fi networks. It provides 256-bit Galois/Counter Mode Protocol (GCMP-256) encryption, Simultaneous Authentication of Equals (SAE) to protect against offline dictionary attacks, and mandates the use of 802.1X/EAP for robust, certificate-based authentication.

**Wireless Intrusion Detection/Prevention System (WIDS/WIPS):** A dedicated security solution that monitors the radio frequency (RF) spectrum to detect and, in prevention mode, block unauthorized wireless devices, misconfigurations, and malicious attack patterns such as evil twin APs, deauthentication attacks, and protocol exploits.

## 4.0 Policy Statement

Intellfence mandates that all wireless network access serving corporate functions shall be implemented and maintained with security as the paramount design principle. The use of wireless technologies within the corporate environment is a privilege granted under strict administrative and technical controls to mitigate risks of unauthorized access, data interception, and network compromise.

All wireless communications involving Intellfence data, systems, or applications must be protected by strong encryption, robust mutual authentication, and are subject to continuous security monitoring. The deployment of unauthorized wireless infrastructure, the intentional circumvention of security controls, or the connection of corporate assets to unsecured wireless networks is strictly prohibited and constitutes a serious violation of this policy.

## 5.0 Policy Requirements

### 5.1 Authorization and Architecture

5.1.1 All wireless infrastructure hardware and software, including access points, wireless controllers, management platforms, and wireless bridges, must be approved, procured, standardized, and deployed solely by the Intellfence Information Technology (IT) Department. No other department or individual is permitted to install wireless networking equipment.

5.1.2 A formal, up-to-date network architecture diagram detailing all wireless infrastructure must be maintained by the Network Team. This diagram must include physical and logical placement of all APs, controller locations, RF coverage maps, channel assignments, transmit power settings, and VLAN segmentation schemas. This architecture document shall be reviewed and validated at least annually, or after any significant network change.

5.1.3 The implementation of personal wireless access points, wireless routers, ad-hoc wireless networks, or software-based virtual APs within corporate facilities is expressly forbidden. This prohibition extends to the use of USB wireless adapters configured to bridge networks or to extend an unauthorized wireless signal.

### 5.2 Encryption and Authentication Standards

5.2.1 All enterprise-class wireless networks at Intellfence shall utilize WPA3-Enterprise with AES-256-GCM encryption as the mandatory standard. Transitional use of WPA2-Enterprise (with AES-CCMP) is permitted only for legacy client devices that lack WPA3 support and must be documented in a formal asset register with a risk-accepted migration plan and decommissioning timeline.

5.2.2 The use of deprecated and cryptographically broken security protocols, including Wired Equivalent Privacy (WEP), Wi-Fi Protected Access Personal (WPA-PSK/WPA2-PSK), or open/unencrypted networks for any corporate purpose, is strictly forbidden. Pre-shared keys (PSKs) are prohibited on the enterprise network.

5.2.3 Authentication for the enterprise wireless network must leverage a centralized, dedicated authentication service (e.g., RADIUS server) integrated with the corporate identity provider (e.g., Microsoft Entra ID / Active Directory). The preferred authentication method is 802.1X/EAP-TLS using client and server certificates for mutual authentication. EAP-PEAP (MSCHAPv2) may be used where certificate deployment is not feasible, provided it is configured to validate server certificates to prevent man-in-the-middle attacks.

5.2.4 Guest network access shall be secured via a captive portal system. Access credentials must be unique, time-limited, and issued per session or per visitor. If a PSK must be used for a specific guest use-case, it must be distinct from any other network key, changed at least quarterly, and its distribution must be logged. Guest network traffic must be routed through a separate internet breakout with no access to the internal corporate network.

### 5.3 Device Configuration and Hardening

5.3.1 All authorized wireless access points and controllers shall be hardened according to a standardized baseline configuration. This includes changing all default administrative credentials, disabling unused services (e.g., Telnet, HTTP management, SNMP if not required), and enabling management interface encryption (SSH, HTTPS). Firmware must be kept up-to-date, with critical security patches applied within 30 days of vendor release, following a tested change management process.

5.3.2 Service Set Identifiers (SSIDs) for corporate networks shall be generic and shall not contain information that could identify Intellfence as the operator, its specific location, department, or function (e.g., "Intellfence_Corp," "Boston_Office," "HR_Network"). SSID broadcasting may be disabled for specialized networks if it does not impact legitimate client functionality.

5.3.3 Wireless signal strength (transmit power) shall be carefully tuned to provide adequate coverage within the intended physical premises while minimizing propagation beyond controllable boundaries (e.g., outside the building, into public areas). Site surveys shall be conducted to optimize coverage and reduce the attack surface from adjacent properties.

### 5.4 Monitoring and Intrusion Detection

5.4.1 A dedicated Wireless Intrusion Detection and Prevention System (WIDS/WIPS) shall be deployed to provide continuous, 24/7 monitoring of the 2.4 GHz and 5 GHz (and 6 GHz if used) radio frequency spectrum. The system must be configured to automatically detect and alert on rogue access points, misconfigured clients, ad-hoc networks, evil twin attacks, deauthentication floods, and other wireless-specific attack patterns.

5.4.2 Comprehensive logging is mandatory. All authentication successes and failures from the wireless infrastructure and RADIUS servers, all administrative access events to wireless controllers and management systems, and all alerts from the WIDS/WIPS must be forwarded to the centralized Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) system. These logs must be retained for a minimum period of 90 days to support incident investigation and compliance audits.

5.4.3 The Information Security Team, in coordination with the IT Department, shall conduct periodic wireless vulnerability assessments and authorized penetration tests at least annually. These assessments must include RF spectrum analysis, attempts to identify and exploit weak encryption or authentication, and testing for rogue device deployment. Findings must be documented, risk-assessed, and remediated according to the corporate vulnerability management policy.

### 5.5 Endpoint and Client Security

5.5.1 All corporate-managed laptops, mobile devices, and other endpoints must have their wireless interfaces configured via a Mobile Device Management (MDM) or Unified Endpoint Management (UEM) solution. Configuration profiles must enforce connection only to approved Intellfence SSIDs and must prevent users from modifying these settings or saving credentials for unauthorized networks.

5.5.2 Users are prohibited from configuring corporate devices to connect to untrusted or public wireless networks (e.g., coffee shops, airports, hotels) for the purpose of accessing sensitive corporate data, email, or internal applications, unless a mandated, company-provided and configured Virtual Private Network (VPN) with always-on or triggered connectivity is used. The VPN must use strong cryptography (e.g., IKEv2/IPsec, WireGuard) and certificate-based authentication.

5.5.3 The use of personal devices (Bring Your Own Device - BYOD) to connect to the enterprise wireless network is permitted only through formal enrollment in the corporate BYOD program. This program mandates device registration, enforcement of minimum security posture (e.g., screen lock, encryption, OS version), and the use of containerization or secure workspace technology to isolate corporate data and applications from personal use.

## 6.0 Compliance & Audit

This policy is designed to enforce controls that support Intellfence''s compliance with relevant legal, regulatory, and contractual obligations. This includes data protection regulations such as the General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR) in the EU and various state-level laws in the US (e.g., CCPA) by protecting the confidentiality and integrity of personal data in transit.

The policy directly maps to and supports the following key industry standards:
*   **ISO/IEC 27001:2022:** Annex A controls including A.8.1 (Asset Management), A.8.9 (Information Access Restriction), A.8.14 (Wireless Access Control), A.8.15 (Logging), A.8.16 (Monitoring), and A.8.23 (Information Security for Use of Cloud Services).
*   **SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria:** Specifically supports the Security criterion (CC6.1, CC6.3, CC6.6, CC6.7, CC6.8) and the Confidentiality criterion.
*   **NIST Cybersecurity Framework:** Aligns with Protect (PR.AC-1, PR.AC-3, PR.DS-2) and Detect (DE.CM-4) functions.
*   **HIPAA Security Rule:** Supports requirements for Access Control (§164.312(a)), Transmission Security (§164.312(e)), and Audit Controls (§164.312(b)) where relevant to protected health information (PHI).

Any requested deviation from this policy for legitimate business or technical necessity requires the submission of a formal risk assessment and an exception request, which must be reviewed and approved in writing by the Chief Information Security Officer (CISO). Exceptions are granted for a limited time and must be re-evaluated periodically.

## 7.0 Roles and Responsibilities

**Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):** Bears ultimate responsibility for the approval, oversight, communication, and enforcement of this policy. The CISO is responsible for reviewing and authorizing any policy exceptions.

**Information Technology (IT) Department / Network Team:** Responsible for the secure design, implementation, maintenance, patching, and operational monitoring of all authorized wireless infrastructure. This team manages the WIDS/WIPS, applies configuration changes, and is the first responder to technical incidents involving wireless infrastructure.

**Information Security Team:** Responsible for the periodic review and update of this policy, conducting wireless security assessments and penetration tests, analyzing WIDS/WIPS and SIEM alerts for malicious activity, and leading the investigation of policy violations and wireless security incidents.

**All Users (Employees, Contractors, Third Parties):** Required to read, understand, and comply with all aspects of this policy. Users must utilize only authorized wireless networks for business activities, must not attempt to install unauthorized equipment, must report any suspected security incidents or observed unauthorized wireless devices immediately to the IT Help Desk or Security Team, and must adhere to endpoint security requirements.

## 8.0 Enforcement and Violations

Compliance with this policy will be enforced through a combination of technical controls (MDM, WIDS/WIPS, NAC), automated monitoring (SIEM), and administrative procedures. The policy is incorporated into employee and contractor agreements.

Violations of this policy may result in disciplinary action, the nature of which will be commensurate with the severity and impact of the violation. Actions may include, but are not limited to: formal written warning, revocation of system access privileges, mandatory security training, termination of employment, or termination of contractual agreement. Violations may also lead to civil or criminal liability where laws are breached.

The IT and Information Security Departments are explicitly authorized to immediately disconnect, disable, or quarantine any unauthorized wireless device or network that is detected and poses an imminent security risk to Intellfence assets, without prior notice to the owner.

All personnel shall be required to formally acknowledge receipt and understanding of this policy upon initial hire or engagement and re-affirm this acknowledgment annually as part of security awareness training.

## 9.0 Policy Review and Maintenance

This Wireless Security Policy shall be reviewed by the Information Security Team, in consultation with the IT Department, at least annually. Reviews shall also be triggered by significant security incidents, material changes in the threat landscape, the introduction of new wireless technologies, or updates to relevant compliance standards (e.g., new ISO 27001 controls, NIST guidelines).

Proposed revisions must be documented with a rationale for change and submitted to the CISO for final review and approval. Upon approval, the revised policy shall be communicated to all relevant stakeholders. The latest approved version of this policy shall be maintained as the authoritative document and made accessible to all personnel through the corporate policy repository or intranet.', 'draft', 1, 'emmanuel', '2026-02-20 10:20:55.163104', '2026-02-20 10:20:55.163104', 'general', true, '"{}"', NULL, NULL, 'pending', NULL, NULL),
	(1631, 3, 58, NULL, 'GDPR Privacy Policy', '# Intellfence GDPR Privacy Policy

## 1.0 Purpose and Policy Statement

This General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR) Privacy Policy establishes the mandatory governance, risk, and compliance framework for Intellfence. Its primary purpose is to define the principles, technical controls, administrative procedures, and organizational responsibilities required to ensure the lawful, fair, and transparent processing of personal data.

As a cybersecurity entity, Intellfence recognizes that its commitment to data protection is foundational to its mission and credibility. This policy is designed to achieve and demonstrate full compliance with Regulation (EU) 2016/679 (GDPR), thereby protecting the fundamental rights and freedoms of data subjects, mitigating organizational risk, and aligning with industry-leading security and privacy frameworks including ISO/IEC 27001:2022, the SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria, and relevant aspects of HIPAA for any applicable processing activities.

## 2.0 Scope and Applicability

This policy applies universally to all processing of personal data, as defined by the GDPR, conducted by or on behalf of Intellfence. This includes all activities performed by its permanent employees, temporary staff, contractors, interns, and authorized third-party Data Processors.

The scope encompasses all technological and physical environments where personal data is processed. This includes, but is not limited to: production and development cloud infrastructure (e.g., AWS, Azure, GCP), on-premises servers, endpoint devices, corporate applications (e.g., CRM, HR systems), security monitoring tools, backup systems, and any structured manual filing systems.

Critically, this policy applies extraterritorially in accordance with Article 3 of the GDPR. It governs processing activities regardless of where they physically occur, if the processing relates to:
*   The offering of cybersecurity goods or services (e.g., threat intelligence feeds, managed detection and response services, software-as-a-service platforms) to data subjects in the European Union (EU) or the European Economic Area (EEA).
*   The monitoring of data subjects'' behavior (e.g., network traffic analysis, user and entity behavior analytics - UEBA) that takes place within the EU/EEA.

## 3.0 Core Data Protection Principles

Intellfence mandates that all personal data processing activities adhere strictly to the following principles articulated in Article 5 of the GDPR. These principles are non-negotiable and form the bedrock of our privacy program:

*   **Lawfulness, Fairness, and Transparency:** Processing must have a valid lawful basis (defined in Section 5.1). It must be conducted in a manner that is fair to the data subject and transparent regarding how and why their data is used.
*   **Purpose Limitation:** Personal data is collected for specified, explicit, and legitimate purposes and not further processed in a manner that is incompatible with those purposes.
*   **Data Minimization:** Personal data collected shall be adequate, relevant, and limited to what is strictly necessary in relation to the purposes for which it is processed. As a cybersecurity firm, we apply this principle rigorously to threat data and log information that may contain personal identifiers.
*   **Accuracy:** Personal data must be kept accurate and, where necessary, kept up to date. Every reasonable step must be taken to ensure that inaccurate data, having regard to the purposes for which it is processed, is erased or rectified without delay.
*   **Storage Limitation:** Personal data is kept in a form which permits identification of data subjects for no longer than is necessary for the purposes for which the personal data is processed. Specific, justified retention schedules are defined and enforced.
*   **Integrity and Confidentiality (Security):** Personal data must be processed in a manner that ensures appropriate security, including protection against unauthorized or unlawful processing and against accidental loss, destruction, or damage, using appropriate technical and organizational measures. Given our industry, we implement security controls that meet or exceed best practices for protecting sensitive information.
*   **Accountability:** Intellfence is responsible for, and must be able to demonstrate compliance with, all of the above principles.

## 4.0 Key Definitions

For the purposes of this policy, the following terms shall have the meanings ascribed to them by the GDPR and as interpreted by relevant supervisory authorities:

*   **Personal Data:** Any information relating to an identified or identifiable natural person (''data subject''); an identifiable natural person is one who can be identified, directly or indirectly, in particular by reference to an identifier such as a name, an identification number, location data, an online identifier (e.g., IP address, cookie ID, device fingerprint), or to one or more factors specific to the physical, physiological, genetic, mental, economic, cultural, or social identity of that natural person.
*   **Processing:** Any operation or set of operations which is performed on personal data or on sets of personal data, whether or not by automated means, such as collection, recording, organization, structuring, storage, adaptation or alteration, retrieval, consultation, use, disclosure by transmission, dissemination or otherwise making available, alignment or combination, restriction, erasure, or destruction.
*   **Data Controller:** The natural or legal person, public authority, agency, or other body which, alone or jointly with others, determines the purposes and means of the processing of personal data. For most of its business activities, Intellfence acts as a Data Controller.
*   **Data Processor:** A natural or legal person, public authority, agency, or other body which processes personal data on behalf of the controller. Intellfence may engage sub-processors (e.g., cloud infrastructure providers) and, in certain service contexts, may act as a Data Processor for its clients.
*   **Special Categories of Data (Sensitive Data):** Personal data revealing racial or ethnic origin, political opinions, religious or philosophical beliefs, or trade union membership, and the processing of genetic data, biometric data for the purpose of uniquely identifying a natural person, data concerning health, or data concerning a natural person''s sex life or sexual orientation. Processing of such data is prohibited unless a specific condition under Article 9 is met.
*   **Data Protection Impact Assessment (DPIA):** A systematic process designed to identify and minimize the data protection risks of a project or processing activity, particularly those involving new technologies, large-scale processing, or systematic monitoring of publicly accessible areas.
*   **Personal Data Breach:** A breach of security leading to the accidental or unlawful destruction, loss, alteration, unauthorized disclosure of, or access to, personal data transmitted, stored, or otherwise processed.

## 5.0 Policy Requirements & Operational Controls

### 5.1 Lawful Basis for Processing

Processing of personal data shall only be conducted under one of the six lawful bases defined in Article 6(1) GDPR. The applicable basis must be determined, documented in the Register of Processing Activities (ROPA), and communicated to the data subject *prior* to the initiation of processing.

*   **Consent:** Must be freely given, specific, informed, and an unambiguous indication of the data subject''s wishes. For cybersecurity services, consent is rarely the primary basis, but may be used for marketing communications. Clear mechanisms for withdrawal must be provided.
*   **Contract:** Processing is necessary for the performance of a contract with the data subject or to take steps at the data subject''s request prior to entering into a contract (e.g., providing a licensed software platform).
*   **Legal Obligation:** Processing is necessary for compliance with a legal obligation to which Intellfence is subject (e.g., tax records, compliance with lawful intercept requests under strict legal authority).
*   **Vital Interests:** Processing is necessary to protect the vital interests of the data subject or of another natural person (e.g., in a life-threatening emergency).
*   **Public Task:** Processing is necessary for the performance of a task carried out in the public interest or in the exercise of official authority vested in Intellfence (typically applicable to public sector contracts).
*   **Legitimate Interests:** Processing is necessary for the purposes of the legitimate interests pursued by Intellfence or by a third party, except where such interests are overridden by the interests or fundamental rights and freedoms of the data subject. This basis requires a documented Legitimate Interests Assessment (LIA), balancing our interests against the individual''s rights. This may be relevant for security monitoring of our own corporate network, fraud prevention, or certain B2B marketing activities.

For **Special Categories of Data**, processing is expressly prohibited unless one of the specific conditions in Article 9(2) is met (e.g., explicit consent, necessity for employment law, vital interests, etc.). Any such processing requires prior approval from the Data Protection Officer (DPO) and heightened security controls.

### 5.2 Data Subject Rights Management

Intellfence shall implement technical and procedural controls to facilitate the exercise of data subject rights within the statutory one-month timeframe (extendable by two further months where necessary). A centralized request management system shall be used to log, track, and audit all requests.

Rights include:
*   **Right of Access (Subject Access Request - SAR):** The right to obtain confirmation as to whether or not personal data concerning them is being processed, and, where that is the case, access to that data and specific information about the processing.
*   **Right to Rectification:** The right to have inaccurate personal data corrected.
*   **Right to Erasure (''Right to be Forgotten''):** The right to have personal data erased under specific circumstances (e.g., where data is no longer necessary, consent is withdrawn, or processing is unlawful).
*   **Right to Restriction of Processing:** The right to restrict processing in certain scenarios (e.g., where accuracy is contested, processing is unlawful, or the data subject needs it for legal claims).
*   **Right to Data Portability:** The right to receive personal data provided to Intellfence in a structured, commonly used, and machine-readable format, and to transmit that data to another controller, where processing is based on consent or contract and carried out by automated means.
*   **Right to Object:** The right to object, on grounds relating to their particular situation, to processing based on legitimate interests or public task, and an absolute right to object to processing for direct marketing.
*   **Rights related to Automated Decision-Making, including Profiling:** The right not to be subject to a decision based solely on automated processing, including profiling, which produces legal effects or similarly significantly affects them. As a cybersecurity company, any automated threat scoring or blocking that significantly impacts a user will incorporate human review or an appeal mechanism.

All requests must be authenticated to prevent unauthorized disclosure. Refusals to act on a request must be justified, documented, and communicated to the data subject along with information on their right to lodge a complaint with a supervisory authority.

### 5.3 Data Protection by Design and by Default (PbD)

Privacy and data protection considerations are integrated into the design and architecture of all new systems, applications, products, and business processes from the outset (''by design''). The default configuration of any system must always be the most privacy-protective setting (''by default'').

*   **Technical Implementation:** Default measures include pseudonymization of personal data within threat intelligence datasets, end-to-end encryption (using standards such as AES-256 or TLS 1.3) for data in transit and at rest, and strict attribute-based or role-based access controls (ABAC/RBAC) with the principle of least privilege.
*   **Data Minimization in Product Development:** Product managers and engineers must define the minimum dataset required for each product feature. Collection of extraneous log fields, metadata, or user information is prohibited.
*   **Privacy Reviews & DPIAs:** All new projects involving personal data processing must undergo a formal privacy review. Projects likely to result in a high risk to rights and freedoms (e.g., large-scale monitoring, processing of sensitive data, systematic use of new technologies) must undergo a full DPIA prior to launch.

### 5.4 Data Inventory and Mapping (Register of Processing Activities - ROPA)

Intellfence shall maintain a comprehensive, dynamic, and centralized Register of Processing Activities (ROPA) as required by Article 30 GDPR. This serves as the single source of truth for all data processing within the organization.

The ROPA must document, for each processing activity:
*   Name and contact details of the controller (and joint controllers, DPO).
*   Purposes of the processing.
*   Categories of data subjects and personal data.
*   Categories of recipients (including third countries).
*   Where applicable, documentation of suitable safeguards for international transfers.
*   Envisaged time limits for erasure of the different data categories.
*   A general description of the technical and organizational security measures (referencing Section 5.6).

The ROPA is maintained by the respective business unit Data Controllers under the oversight of the DPO and must be available for inspection by supervisory authorities.

### 5.5 Vendor and Third-Party Processor Management

All engagements where a third party will process personal data on behalf of Intellfence require a GDPR-compliant Data Processing Agreement (DPA) prior to any data transfer. The DPA must stipulate the processor''s obligations regarding confidentiality, security, sub-processing, cooperation with rights requests, breach notification, and audit rights.

*   **Due Diligence:** A security and privacy assessment is mandatory prior to onboarding. This includes reviewing the vendor''s certifications (e.g., ISO 27001, SOC 2 Type II reports), security questionnaires, and audit reports.
*   **Ongoing Monitoring:** Vendor risk is reassessed annually or following a significant security incident. Intellfence reserves the right to conduct audits or request up-to-date attestations.
*   **Sub-processor Governance:** Any sub-processor engaged by our processor requires our prior specific or general written authorization. A list of authorized sub-processors will be maintained and made available.

### 5.6 Security of Processing

Given our core competency in cybersecurity, Intellfence implements a defense-in-depth strategy to protect personal data that exceeds baseline requirements. Our security program is aligned with the ISO 27001 control framework and NIST Cybersecurity Framework.

Mandatory controls include, but are not limited to:
*   **Cryptography:** Encryption of all personal data at rest (using AES-256 or stronger) and in transit (TLS 1.2+). Management of encryption keys via a dedicated, secure key management service (KMS).
*   **Access Control:** Mandatory Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA) for all administrative access and access to production systems containing personal data. Just-in-Time (JIT) and role-based access controls are enforced.
*   **Logging and Monitoring:** Comprehensive, immutable logging of all access, modification, and deletion events related to personal data repositories. Logs are aggregated in a centralized Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) system with a minimum 90-day retention for active analysis and one year for archival.
*   **Resilience:** Regular, tested backup procedures for personal data, with backups encrypted and stored separately.
*   **Testing:** Regular vulnerability scanning (at least quarterly) and annual penetration testing conducted by independent third parties on all systems processing personal data. Findings are tracked to remediation in a risk-based manner.
*   **Physical Security:** For any on-premises infrastructure, physical access controls including biometrics, access logs, and environmental controls are implemented.

### 5.7 Personal Data Breach Notification Protocol

Intellfence maintains a formal, tested Incident Response Plan (IRP) that specifically addresses personal data breaches. The protocol is designed for rapid detection, assessment, containment, and notification.

*   **Internal Reporting:** All employees and contractors are trained to immediately report any suspected breach to the Security Operations Center (SOC) and the DPO.
*   **Assessment:** The DPO, in conjunction with the CISO, will lead an assessment to determine if the incident constitutes a personal data breach, the likely risk to data subjects, and the categories of data and individuals affected.
*   **Regulatory Notification:** Where the breach is likely to result in a risk to the rights and freedoms of natural persons, the relevant supervisory authority (e.g., the Irish Data Protection Commission, as our likely lead authority) shall be notified **within 72 hours** of becoming aware of the breach. The notification will include all required details per Article 33(3).
*   **Data Subject Notification:** If the breach is likely to result in a **high risk** to rights and freedoms, affected data subjects will be notified **without undue delay** in clear and plain language, describing the nature of the breach and recommended mitigation steps.
*   **Documentation:** All breaches, regardless of notification requirement, are fully documented, including facts, effects, and remedial actions taken, to demonstrate compliance with Article 33(5).

### 5.8 Data Retention and Secure Disposal

Personal data shall not be retained indefinitely. Retention schedules are established for each category of personal data, linked directly to the processing purpose and legal/regulatory requirements (e.g., tax law, statute of limitations for claims).

*   **Schedules:** Retention periods are documented in the ROPA and enforced through automated system rules where possible (e.g., automated deletion scripts, lifecycle policies in cloud storage).
*   **Secure Disposal:** Upon expiry of the retention period, personal data is securely and irreversibly destroyed. Methods include:
    *   **Digital Data:** Cryptographic erasure (crypto-shredding) of encryption keys, rendering data irrecoverable, or secure deletion algorithms meeting standards like NIST SP 800-88.
    *   **Physical Media:** Physical destruction (shredding, degaussing) of hard drives and storage media.
    *   **Paper Records:** Cross-cut shredding or incineration by a certified vendor.

### 5.9 International Data Transfers

Transfers of personal data to countries outside the EU/EEA not covered by an Adequacy Decision are strictly controlled.

*   **Adequacy:** Transfers are permitted to countries deemed ''adequate'' by the European Commission (e.g., UK, Switzerland, Japan).
*   **Appropriate Safeguards:** In the absence of adequacy, transfers may proceed only if one of the following safeguards is implemented:
    *   **Standard Contractual Clauses (SCCs):** The current EU Commission-approved SCCs must be incorporated into contracts with the importer. A documented **Transfer Impact Assessment (TIA)** must be conducted to evaluate the legal regime of the destination country and implement supplementary technical measures (e.g., encryption with client-held keys) to mitigate risks.
    *   **Binding Corporate Rules (BCRs):** For intra-group transfers, BCRs may be pursued as a long-term framework.
*   **Derogations:** In limited, specific situations, derogations under Article 49 may be used (e.g., explicit consent, necessity for contract performance). These are applied restrictively and documented.

## 6.0 Compliance with Legal and Certification Frameworks

This policy is the cornerstone of Intellfence''s data protection compliance program, designed to satisfy the requirements of the GDPR. Its implementation directly supports and provides evidence for compliance with several overlapping frameworks:

*   **ISO/IEC 27001:2022:** Specific controls are addressed, including A.5.34 (Privacy and protection of PII), A.8.3 (Information access restriction), A.8.10 (Information deletion), A.8.12 (Data leakage prevention), and the entirety of Annex A.8 (Asset Management).
*   **SOC 2 (Trust Services Criteria):** The policy enforces criteria for Security, Availability, Processing Integrity, and crucially, **Privacy** (P-series criteria regarding notice, choice, collection, use/retention/disclosure, and access).
*   **HIPAA:** For', 'draft', 1, NULL, '2026-02-20 12:19:11.481599', '2026-02-20 12:19:11.481599', 'general', true, '"{}"', NULL, NULL, 'pending', NULL, NULL),
	(1632, 3, 58, NULL, 'GDPR Privacy Policy', '# Intellfence GDPR Privacy Policy

## 1.0 Purpose and Policy Statement

This General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR) Privacy Policy establishes the mandatory governance, technical, and operational framework for Intellfence to ensure the lawful, fair, and transparent processing of personal data. As a cybersecurity entity, we recognize our heightened responsibility in modeling exemplary data stewardship. The purpose of this document is to define the principles, controls, and procedures required to achieve, maintain, and demonstrably prove compliance with Regulation (EU) 2016/679 (GDPR). This policy is designed to protect the fundamental rights and freedoms of data subjects, mitigate organizational risk, and build trust with our clients, partners, and employees by embedding privacy and security into our core operations.

Intellfence is unequivocally committed to protecting the privacy and security of all personal data for which we act as a Data Controller or Data Processor. All processing activities shall adhere to the core data protection principles of lawfulness, fairness, and transparency; purpose limitation; data minimization; accuracy; storage limitation; integrity and confidentiality (security); and accountability. We mandate a ''privacy by design and by default'' philosophy across all product development, service delivery, and internal business processes.

## 2.0 Scope

This policy applies universally to all processing of personal data, as defined by the GDPR, conducted by or on behalf of Intellfence. This includes processing activities performed by our permanent employees, temporary staff, contractors, consultants, and any authorized third-party Data Processors acting under our instruction.

The policy encompasses all technological and physical environments where personal data is processed, including but not limited to: production and development cloud infrastructure (e.g., AWS, Azure, GCP), on-premises servers, endpoint devices, corporate applications (CRM, HR systems), security monitoring platforms, and any structured manual filing systems. It applies regardless of the geographic location of the processing, if the activities relate to the offering of cybersecurity goods or services to data subjects in the European Economic Area (EEA) or the monitoring of their behavior within the EEA, including through our website, threat intelligence platforms, or managed security services.

## 3.0 Definitions

For the purposes of this policy, the following terms shall have the meanings ascribed to them by the GDPR and as detailed below:

*   **Personal Data:** Any information relating to an identified or identifiable natural person (''data subject''); an identifiable natural person is one who can be identified, directly or indirectly, in particular by reference to an identifier such as a name, an identification number, location data, an online identifier (e.g., IP address, cookie ID) or to one or more factors specific to the physical, physiological, genetic, mental, economic, cultural, or social identity of that natural person.
*   **Processing:** Any operation or set of operations which is performed on personal data or on sets of personal data, whether or not by automated means, such as collection, recording, organization, structuring, storage, adaptation or alteration, retrieval, consultation, use, disclosure by transmission, dissemination or otherwise making available, alignment or combination, restriction, erasure, or destruction.
*   **Data Controller:** The natural or legal person, public authority, agency, or other body which, alone or jointly with others, determines the purposes and means of the processing of personal data. For most client service delivery, Intellfence acts as a Data Processor. For its own HR, marketing, and business operations, Intellfence is the Data Controller.
*   **Data Processor:** A natural or legal person, public authority, agency, or other body which processes personal data on behalf of the controller, under explicit contractual instruction.
*   **Special Categories of Data (Sensitive Data):** Personal data revealing racial or ethnic origin, political opinions, religious or philosophical beliefs, or trade union membership, and the processing of genetic data, biometric data for the purpose of uniquely identifying a natural person, data concerning health, or data concerning a natural person''s sex life or sexual orientation. Processing of such data is prohibited unless a specific condition under Article 9 of the GDPR is met.
*   **Data Protection Impact Assessment (DPIA):** A systematic, risk-based process designed to identify, assess, and mitigate data protection risks arising from new or significantly changed processing activities, particularly those involving innovative technologies, large-scale processing of special category data, or systematic monitoring of publicly accessible areas.
*   **Personal Data Breach:** A breach of security leading to the accidental or unlawful destruction, loss, alteration, unauthorized disclosure of, or access to, personal data transmitted, stored, or otherwise processed.

## 4.0 Core Policy Requirements

### 4.1 Lawful Basis for Processing

Intellfence shall only process personal data where a valid, documented lawful basis exists under Article 6 of the GDPR. The applicable basis must be determined and recorded in the Register of Processing Activities (ROPA) prior to the initiation of processing. The lawful bases are: (a) the data subject has given consent; (b) processing is necessary for the performance of a contract; (c) processing is necessary for compliance with a legal obligation; (d) processing is necessary to protect the vital interests of the data subject; (e) processing is necessary for the performance of a task carried out in the public interest; or (f) processing is necessary for the purposes of the legitimate interests pursued by Intellfence or a third party, except where such interests are overridden by the interests or fundamental rights of the data subject. A Legitimate Interests Assessment (LIA) must be documented for any processing relying on basis (f).

For any processing of Special Categories of Data, an additional explicit condition under Article 9 must be satisfied and documented (e.g., explicit consent, necessity for employment law obligations). Such processing is subject to enhanced governance and requires prior approval from the Data Protection Officer (DPO).

### 4.2 Data Subject Rights Management

Intellfence shall implement robust technical and organizational measures to facilitate the exercise of data subject rights within the statutory one-month period. We will establish and maintain a secure, auditable process for receiving, validating, and fulfilling requests for:
*   **Right of Access (Subject Access Request):** Providing a copy of personal data and related processing information.
*   **Right to Rectification:** Correcting inaccurate or incomplete data.
*   **Right to Erasure (''Right to be Forgotten''):** Deleting personal data under specific circumstances.
*   **Right to Restriction of Processing:** Temporarily halting processing of data.
*   **Right to Data Portability:** Receiving personal data in a structured, commonly used, machine-readable format.
*   **Right to Object:** Objecting to processing based on legitimate interests or direct marketing.

All requests will be logged in a dedicated tracking system with immutable audit trails, recording the request, validation steps, actions taken, and final outcome. Identity verification procedures will be employed to prevent unauthorized disclosure. Where we act as a Processor, we will contractually commit to assisting the Controller in fulfilling these obligations.

### 4.3 Data Protection by Design and by Default

Privacy and data protection considerations are integrated into the inception, design, development, and operation of all products, services, systems, and business processes. This is achieved through:
*   **Privacy Reviews:** Mandatory privacy reviews for all new projects involving personal data, informed by the ROPA.
*   **Technical Safeguards:** Default implementation of state-of-the-art security measures, including pseudonymization and encryption (AES-256 or stronger for data at rest, TLS 1.2+ for data in transit), strict access controls following the principle of least privilege (Role-Based Access Control - RBAC), and comprehensive logging.
*   **Data Minimization:** Collection and processing of personal data is limited to what is strictly necessary, adequate, and relevant for the explicitly specified, legitimate purpose. Data collection fields, retention periods, and access permissions are minimized by default.

### 4.4 Data Inventory and Mapping (Register of Processing Activities - ROPA)

A centralized, dynamic, and accurate Register of Processing Activities (ROPA) shall be maintained as required by Article 30 of the GDPR. This register will document, for each processing activity:
*   The name and contact details of the Controller/Processor and the DPO.
*   The purposes of the processing.
*   Categories of data subjects and personal data.
*   Categories of recipients, including third countries or international organizations.
*   Where applicable, documentation of suitable safeguards for international transfers.
*   Envisaged time limits for erasure of the different data categories.
*   A general description of the technical and organizational security measures.

The ROPA is a living document, updated promptly with any change in processing activities, and is made available to supervisory authorities upon request.

### 4.5 Vendor and Third-Party Processor Management

All engagements where a third party will process personal data on behalf of Intellfence (as a Processor) or where we share Controller data require stringent oversight:
*   **Due Diligence:** Prior to engagement, a risk-based assessment of the vendor''s technical and organizational security measures must be conducted, reviewing certifications (e.g., ISO 27001, SOC 2 Type II), audit reports, and security questionnaires.
*   **Data Processing Agreement (DPA):** A GDPR-compliant DPA, incorporating the mandatory clauses from Article 28, must be executed before any personal data is transferred. The DPA must clearly stipulate the subject matter, duration, nature, and purpose of processing, the type of personal data, the obligations and rights of the Controller, and the Processor''s commitments regarding sub-processing, security, breach notification, and assistance.
*   **Ongoing Monitoring:** Vendor performance and compliance will be monitored through regular reviews, updated audit reports, and as part of the incident management process.

### 4.6 Security of Processing

In line with our cybersecurity expertise, Intellfence shall implement a risk-based suite of technical and organizational measures to ensure a level of security appropriate to the risk, including protection against unauthorized or unlawful processing and against accidental loss, destruction, or damage. Mandatory controls include, but are not limited to:
*   **Cryptography:** Encryption of all personal data at rest within our environments and strong encryption (TLS 1.2+) for all data in transit across public networks.
*   **Access Control:** Multi-factor authentication (MFA) is mandatory for all administrative and privileged access to systems handling personal data. Access is granted on a strict need-to-know basis following the principle of least privilege, with regular access reviews.
*   **Logging and Monitoring:** Comprehensive, tamper-evident logging of all access, modification, and deletion events related to personal data processing systems. Logs are centrally aggregated, analyzed by our Security Operations Center (SOC), and retained for a minimum of 90 days for investigative purposes.
*   **Resilience and Testing:** Systems are designed for resilience and availability. Regular vulnerability scanning, application security testing (SAST/DAST), and independent penetration tests are conducted to identify and remediate security weaknesses.
*   **Physical Security:** Where applicable, data center and office facilities are protected by physical access controls, surveillance, and environmental safeguards.

### 4.7 Personal Data Breach Notification Protocol

Intellfence maintains a formal, tested Incident Response Plan that specifically addresses personal data breaches. The protocol mandates:
*   **Detection and Assessment:** Immediate action to contain any suspected breach and a prompt assessment to determine if the incident constitutes a personal data breach, evaluating the likely risk to individuals'' rights and freedoms.
*   **Notification to Supervisory Authority:** Where a breach is likely to result in a risk to rights and freedoms, the relevant lead supervisory authority will be notified without undue delay and, where feasible, within 72 hours of becoming aware of the breach. The notification will include all required details as per Article 33(3).
*   **Communication to Data Subjects:** If the breach is likely to result in a **high risk** to the rights and freedoms of individuals, Intellfence (as Controller) will communicate the breach to the affected data subjects without undue delay, in clear and plain language, describing the nature of the breach and recommended mitigation steps.
*   **Documentation:** All breaches, regardless of notification requirement, will be fully documented, including the facts, effects, and remedial actions taken, to demonstrate compliance with Article 33(5).

### 4.8 Data Retention and Secure Disposal

Personal data shall not be retained in an identifiable form for longer than is necessary for the purposes for which it was collected. Department-specific, legally justified retention schedules are defined, documented in the ROPA, and technically enforced where possible.

Upon expiry of the retention period, or upon valid request for erasure, personal data shall be securely and irreversibly destroyed. Methods include:
*   **Electronic Data:** Secure deletion tools that overwrite data, or cryptographic erasure where encryption keys are securely destroyed.
*   **Physical Media:** Physical destruction (e.g., shredding, degaussing) of hard drives, paper records, and other media.
*   **Cloud Storage:** Utilization of cloud provider secure deletion APIs, with verification of data purging from all backups and redundant systems.

### 4.9 International Data Transfers

Transfers of personal data to a third country (outside the EEA) or an international organization are prohibited unless the transfer is subject to one of the appropriate safeguards outlined in Chapter V of the GDPR. Intellfence''s approach is:
*   **Adequacy Decisions:** Preferring transfers to countries deemed adequate by the European Commission.
*   **Standard Contractual Clauses (SCCs):** For other transfers, the use of the current European Commission-approved SCCs. Prior to transfer, a documented Transfer Impact Assessment (TIA) will be conducted to evaluate the laws and practices of the third country and to implement any necessary supplementary technical measures (e.g., encryption with key control in the EEA) to ensure an essentially equivalent level of protection.
*   **Binding Corporate Rules (BCRs):** Considered for future intra-organizational transfers if applicable.
*   **Derogations:** Reliance on derogations (e.g., explicit consent, necessity for contract performance) is limited and used only where no other mechanism is available, with prior DPO approval.

## 5.0 Compliance and Alignment with Frameworks

This policy is the cornerstone of Intellfence''s data protection compliance program, designed to meet the requirements of Regulation (EU) 2016/679 (GDPR) and relevant member state implementing laws.

Adherence to this policy directly supports and evidences compliance with key industry standards and frameworks, including:
*   **ISO/IEC 27001:2022:** Specifically Annex A controls such as A.5.34 (Privacy by design and default), A.8.3 (Information access restriction), A.8.10 (Information deletion), and A.8.12 (Data leakage prevention).
*   **SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria:** Provides a structured approach to fulfilling the Security, Availability, Processing Integrity, Confidentiality, and Privacy criteria, with a focus on the Privacy points of focus.
*   **Cybersecurity Best Practices:** Aligns with frameworks from NIST, CIS Controls, and other cybersecurity standards, reinforcing the technical security measures required for data protection.

The DPO is responsible for monitoring regulatory developments (e.g., EU AI Act, ePrivacy Regulation) and ensuring this policy and related procedures are updated to maintain continuous compliance.

## 6.0 Roles and Responsibilities

*   **Data Protection Officer (DPO):** The DPO operates independently and is responsible for monitoring compliance with the GDPR and this policy, providing expert advice, conducting DPIAs, acting as the primary contact for data subjects and supervisory authorities, and fostering a culture of data protection awareness. The DPO reports directly to the highest level of management.
*   **Executive Management / Board of Directors:** Accountable for the ultimate success of the privacy program. They are responsible for allocating sufficient resources, integrating data protection into corporate strategy, and setting the ''tone from the top'' to foster a culture of compliance and accountability.
*   **Information Security & Technology Departments:** Responsible for the implementation, maintenance, and continuous improvement of the technical security controls specified in Section 4.6. This includes managing security infrastructure, vulnerability management, access control systems, and encryption.
*   **Data Controllers (Business Unit Heads / Product Owners):** Responsible for defining the lawful basis, purpose, and data minimization parameters for their respective processing activities. They must ensure their activities are accurately recorded in the ROPA and that processing is conducted in accordance with this policy.
*   **All Employees, Contractors, and Temporary Staff:** Mandated to complete comprehensive data protection and security training upon hiring and annually thereafter. They are required to process personal data only in accordance with this policy, their documented instructions, and to immediately report any suspected policy violation or security incident to the DPO or the Security team.

## 7.0 Enforcement and Non-Compliance

Adherence to this policy is a fundamental condition of employment and engagement with Intellfence. Violation of this policy constitutes a serious breach of professional conduct and company standards.

Depending on the severity and circumstances, violations may result in disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contractual relationship. Deliberate, negligent, or repeated violations that lead to a personal data breach, regulatory investigation, or reputational harm may also expose the individual to personal liability and regulatory fines imposed by data protection supervisory authorities.

## 8.0 Policy Review and Revision

This GDPR Privacy Policy shall be formally reviewed at least annually by the Data Protection Officer in consultation with executive management. Additional reviews will be triggered by:
*   Significant changes in data protection law or regulatory guidance.
*   Major changes in Intellfence''s business structure, technology, or processing activities.
*   The occurrence of a significant personal data breach or audit finding.
*   Changes in the risk environment or threat landscape.

All proposed revisions must be documented, approved by authorized executive management, and communicated to all relevant personnel. A version-controlled history of this policy, including dates of changes and summaries of updates, shall be maintained as part of the company''s compliance documentation. The current version of this policy is always available to all staff on the company intranet.', 'draft', 1, 'emmanuel', '2026-02-20 12:21:17.954861', '2026-02-20 12:21:17.954861', 'general', true, '"{}"', NULL, NULL, 'pending', NULL, NULL),
	(1633, 3, 59, NULL, 'Data Retention Policy', '# Intellfence Data Retention Policy

## 1.0 Purpose and Objectives

This Data Retention Policy establishes the mandatory, enterprise-wide framework for the systematic governance of the data lifecycle at Intellfence. Its primary purpose is to ensure that all data assets—whether created, processed, stored, or transmitted—are retained only for a predefined, legally justified, and business-necessary period before being securely and irreversibly disposed of.

The core objectives of this policy are to:
*   Achieve and demonstrate compliance with the complex web of global data protection, cybersecurity, and industry-specific regulations applicable to a cybersecurity services provider operating in North America and the European Union.
*   Proactively mitigate legal and regulatory risk by ensuring data is available for e-discovery, audit, and investigation when legally mandated, while systematically eliminating data that no longer serves a legitimate purpose, thereby reducing the "attack surface" and potential liability.
*   Enforce the principle of data minimization, a cornerstone of modern privacy frameworks, by prohibiting the indefinite or unjustified retention of data.
*   Optimize the cost and efficiency of information storage and management infrastructure by systematically archiving and purging obsolete data.
*   Protect the confidentiality and integrity of sensitive information, including client threat intelligence, internal security telemetry, and personal data, by defining strict controls for its archival and destruction.

## 2.0 Scope

This policy applies universally across the Intellfence organization and its extended ecosystem.

**Data Scope:** It governs all data in any format or medium, including but not limited to:
*   **Electronic Data:** Databases, application records, source code, system and security logs (e.g., SIEM data, EDR telemetry, firewall logs), email and instant messaging communications, documents, spreadsheets, presentations, audio/video recordings, and backup tapes.
*   **Physical Data:** Paper documents, printed reports, contracts, and any other hard-copy materials containing business information.
*   **Categories:** This encompasses Intellectual Property, Client Data (including security assessment reports, vulnerability data, and personally identifiable information (PII) processed as part of services), Employee Data, Financial and Operational Records, and Marketing Information.

**Personnel and Entity Scope:** This policy is binding on all Intellfence employees (full-time, part-time), contractors, temporary staff, interns, and board members. It equally applies to any third-party service provider, vendor, or partner (e.g., cloud infrastructure providers, MSSP partners) that processes data on Intellfence''s behalf. Contractual agreements with such third parties must mandate compliance with this policy or equivalent standards.

**System Scope:** All information systems, networks, applications, cloud services (IaaS, PaaS, SaaS), and endpoint devices owned, leased, or operated by Intellfence fall under this policy.

## 3.0 Definitions

*   **Archival:** The process of moving data that is no longer in active use but must be retained for compliance or business reasons to a separate, secure, and cost-effective storage system designed for long-term preservation and integrity.
*   **Data Controller:** The entity that determines the purposes and means of processing personal data. For much of its processing, Intellfence acts as a Controller.
*   **Data Processor:** The entity that processes personal data on behalf of the Controller. Intellfence may act as a Processor for specific client service data.
*   **Data Disposal/Destruction:** The secure, irreversible, and auditable process of destroying data at the end of its mandated retention period, rendering it non-retrievable by any means.
*   **Data Owner:** The business unit leader or department head (e.g., Head of Engineering, CFO, Head of Sales) who is ultimately accountable for a specific data set. The Data Owner approves its classification, authorizes access, and defines the business rationale for its retention period.
*   **Data Custodian:** The Information Technology (IT) and Security Operations teams responsible for implementing and maintaining the technical controls, systems, and procedures that enforce the retention, archival, and disposal rules defined by Data Owners.
*   **Legal Hold (Litigation Hold):** A legally mandated, formal process that suspends the normal data retention and disposal lifecycle for information that is relevant to actual, pending, or reasonably anticipated litigation, audit, government investigation, or other legal proceeding.
*   **Record:** Data in any format that is created or received as evidence of an organization''s activities, transactions, and decisions, and which requires retention for a specified period.
*   **Retention Period:** The definitive, pre-established duration for which a specific category of data must be retained in an accessible and usable state. This period is triggered by a specific event (e.g., contract termination, employee separation, fiscal year-end).
*   **Retention Schedule:** The formal, documented matrix that lists all data categories, their assigned Data Owner, applicable legal citations, defined retention period, trigger event, and final disposition method (archive or destroy).

## 4.0 Policy Statement

Intellfence is committed to the principled governance of its information assets. It is the policy of Intellfence that all data shall be retained only for a predefined period that is justified by legal, regulatory, contractual, or legitimate business necessity. Indefinite, ad-hoc, or "just-in-case" retention is strictly prohibited.

All retention practices must adhere to the core principles of **Storage Limitation** and **Data Minimization**. Upon the expiration of the defined retention period, data must be securely and irreversibly disposed of in accordance with industry-leading standards, unless a Legal Hold is in effect. This policy shall be enforced through a combination of organizational accountability, documented procedures, and automated technical controls.

## 5.0 Policy Requirements

### 5.1 Data Inventory and Classification

A dynamic and comprehensive data inventory is the foundational control for effective retention management. Intellfence shall maintain a centralized register of significant data processing activities and data assets.

*   **Inventory:** The inventory must map data assets to their system of residence, format, volume, Data Owner, and the jurisdictions in which they are processed and stored.
*   **Classification:** All data must be classified according to the Intellfence Data Classification Policy (e.g., Public, Internal, Confidential, Restricted). The classification level directly informs the security controls applied during the retention period and the rigor required for its disposal. Special attention must be paid to datasets containing **Personal Data (as defined by GDPR)**, **Client Confidential Information** (e.g., penetration test results, network diagrams), and **Proprietary Threat Intelligence**.

### 5.2 Development and Maintenance of Retention Schedules

Formal, documented retention schedules are mandatory for all data categories.
*   **Ownership:** The Data Owner, in consultation with the Legal Department, is responsible for defining and approving the retention schedule for their data.
*   **Schedule Content:** Each entry in the schedule must explicitly state:
    *   The data category/description.
    *   The assigned Data Owner.
    *   The specific retention period (e.g., "7 years", "3 months after contract termination").
    *   The precise **trigger event** that starts the retention clock (e.g., date of invoice, end of employment, product license expiration).
    *   The legal, regulatory, or business rationale (citing specific statutes like GDPR Art. 5(1)(e), IRC §6001, or business continuity requirements).
    *   The final disposition method (Secure Deletion or Secure Archival).
*   **Hierarchy of Obligations:** Where multiple regulations apply to a data set, the retention period that satisfies the **longest applicable mandate** shall be applied to ensure compliance.

### 5.3 Technical Enforcement and Automation

To ensure consistent, reliable, and auditable enforcement, retention and disposal rules must be implemented via automated technical controls wherever technically and economically feasible.
*   **System Configuration:** Data repositories (e.g., CRM, ERP, document management systems, SIEM platforms) shall be configured with automated retention timers and archival policies based on the approved schedules.
*   **Immutable Storage:** Data placed under a Legal Hold must be secured on immutable or Write-Once-Read-Many (WORM) storage to prevent tampering or deletion.
*   **Automated Workflows:** Processes for moving data from primary storage to archival systems, and for initiating secure deletion workflows upon schedule expiration, shall be automated to minimize human error and intervention.

### 5.4 Standards for Secure Data Disposal and Destruction

At the end of the retention period, data must be disposed of using methods that render recovery infeasible given current technology.
*   **Electronic Data:** Disposal must align with the sensitivity of the data and follow standards such as NIST SP 800-88 Rev. 1, "Guidelines for Media Sanitization."
    *   **Clear (Logical):** Using standard erase commands for data classified as Internal. This may be sufficient for re-use within Intellfence.
    *   **Purge (Cryptographic):** Cryptographic erasure by deleting the encryption key for encrypted datasets, or using block-erase commands. This is the minimum standard for Confidential and Restricted data.
    *   **Destroy (Physical):** Physical destruction (e.g., disintegration, pulverization, incineration) of storage media is required for the most sensitive data or when media is decommissioned.
*   **Physical Media (Paper):** Physical documents must be cross-shredded to a minimum particle size or incinerated by a bonded and insured vendor providing a **Certificate of Destruction**.
*   **Cloud/Third-Party Assets:** Contracts with cloud service providers must stipulate secure, verifiable data deletion processes upon contract termination or data expiration, in accordance with this policy.

### 5.5 Legal Hold Process

The normal data retention and disposal rules are immediately suspended upon the enactment of a Legal Hold.
*   **Initiation:** Only the Legal Department has the authority to issue a formal, written Legal Hold notice.
*   **Notification & Action:** Upon receipt, the DPO/Compliance Lead and IT Custodian must immediately identify all custodians and systems holding relevant data. Automated deletion jobs must be suspended, and data must be isolated and preserved in an unaltered state.
*   **Tracking:** A central register of all active Legal Holds shall be maintained, detailing the scope, custodian list, and preservation methods.
*   **Release:** Data under a Legal Hold may only be released back to its normal lifecycle upon written authorization from the Legal Department.

### 5.6 Archival Storage Integrity and Security

Data archived for long-term retention remains subject to the confidentiality, integrity, and availability requirements of its classification.
*   **Security Controls:** Archived data shall be encrypted at rest using strong, standard algorithms (e.g., AES-256). Access shall be strictly limited based on the principle of least privilege.
*   **Integrity Verification:** Regular integrity checks (e.g., cyclic redundancy checks, hash verification) shall be performed on archival stores to detect and alert on data corruption.
*   **Accessibility:** The archival system must ensure data remains readable and usable throughout its retention period, accounting for software and format obsolescence.

### 5.7 Logging, Monitoring, and Auditability

All actions related to data retention, archival, and disposal must be logged to provide a verifiable audit trail.
*   **Log Content:** Immutable audit logs must capture, at a minimum: data set identifier, action performed (archive/delete), date/time, user or service account initiating the action, source and destination systems, and the authority (e.g., retention schedule ID, Legal Hold notice ID).
*   **Log Retention:** These operational logs are critical compliance evidence and are themselves subject to a separate, extended retention period (e.g., 7 years) as defined in the retention schedule.
*   **Review:** Logs shall be subject to periodic review by the Security Operations team for anomalies and by the Compliance function during internal audits.

## 6.0 Compliance with Legal and Regulatory Frameworks

This policy is designed to ensure Intellfence meets its obligations under a multitude of international, national, and industry-specific regulations and standards relevant to a cybersecurity firm. Key frameworks include, but are not limited to:

*   **General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR) - Article 5(1)(e):** Directly enforces the "storage limitation" principle, requiring personal data be kept no longer than necessary.
*   **ISO/IEC 27001:2022 - Annex A Controls:** Specifically A.8.10 (Information deletion), A.8.12 (Data leakage prevention), and A.8.16 (Monitoring activities).
*   **SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria:** Addresses retention and disposal under CC6.1 (Logical and physical access controls) and CC7.1 (System operations).
*   **Cybersecurity-Specific Considerations:** Compliance with contractual obligations in client agreements (which often mandate specific retention for security incident data), and alignment with industry best practices from NIST CSF (PR.IP-6, PR.DS-3) and CIS Controls.
*   **Other Potential Regulations:** Data Owners must identify if sector-specific rules apply (e.g., data breach notification laws which influence how long incident evidence is kept, or export control regulations affecting technical data).

## 7.0 Roles and Responsibilities

*   **Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):** Holds ultimate responsibility for the approval, implementation, and enforcement of this policy. Chairs the annual policy review.
*   **Data Protection Officer (DPO) / Compliance Lead:** Maintains this policy, provides guidance on regulatory requirements, manages the Data Inventory, coordinates Legal Holds, and serves as the primary contact for data protection authorities.
*   **Data Owner (Business Unit Head/Department Lead):** Accountable for defining the business need, classification, and authorized retention schedule for their data. Must formally approve schedules and respond to Legal Hold notifications.
*   **Information Technology (IT) / Security Operations (Custodian):** Responsible for the technical implementation, including configuring systems for automated retention/disposal, managing archival infrastructure, executing secure destruction, and generating audit logs.
*   **Legal Department:** Identifies all legal and contractual retention obligations, drafts and manages the Legal Hold process, and advises on regulatory changes.
*   **All Personnel:** Required to complete training on this policy, handle data in accordance with its classification and retention rules, use approved systems for storage, and immediately report any suspected non-compliance or data spills to the DPO or CISO.

## 8.0 Exceptions

Exceptions to this policy are strongly discouraged. Any requested exception must be submitted in writing to the CISO and DPO, providing a detailed business justification, risk assessment, and proposed compensatory controls. Exceptions must be reviewed annually and will only be granted for a limited, pre-defined period. No exception is permitted for the Secure Disposal Standards or the Legal Hold process.

## 9.0 Enforcement and Violations

Adherence to this policy is a condition of employment and engagement with Intellfence. Violations may result in disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contract, and may lead to civil or criminal liability for the individual and the company. The CISO and DPO are authorized to conduct investigations into potential violations. Willful disregard of a Legal Hold or negligent disposal of regulated data is considered a major violation.

## 10.0 Review, Revision, and Communication

This policy shall be reviewed **annually** by the CISO, DPO, and Legal Department to ensure its continued relevance and effectiveness in light of regulatory changes, technological evolution, and business developments. It shall be revised as necessary.

All revisions require formal approval by the CISO. The most current version of this policy shall be made accessible to all personnel via the company''s internal policy repository. All personnel shall receive training on this policy upon hire and annually thereafter.', 'draft', 1, 'emmanuel', '2026-02-20 12:22:24.124563', '2026-02-20 12:22:24.124563', 'general', true, '"{}"', NULL, NULL, 'pending', NULL, NULL),
	(1634, 3, 60, NULL, 'Data Breach Response Policy', '<h1>Data Breach Response Policy</h1>
<p><strong>Policy ID:</strong> DBRP-001-INTELL
<strong>Version:</strong> 2.1
<strong>Effective Date:</strong> October 26, 2023
<strong>Classification:</strong> Confidential &amp; Internal Use Only
<strong>Applicable Standards:</strong> ISO/IEC 27001:2022, SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria, NIST SP 800-61 Rev. 2, GDPR, CCPA/CPRA, NY SHIELD Act.</p>
<h2>1.0 Purpose</h2>
<p>This policy establishes the mandatory framework for the timely identification, containment, eradication, investigation, notification, and recovery from a Data Breach or Security Incident involving Intellfence information assets, including client data for which Intellfence acts as a Data Processor or Sub-processor. The primary purpose is to minimize operational disruption, financial loss, reputational damage, and legal liability. As a cybersecurity provider, our response must exemplify industry best practices and ensure strict compliance with statutory, regulatory, and contractual obligations across our operational regions (North America and European Union). This policy is designed to meet and exceed the requirements of key frameworks including ISO/IEC 27001:2022, SOC 2, the EU General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR), and various U.S. state data breach notification laws, thereby preserving client trust and our market credibility.</p>
<h2>2.0 Scope</h2>
<p>This policy applies universally to all Intellfence employees (full-time, part-time), contractors, consultants, temporary staff, interns, and any other third-party agents (collectively, &quot;Personnel&quot;) who are granted access to Intellfence&#39;s information systems, networks, data, or physical facilities. It encompasses all data classified as &quot;Confidential&quot; or &quot;Restricted&quot; under the Intellfence Data Classification Policy, including but not limited to:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Corporate Intellectual Property:</strong> Source code, algorithms, threat intelligence data, vulnerability research, product roadmaps, and internal security tool configurations.</li>
<li><strong>Client Data:</strong> Any data processed on behalf of clients, which may include Personal Data, Protected Health Information (PHI), financial information, or other sensitive datasets, as defined in relevant Data Processing Agreements (DPAs).</li>
<li><strong>Employee &amp; Personnel Data:</strong> HR records, payroll information, and performance data.</li>
<li><strong>Internal Infrastructure Data:</strong> Network diagrams, security key material, administrative credentials, and audit logs.</li>
</ul>
<p>This policy is effective regardless of the data&#39;s form (electronic, physical, verbal), location (on-premises data centers, cloud environments like AWS/Azure/GCP, endpoint devices, or third-party SaaS platforms), or the vector of the breach (external attack, insider threat, accidental disclosure, or supply chain compromise).</p>
<h2>3.0 Definitions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Data Breach:</strong> A specific type of Security Incident that results in the confirmed accidental or unlawful destruction, loss, alteration, unauthorized disclosure of, or access to, Personal Data or other Confidential Information. This includes incidents like successful data exfiltration, ransomware encryption of sensitive data, or the misdirected email of a sensitive report.</li>
<li><strong>Security Incident:</strong> Any identified occurrence that indicates a possible compromise of the confidentiality, integrity, or availability of an information system or the data it processes. This includes attempted attacks, policy violations, or suspicious activity that has not yet been confirmed as a breach (e.g., a phishing attempt blocked at the gateway, an anomalous login attempt).</li>
<li><strong>Personal Data (GDPR) / Personal Information (CCPA):</strong> Any information relating to an identified or identifiable natural person. An identifiable person is one who can be identified, directly or indirectly, by reference to an identifier such as a name, identification number, location data, online identifier, or to one or more factors specific to the physical, physiological, genetic, mental, economic, cultural, or social identity of that person.</li>
<li><strong>Breach Response Team (BRT):</strong> The formally designated, cross-functional team of senior leaders and subject matter experts responsible for strategic command, control, and coordination during a declared Data Breach. Its composition is detailed in Section 7.0.</li>
<li><strong>Incident Response Team (IRT):</strong> The tactical team, typically led by the Security Operations Center (SOC) Manager, responsible for the initial triage, technical containment, and forensic evidence collection. The IRT escalates to the BRT upon confirmation of a Data Breach.</li>
<li><strong>Regulatory Notification Clock:</strong> The legally mandated maximum period between the confirmation of a reportable Data Breach and the obligation to notify the relevant supervisory authority. The most stringent common standard is 72 hours under the GDPR. This clock starts upon confirmation, not discovery.</li>
<li><strong>Data Controller, Data Processor, Data Sub-processor:</strong> Roles as defined under the GDPR. Intellfence&#39;s role is explicitly defined in contractual agreements with clients and partners. This policy applies irrespective of role, though notification obligations may differ.</li>
<li><strong>Chain of Custody:</strong> The chronological documentation and paper trail that records the sequence of custody, control, transfer, analysis, and disposition of physical or electronic evidence, critical for legal admissibility.</li>
</ul>
<h2>4.0 Policy Statement</h2>
<p>Intellfence operates under a principle of proactive resilience and mandated transparency regarding Data Breaches. All Personnel are required to immediately report any suspected Security Incident or Data Breach through designated channels. The organization is committed to responding to all incidents in a structured, swift, and legally compliant manner to mitigate harm, preserve forensic integrity, restore secure operations, and fulfill all contractual and regulatory duties. The deliberate concealment of, failure to report, or obstruction of the investigation into a breach constitutes a severe violation of company policy and ethics, and will result in disciplinary action up to and including termination, and may lead to civil or criminal prosecution.</p>
<h2>5.0 Policy Requirements &amp; Procedures</h2>
<h3>5.1 Preparation &amp; Training</h3>
<p><strong>5.1.1</strong> The Information Security Department, in collaboration with Legal and HR, shall maintain this policy and all subordinate plans, including detailed Incident Response Playbooks and Communication Templates.</p>
<p><strong>5.1.2</strong> All members of the Breach Response Team and Incident Response Team shall be formally identified, with designated alternates. Contact information shall be maintained in a secure, offline directory and tested quarterly.</p>
<p><strong>5.1.3</strong> All Personnel shall complete mandatory Data Breach Awareness and Reporting training upon hire and annually thereafter. This training shall cover identification of red flags, reporting procedures, and the prohibition of interference.</p>
<p><strong>5.1.4</strong> The CISO shall oversee the execution of at least two (2) comprehensive, scenario-based tabletop exercises per calendar year, simulating a variety of breach types (e.g., ransomware, insider data theft, cloud misconfiguration). Lessons learned shall be formally documented and used to update this policy and associated playbooks.</p>
<h3>5.2 Identification &amp; Initial Reporting</h3>
<p><strong>5.2.1</strong> Security Monitoring: Security Information and Event Management (SIEM), Endpoint Detection and Response (EDR), Data Loss Prevention (DLP), Intrusion Detection/Prevention Systems (IDS/IPS), and cloud security posture management tools shall be configured to generate alerts for anomalous activities indicative of a potential breach.</p>
<p><strong>5.2.2</strong> Mandatory Reporting Obligation: Any Personnel who suspects or witnesses a potential Security Incident or Data Breach <strong>must</strong> immediately report it via one of the following dedicated, 24/7 channels:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Primary:</strong> Email to <code>security-incident@intellfence.com</code> (monitored by SOC).</li>
<li><strong>Secondary:</strong> Phone call to the SOC hotline at <code>+1-555-123-4567</code> (number documented internally).</li>
<li><strong>Tertiary:</strong> Direct notification to the individual&#39;s manager or any member of the Information Security team, who must then formally report it.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>5.2.3</strong> Initial Report Content: The initial report should include, if known: date and time of discovery/event, systems/data/assets involved, description of the suspicious activity, the potential impact observed, and the reporter&#39;s name and contact information. Speculation on root cause should be avoided.</p>
<p><strong>5.2.4</strong> Triage &amp; Classification: The on-duty Security Analyst shall perform a preliminary triage within <strong>one (1) hour</strong> of notification. The event shall be logged in the Incident Management System and classified per the following criteria:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Event:</strong> An observable occurrence in a system or network with no immediate negative impact. (e.g., a single failed login).</li>
<li><strong>Security Incident:</strong> A violation or imminent threat of violation of security policies, acceptable use policies, or standard security practices. (e.g., a malware detection, a policy violation).</li>
<li><strong>Data Breach:</strong> A Security Incident confirmed to have resulted in unauthorized access to or disclosure of Confidential or Restricted data.</li>
</ul>
<h3>5.3 Containment, Eradication &amp; Recovery</h3>
<p><strong>5.3.1</strong> Immediate Containment: Upon classification as a Security Incident or Breach, the IRT shall execute immediate, pre-defined containment actions from relevant playbooks to limit damage. Actions are prioritized by criticality and may include:</p>
<ul>
<li>Isolating compromised hosts from the network via network segmentation or host-based firewall rules.</li>
<li>Disabling breached user accounts, API keys, or service principals.</li>
<li>Redirecting or blocking malicious traffic at the network perimeter (firewall/WAF) or DNS level.</li>
<li>Taking forensic disk and memory images of affected systems <strong>before</strong> any remediation actions.</li>
<li>For cloud incidents: snapshotting affected instances, revoking compromised IAM roles, and isolating compromised storage buckets or databases.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>5.3.2</strong> Breach Response Team Activation: Upon confirmation of a <strong>Data Breach</strong>, the CISO (or delegate) shall formally activate the full Breach Response Team within <strong>two (2) hours</strong>. Activation shall occur via a secure, pre-established method (e.g., encrypted messaging channel, phone tree). A secure, isolated virtual &quot;war room&quot; shall be established for all BRT communications to maintain attorney-client privilege.</p>
<p><strong>5.3.3</strong> Eradication: After initial containment and evidence preservation, the IRT shall work to remove all components of the incident from the environment. This includes:</p>
<ul>
<li>Eliminating malware, backdoors, persistence mechanisms, and attacker tools.</li>
<li>Identifying and patching the vulnerability that was exploited.</li>
<li>For compromised systems: rebuilding from known-good, hardened images or gold masters, not simply cleaning the existing system.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>5.3.4</strong> Recovery: Affected systems and services may be restored to operational status only after:</p>
<ul>
<li>Eradication is verified as complete.</li>
<li>Systems pass security validation tests (vulnerability scans, configuration compliance checks).</li>
<li>The recovery plan is approved by the BRT, considering the broader business context and potential for re-infection.</li>
</ul>
<h3>5.4 Assessment, Investigation &amp; Forensic Analysis</h3>
<p><strong>5.4.1</strong> Investigation Scope: A Lead Investigator, appointed by the CISO, shall oversee a formal investigation to determine:</p>
<ul>
<li>Root cause and initial attack vector.</li>
<li>Full scope: data types involved, number of records/data subjects impacted, systems affected, and timeframes of unauthorized access.</li>
<li>Identity of the threat actor (if possible), including tactics, techniques, and procedures (TTPs).</li>
<li>Whether the breach triggers notification obligations under any law or contract.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>5.4.2</strong> Forensic Integrity: Forensic analysis shall be conducted by qualified personnel, either from the internal security team or a pre-engaged third-party Digital Forensics and Incident Response (DFIR) firm. A strict chain of custody shall be maintained for all evidence. All investigative actions shall be documented in a timeline.</p>
<p><strong>5.4.3</strong> Breach Investigation Report: A comprehensive, written Breach Investigation Report shall be produced. This report is considered privileged and confidential, and shall be stored securely with access limited to the BRT and legal counsel.</p>
<h3>5.5 Notification &amp; Communication</h3>
<p><strong>5.5.1</strong> Legal Determination: The Legal Department, in consultation with the Data Protection Officer (DPO) for EU-related issues, is solely responsible for determining all notification obligations to regulators, affected individuals, clients (where Intellfence is a processor), partners, and the public. This determination must be made within <strong>48 hours</strong> of breach confirmation to allow time for preparation within the 72-hour GDPR window.</p>
<p><strong>5.5.2</strong> Regulatory Notification: Notifications to supervisory authorities (e.g., Data Protection Authorities, State Attorneys General) shall be drafted by Legal and include all legally required elements. The DPO shall serve as the primary point of contact for EU authorities.</p>
<p><strong>5.5.3</strong> Individual Notification: Where required, notifications to affected data subjects shall be clear, conspicuous, and delivered without undue delay via direct means (e.g., email, letter). Content shall include a description of the incident, categories of data involved, steps taken to mitigate harm, advice on self-protection (e.g., credit monitoring offers), and a point of contact for inquiries.</p>
<p><strong>5.5.4</strong> Client &amp; Partner Notification: If client or partner data is impacted, the relevant Account Management and Technical Account Management leads, guided by Legal and Communications, shall notify the designated client contacts as stipulated in the relevant contract or DPA, typically without undue delay.</p>
<p><strong>5.5.5</strong> Public &amp; Media Communication: All external public statements, press releases, and website notices shall be coordinated and approved by the Head of Corporate Communications in conjunction with Legal. A single, consistent message shall be maintained.</p>
<h3>5.6 Post-Incident Activity &amp; Lessons Learned</h3>
<p><strong>5.6.1</strong> Post-Incident Review: Within <strong>30 days</strong> of declaring the incident resolved, the BRT shall conduct a formal &quot;Lessons Learned&quot; meeting. The objective is a blameless review of the response effectiveness.</p>
<p><strong>5.6.2</strong> Post-Incident Report: A Post-Incident Report shall be generated, containing:</p>
<ul>
<li>Executive summary of the incident and response.</li>
<li>Timeline of key events from detection to closure.</li>
<li>Assessment of what worked well and what did not in the response.</li>
<li>Root cause analysis.</li>
<li>A detailed, actionable remediation plan with specific tasks, assigned owners, and deadlines to address identified gaps in people, process, or technology.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>5.6.3</strong> Executive &amp; Board Reporting: The CISO shall present a summary of the Post-Incident Report and the remediation plan to the Executive Management Team and, for significant breaches, the Board of Directors.</p>
<p><strong>5.6.4</strong> Evidence Retention: All documentation, evidence, reports, and communications related to the breach shall be archived in a secure, immutable format for a minimum period defined by legal counsel, but not less than <strong>seven (7) years</strong>, to satisfy potential legal, regulatory, or audit requirements.</p>
<h2>6.0 Compliance &amp; Legal Framework</h2>
<p>This policy is designed to ensure operational compliance with the following key frameworks and regulations. The Legal Department maintains the authoritative register of applicable laws.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>ISO/IEC 27001:2022:</strong> Specifically controls A.5.24 (Information security incident management planning), A.5.25 (Assessment and decision on information security events), A.5.26 (Response to information security incidents), A.5.27 (Learning from information security incidents), and A.5.28 (Collection of evidence).</li>
<li><strong>SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria:</strong> Relevant criteria within the Security, Confidentiality, and Privacy categories, particularly CC6.1 (Logical and Physical Access Controls), CC7.1 (System Operations), and CC7.2 (Incident Management).</li>
<li><strong>General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR):</strong> Articles 33 (Notification to supervisory authority), 34 (Communication to data subject), and Article 32 (Security of processing).</li>
<li><strong>U.S. State Laws:</strong> California Consumer Privacy Act as amended by CPRA, New York SHIELD Act, and other state data breach notification statutes.</li>
<li><strong>Contractual Obligations:</strong> Requirements stipulated in client Data Processing Agreements (DPAs), vendor contracts, and partner agreements.</li>
</ul>
<p>Non-compliance with this policy may result in severe consequences including regulatory fines (e.g., GDPR fines of up to 4% of global turnover), civil litigation, loss of client contracts, reputational damage, and invalidation of cyber insurance claims.</p>
<h2>7.0 Roles &amp; Responsibilities</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Policy owner. Authorizes BRT activation, oversees the entire response lifecycle, serves as primary executive communicator on technical response, and is accountable for post-incident remediation. Reports to the CEO and Board.</li>
<li><strong>Breach Response Team (BRT) Core Members:</strong><ul>
<li><strong>CISO / Head of Security:</strong> Chair of the BRT.</li>
<li><strong>General Counsel / Legal Representative:</strong> Directs all legal strategy, determines notification obligations, manages regulator interaction, oversees preservation of legal privilege.</li>
<li><strong>Data Protection Officer (DPO):</strong> Advises on GDPR compliance, acts as contact point for data subjects and EU supervisory authorities.</li>
<li><strong>Head of IT / Cloud Operations:</strong> Executes technical containment, eradication, and recovery actions across infrastructure.</li>
<li><strong>Head of Corporate Communications:</strong> Manages all internal and external messaging, drafts public statements, interfaces with media.</li>
<li><strong>Head of Human Resources:</strong> Manages internal personnel issues if the breach involves an employee (e.g., insider threat), coordinates employee communications.</li>
<li><strong>Head of Product / Engineering:</strong> Provides technical expertise on product-related incidents, coordinates code or infrastructure fixes.</li>
<li><strong>Chief Financial Officer (CFO):</strong> Assesses financial impact, manages cyber insurance claims, approves emergency budgets.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Incident Response Team (IRT):</strong> Led by SOC Manager. Comprised of security analysts, forensic specialists, and system engineers. Responsible for initial triage, tactical containment, evidence collection, and ongoing threat hunting.</li>
<li><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Mandated to know and follow reporting procedures. Required to cooperate fully with any investigation and to maintain the confidentiality of the incident during and after the response. Must complete annual training.</li>
<li><strong>Third-Party Processors &amp; Vendors:</strong> Contractually obligated per their agreements to notify Intellfence&#39;s designated contact (<code>vendor-security@intellfence.com</code>) without undue delay upon discovering any incident affecting Intellfence data or systems.</li>
</ul>
<h2>8.0 Exceptions</h2>
<p>Requests for exceptions to this policy are strongly discouraged due to the critical nature of breach response. Any proposed exception must be formally justified, documented, and approved.</p>
<p><strong>8.1 Submission:</strong> Exception requests must be submitted in writing to the CISO and General Counsel, detailing the specific policy requirement(s) for which an exception is sought, the business justification, the duration of the exception, and a detailed risk assessment outlining the compensating controls that will be implemented to mitigate the associated risk.</p>
<p><strong>8.2 Review &amp; Approval:</strong> The CISO and General Counsel will jointly review the request. Approval will only be granted in exceptional circumstances where a legitimate business need exists, the risk is deemed acceptable by executive leadership, and compensating controls are verified as adequate. All approved exceptions shall be documented, assigned an expiration date (not to exceed one year), and reviewed annually.</p>
<p><strong>8.3 Revocation:</strong> The CISO or General Counsel may revoke an approved exception at any time if the risk profile changes, the compensating controls fail, or the business need no longer exists. Personnel must comply with the full policy immediately upon revocation of an exception.</p>
<p><strong>8.4 Non-Applicability:</strong> No exceptions will be granted for the core obligations of reporting a suspected breach, cooperating with an investigation, or for any action that would violate applicable law or a material contractual obligation.</p>
<h2>9.0 Enforcement</h2>
<p>Personnel found to have violated this policy—including through failure to report, obstruction of an investigation, or unauthorized disclosure of incident details—</p>
