	(1663, 3, 55, NULL, 'Cabling Security Policy', '<h1>Cabling Security Policy</h1>
<h2>1.0 Purpose and Objectives</h2>
<p>This policy establishes the mandatory technical and administrative controls for the entire lifecycle of data and telecommunications cabling infrastructure at Intellfence. Its primary purpose is to protect the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of sensitive information and critical services by mitigating risks of interception, tampering, physical damage, and service disruption inherent in cabling systems. This policy is a foundational, auditable component of Intellfence''s Information Security Management System (ISMS) and directly supports compliance with cybersecurity frameworks and client contractual obligations.</p>
<ul>
<li>The policy objectives are to prevent unauthorized physical and logical access to network communications, ensure reliable network performance, and maintain a defensible and documented physical security posture.</li>
<li>All cabling work must adhere to the principle of least privilege and defense-in-depth, ensuring that physical layer security complements logical network security controls.</li>
<li>This document provides the authoritative standard for all personnel and third parties involved with the company''s cabling infrastructure, from design and installation to maintenance and secure disposal.</li>
</ul>
<h2>2.0 Scope</h2>
<p>This policy applies to all information transmitted across, and all physical components of, the corporate data and telecommunications cabling infrastructure owned, leased, or operated by Intellfence.</p>
<ul>
<li>This includes, but is not limited to, all copper (e.g., Cat 6A, Cat 8) and fiber optic cabling (singlemode/multimode), associated patch panels, wall outlets, network jacks, cable trays, conduits, raceways, and demarcation points.</li>
<li>Covered locations encompass all corporate facilities, primary and secondary data centers, co-location spaces, wiring closets (IDFs/MDFs), laboratory environments, and remote office locations.</li>
<li>The policy applies to all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and third-party service providers who design, install, configure, maintain, access, or manage the in-scope cabling infrastructure.</li>
<li>Exceptions to this policy are strictly prohibited without a formally documented and CISO-approved risk acceptance and compensatory control plan.</li>
</ul>
<h2>3.0 Definitions</h2>
<p>Clear terminology is essential for consistent policy implementation and audit compliance.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Demarcation Point (Demarc):</strong> The physical point, typically in the Main Distribution Frame (MDF), where the service provider''s (e.g., ISP, carrier) network responsibility ends and Intellfence''s internal network responsibility begins.</li>
<li><strong>Intermediate Distribution Frame (IDF):</strong> A secondary secured communications room or enclosure that aggregates end-user cabling runs and provides connectivity back to the Main Distribution Frame (MDF).</li>
<li><strong>Main Distribution Frame (MDF):</strong> The primary secured communications room for a building or campus, housing core networking equipment (e.g., core switches, routers, firewalls) and containing the demarcation point(s).</li>
<li><strong>Patch Panel:</strong> A passive hardware assembly containing ports (RJ45, LC, etc.) used to terminate and administratively manage the connection points for permanent horizontal and backbone cabling.</li>
<li><strong>Plenum-rated Cable:</strong> Cable jacketing material (e.g., CMP for copper, OFNP for fiber) that meets stringent fire-resistance and low-smoke toxicity standards as defined by the National Fire Protection Association (NFPA) and local building codes for installation in air-handling spaces (plenums).</li>
<li><strong>Temporary Cable:</strong> Any cable run that is not installed within a permanent, secured pathway (e.g., conduit, cable tray) and is intended for a specific, time-bound purpose. By policy, this status cannot exceed 90 days.</li>
</ul>
<h2>4.0 Policy Statement</h2>
<p>Intellfence mandates that all network and telecommunications cabling infrastructure shall be designed, installed, operated, maintained, and decommissioned using a risk-based approach that prioritizes security, reliability, and auditability. The use of unsecured, ad-hoc, or non-compliant cabling is strictly prohibited as it introduces unacceptable threats of eavesdropping, data leakage, and network instability.</p>
<ul>
<li>All cabling work must conform to the technical and procedural controls defined in this policy, which are based on industry best practices (ANSI/TIA, ISO/IEC) and tailored for a cybersecurity operating environment.</li>
<li>Security of the physical layer is considered equally critical as logical security controls; a compromise of cabling can bypass sophisticated cybersecurity defenses.</li>
<li>The IT Director/Network Operations Manager is empowered and responsible for the immediate disconnection and removal of any cabling found to be in violation of this policy to mitigate active security risks.</li>
</ul>
<h2>5.0 Detailed Security Requirements and Controls</h2>
<h3>5.1 Physical Pathway and Access Security</h3>
<p>All cabling must be protected within secured physical pathways to prevent unauthorized access, tampering, or accidental damage. Physical access to network aggregation points is a critical control point.</p>
<ul>
<li>All permanent horizontal and backbone cabling shall be installed within locked conduits, secured cable trays with lockable lids, or within raised floors in access-controlled data centers. Exposed cabling in ceilings, under floors accessible to common areas, or across walkways is prohibited.</li>
<li>All wiring closets, including Main Distribution Frames (MDFs) and Intermediate Distribution Frames (IDFs), must be dedicated, locked rooms or secured cabinets. Access must be controlled via an electronic access control system (e.g., card reader) or robust physical lock, with logs retained for a minimum of 90 days.</li>
<li>Access to these secured areas is restricted to authorized Network Operations personnel and escorted, pre-approved visitors only. A current access roster must be maintained and reviewed quarterly by the IT Director.</li>
<li>Conduit entry and egress points into secured rooms must be sealed with fire-stop putty or approved sealing systems to prevent the passage of smoke, fire, and unauthorized cable insertion.</li>
</ul>
<h3>5.2 Cable Installation, Certification, and Labeling Standards</h3>
<p>Professional installation and clear documentation are non-negotiable requirements for maintaining network integrity and enabling effective incident response and change management.</p>
<ul>
<li>All cable plant installations and major modifications must be performed in compliance with the latest relevant ANSI/TIA-568 and ISO/IEC 11801 series standards for commercial building telecommunications cabling.</li>
<li>Upon installation, every permanent cable link must be tested and certified using a qualified cable analyzer (e.g., Fluke Networks DSX) to meet or exceed the performance specifications for its designated category/class (e.g., Cat 6A, OM4). Certification reports are mandatory deliverables and must be archived by the Network Operations team.</li>
<li>A consistent, unique, and durable labeling scheme must be applied to both ends of every cable, every patch panel port, and every wall outlet. The scheme must identify the termination points (e.g., <code>JACK-A12</code> &lt;-&gt; <code>PP3-A12</code>) and should be documented in a cable management database or network diagrams.</li>
<li>Labels must be machine-printed or professionally engraved to ensure longevity. Hand-written labels are prohibited for permanent installations.</li>
</ul>
<h3>5.3 Patch Panel, Port, and Cable Management</h3>
<p>Orderly and disciplined management of interconnection points is vital for security, troubleshooting, and preventing service outages.</p>
<ul>
<li>All unused ports on network switches, routers, and patch panels must be administratively disabled via the network management system. In secured areas, unused fiber optic ports should additionally be covered with approved dust caps.</li>
<li>Physical patch panels and equipment racks must be kept neat. Cables must be dressed and managed using appropriate velcro ties, horizontal and vertical managers, and appropriate strain relief. "Spaghetti" cabling that obstructs airflow, visibility, or access is prohibited.</li>
<li>A live document (patch schedule or database) mapping physical patch panel ports to logical switch ports, VLANs, and device assignments must be maintained by the Network Operations team. This document must be verified for accuracy during any change and reviewed in full at least quarterly.</li>
<li>The use of "pass-through" or "coupler" panels that bypass structured patching is forbidden, as they undermine port security and documentation.</li>
</ul>
<h3>5.4 Prohibition and Management of Ad-Hoc Cabling</h3>
<p>Ad-hoc cabling represents a significant security vulnerability and operational hazard, creating opportunities for unauthorized network taps and trip hazards.</p>
<ul>
<li>The use of temporary cabling runs across floors, under doors, through ceiling tiles, or suspended in any manner is strictly forbidden for standard operations.</li>
<li>Any exception for a genuine, business-critical temporary need (e.g., a short-term lab setup, emergency bypass) requires a written work ticket and pre-approval from the Network Operations Manager.</li>
<li>Approved temporary cables must be clearly labeled with a unique ID, a documented purpose, and a mandatory removal date not to exceed 90 days from installation. The ticket must include this removal date, and the Network Operations team is responsible for enforcing the removal.</li>
<li>Network monitoring systems shall be configured where possible to alert on the appearance of new, unauthorized MAC addresses associated with physical ports in sensitive areas, which may indicate illicit cabling.</li>
</ul>
<h3>5.5 Cable Type and Environmental Specifications</h3>
<p>Cabling must be selected and installed to withstand its specific environmental conditions and to comply with safety codes.</p>
<ul>
<li>Any cabling installed in air plenums or other environmental air spaces must be plenum-rated (CMP for copper, OFNP for fiber) as mandated by NFPA 70 (National Electrical Code) and local fire codes.</li>
<li>Cabling for outdoor runs between buildings must be specifically rated for external use (e.g., UV-resistant, waterproof, burial-grade if applicable) and should be run within grounded metallic conduit for physical protection and to mitigate lightning strike risks.</li>
<li>Within data centers, cabling must not impede cold aisle/hot aisle containment systems. Use of low-profile or angled patch cables is encouraged to maintain optimal airflow.</li>
<li>Power cables for IT equipment must never be run in the same cable tray or conduit as data/telecommunications cables to prevent electromagnetic interference (EMI). A minimum separation distance as per TIA standards must be maintained.</li>
</ul>
<h3>5.6 Secure Decommissioning and Disposal</h3>
<p>The end-of-life phase for cabling must be handled with the same security rigor as its operational life to prevent data remnant threats and ensure proper asset management.</p>
<ul>
<li>Decommissioned cables must be physically removed from pathways, conduits, and equipment racks. They must not be left abandoned in ceilings or walls.</li>
<li>Prior to disposal or recycling, all cabling must be assessed for data sensitivity. Cabling that has carried sensitive data (e.g., trunk links between security zones, storage network links) must be securely destroyed.</li>
<li>Secure destruction methods include industrial-grade shredding or cutting that renders the cable and connectors physically irrecoverable. A certificate of destruction must be obtained from the vendor and retained by the IT Asset Manager.</li>
<li>The decommissioning process must be logged in the asset management system, linking the cable identifiers to the destruction certificate.</li>
</ul>
<h3>5.7 Third-Party and Contractor Management</h3>
<p>Third-party work on the cabling infrastructure introduces significant risk and must be rigorously governed.</p>
<ul>
<li>All third-party cabling design, installation, or maintenance work must be pre-approved through a formal procurement and security review process managed by the IT Director.</li>
<li>Contractors must provide evidence of relevant certifications (e.g., BICSI Installer), proof of insurance, and signed acknowledgment of compliance with this Cabling Security Policy and Intellfence''s Non-Disclosure Agreement (NDA) before work commences.</li>
<li>A member of the Intellfence Network Operations team must be assigned to supervise the work-in-progress for quality and security adherence. This sponsor is responsible for the final validation and acceptance testing of the installation.</li>
<li>Upon project completion, the contractor must surrender all physical and logical access credentials granted for the work. All contractor-provided documentation (as-builts, test results) becomes the property of Intellfence.</li>
</ul>
<h2>6.0 Compliance and Audit Alignment</h2>
<p>This policy is designed to provide clear, auditable evidence for major cybersecurity and privacy frameworks relevant to Intellfence''s business as a cybersecurity provider.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>ISO/IEC 27001:2022:</strong> Directly supports controls including <strong>A.7.4 (Physical Security Perimeters)</strong>, <strong>A.7.10 (Equipment Siting and Protection)</strong>, <strong>A.7.13 (Secure Disposal or Re-use of Equipment)</strong>, <strong>A.8.9 (Information Leakage)</strong>, and <strong>A.8.23 (Network Security)</strong>.</li>
<li><strong>SOC 2 (Security &amp; Availability):</strong> Provides controls for criteria such as CC6.1 (Logical Access), CC6.6 (Change Management), and CC7.1 (Physical Access). The labeling, documentation, and access logs mandated herein are critical for audit trails.</li>
<li><strong>General Data Protection:</strong> While not a primary data controller, secure cabling controls support the security principle (Article 5(1)(f) and Article 32) by protecting data in transit from interception, a relevant concern for client data processed in labs or internal systems.</li>
<li>Compliance with this policy is a mandatory requirement for maintaining client trust and meeting the security obligations outlined in Master Service Agreements (MSAs) and Statements of Work (SOWs).</li>
</ul>
<h2>7.0 Roles and Responsibilities</h2>
<p>Effective policy execution requires clear assignment of accountability across the organization.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Holds ultimate authority for policy approval, periodic review, and overall enforcement. Responsible for reporting on policy compliance to executive management and the board.</li>
<li><strong>IT Director / Network Operations Manager:</strong> Responsible for the day-to-day implementation, operational monitoring, and enforcement of this policy. Manages exceptions, oversees third-party work, and ensures the team maintains required documentation and procedures.</li>
<li><strong>Network Engineers &amp; Technicians:</strong> Responsible for the hands-on execution of this policy, including performing compliant installations, maintaining accurate patch records, enforcing labeling standards, and conducting regular physical inspections of cabling infrastructure.</li>
<li><strong>Facilities Management:</strong> Responsible for the integrity of the physical security perimeters (walls, doors, locks) of MDF/IDF rooms and the maintenance of shared pathways (conduits, cable trays). Must coordinate with IT on any construction or renovation projects that may impact cabling.</li>
<li><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Are required to adhere to the policy (e.g., not creating ad-hoc cables) and have a positive security duty to immediately report any observed damage, tampering, suspicious devices attached to cables, or non-compliant cabling to the IT Service Desk or Security Team.</li>
</ul>
<h2>8.0 Policy Enforcement and Violations</h2>
<p>Violations of this policy undermine the company''s security posture and will be treated with appropriate severity.</p>
<ul>
<li>Any employee or contractor found to have willfully violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contract, and may be held personally liable for damages or security incidents resulting from the violation.</li>
<li>The IT Director/Network Operations Manager is authorized to immediately disconnect, remove, or isolate any cabling infrastructure found to be non-compliant, without prior notice, to mitigate an identified security risk.</li>
<li>All violations and subsequent remedial actions must be documented. Significant or willful violations must be reported to the CISO and may be reported to Human Resources or Legal departments for further action.</li>
</ul>
<h2>9.0 Policy Review and Maintenance</h2>
<p>This policy is a living document that must evolve with the threat landscape, technology, and business needs.</p>
<ul>
<li>This Cabling Security Policy shall be formally reviewed by the CISO and the IT Director at least annually. A review must also be triggered following any significant security incident involving physical infrastructure, a major change in network architecture, or updates to relevant regulatory standards.</li>
<li>Proposed revisions must be documented, circulated to relevant stakeholders for comment, and require final approval by the CISO before publication.</li>
<li>The officially published version on the company''s policy portal (e.g., intranet/wiki) is the only valid version. All previous versions shall be archived.</li>
</ul>
